INSERT INTO `questions` VALUES (1,1,'What is OOP?','EASY','Object-Oriented Programming is a programming paradigm based on objects and classes.','OOP organizes code using objects and classes.'),(2,16,'What is Java?','EASY','A programming language used to create applications.','Java is a programming language used to create applications. It provides high performance and high security.'),(3,16,'What is a program or code?','EASY','A set of instructions used to create an application.','A program or code is a set of instructions used to create an application.'),(4,16,'What is a programming language?','EASY','A language used to write a program.','A programming language is the language used to write a program.'),(5,16,'What is an application?','EASY','A collection of programs put together.','An application is a collection of programs or a set of instructions put together.'),(6,16,'What are the three types of applications?','EASY','Standalone, Web and Mobile.','According to your notes, applications are classified into three types: standalone, web and mobile.'),(7,17,'In which year was Java officially announced?','EASY','1995','According to your notes, Java was officially announced in 1995.'),(8,17,'What was the initial project name of Java?','EASY','Oak','The initial project name was Oak. It was later renamed Java due to trademark conflicts.'),(9,17,'Who is called the Father of Java?','EASY','James Gosling','James Gosling is called the Father of Java according to your notes.'),(10,17,'Which company initially owned Java?','EASY','Sun Microsystems','Java was initially owned by Sun Microsystems. Oracle acquired Java in 2010.'),(11,17,'What does WORA stand for?','EASY','Write Once, Run Anywhere','Java is platform independent. WORA means Write Once, Run Anywhere.'),(12,17,'Which of the following is a feature of Java?','EASY','Platform independence','Your notes list features such as simplicity, performance, security, object orientation, platform independence, robustness, architecture neutrality, multithreading and file handling.'),(13,17,'Why is Java called a robust programming language?','EASY','Because of its strong memory management and exception-handling capability.','Your notes explain that Java is robust because it has strong memory management and exception-handling capability to deal with runtime errors.'),(14,18,'What is the full form of JDK?','EASY','Java Development Kit','JDK stands for Java Development Kit.'),(15,18,'What is the full form of JRE?','EASY','Java Runtime Environment','JRE stands for Java Runtime Environment.'),(16,18,'What is the full form of JVM?','EASY','Java Virtual Machine','JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine.'),(17,18,'What is the full form of JIT?','EASY','Just-In-Time Compiler','JIT stands for Just-In-Time Compiler. Your notes explain that it converts bytecode into machine binary and improves performance.'),(18,18,'Which of the following correctly represents the relationship between JDK, JRE, JVM and JIT?','MEDIUM','JDK contains JRE, JRE contains JVM, and JVM contains JIT.','According to your notes, JDK contains JRE and development tools; JRE contains JVM and Java libraries; JVM contains JIT.'),(19,19,'What are Tokens in Java?','EASY','The basic building blocks of a Java program.','Tokens are the basic building blocks of a Java program.'),(20,19,'How many types of tokens are there in Java according to your notes?','EASY','Five','The five types are Keywords, Identifiers, Separators, Operators and Literals.'),(21,19,'What are Keywords in Java?','EASY','Predefined words that have a fixed meaning.','Keywords are predefined words in a programming language. Each keyword has a fixed meaning that programmers cannot change.'),(22,19,'Which of the following is a valid rule for Java identifiers?','EASY','Java identifiers are case-sensitive.','Your notes state that identifiers cannot start with a number, only underscore and dollar are allowed as special characters, and identifiers are case-sensitive.'),(23,19,'What are Separators in Java?','EASY','Symbols used to separate parts of Java code.','Separators are used to separate parts of Java code. Examples include {}, [], (), comma and semicolon.'),(24,19,'What does a datatype help the compiler understand?','EASY','The type of data stored inside a variable and the memory to be allocated.','A datatype helps the compiler understand what type of data is stored inside a variable and decides how much memory should be allocated.'),(25,19,'How many primitive datatypes are available in Java?','EASY','Eight','Java has eight primitive datatypes: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char and boolean.'),(26,20,'What is a variable in Java?','EASY','A container used to store data.','A variable is a container used to store data.'),(27,20,'Based on scope, how many types of variables are there according to your notes?','EASY','Two: Local and Global variables.','Your notes classify variables based on scope into Local variables and Global variables.'),(28,20,'Where is a local variable declared?','EASY','Inside a method block.','A local variable is declared inside the method block and is visible only within that method.'),(29,20,'Do local variables receive default values automatically?','EASY','No, they must be initialized before use.','According to your notes, local variables do not contain default values. They must be assigned a value before use.'),(30,20,'What is typecasting?','MEDIUM','The process of converting one primitive datatype into another primitive datatype.','Your notes define typecasting as converting one primitive datatype into another primitive datatype.'),(31,20,'What is widening in primitive typecasting?','MEDIUM','Converting a lower-range datatype into a higher-range datatype.','Widening converts a lower-range datatype into a higher-range datatype. It happens implicitly or automatically.'),(32,20,'Which operator is used to perform explicit narrowing?','MEDIUM','Type cast operator.','Narrowing converts a higher-range datatype into a lower-range datatype. The type cast operator allows explicit narrowing.'),(33,21,'What are operators in Java?','EASY','Predefined symbols used to perform operations with operands.','Operators are predefined symbols used to perform an operation with operands.'),(34,21,'What is an operand?','EASY','A value on which an operator works.','An operand is a value on which an operator works.'),(35,21,'Based on the number of operands, how many types of operators are there?','EASY','Three: Unary, Binary and Ternary.','Your notes classify operators based on operands into Unary, Binary and Ternary operators.'),(36,21,'Which of the following is an arithmetic operator in Java?','EASY','%','Your notes list +, -, *, / and % as arithmetic operators.'),(37,21,'What is the return type of a relational operator?','EASY','boolean','Your notes state that relational operators such as ==, >, <, >= and <= return a boolean result.'),(38,21,'Which of the following represents logical operators in Java?','EASY','&&, || and !','Your notes list Logical AND (&&), Logical OR (||) and Logical NOT (!) as logical operators.'),(39,21,'Which operator is a ternary operator used to check a condition and make a decision?','MEDIUM','Conditional operator (?:)','The conditional operator is a ternary operator. Its syntax is condition ? true : false.'),(40,21,'What is the purpose of the increment operator (++)?','EASY','To increase the value of a variable by one.','Your notes explain that ++ increases the value of a variable by one unit, while -- decreases it by one unit.'),(41,22,'Which class is used to read dynamic input from the user in Java?','EASY','Scanner','Your notes explain that dynamic input can be read using the Scanner class.'),(42,22,'Which package contains the Scanner class?','EASY','java.util','The Scanner class is available in the java.util package.'),(43,22,'Which Scanner method reads a complete line including spaces?','EASY','nextLine()','nextLine() reads the entire line, including spaces, until Enter is pressed.'),(44,22,'What is the difference between next() and nextLine()?','MEDIUM','next() reads one word, while nextLine() reads the complete line.','Your notes explain that next() stops at the first space, whereas nextLine() reads the entire line until Enter is pressed.'),(45,22,'How many types of control flow statements are there?','EASY','Three: Decision-making, Looping and Jump statements.','Your notes classify control flow statements into decision-making, looping and jump statements.'),(46,22,'Which decision-making statement checks multiple conditions in sequence?','EASY','else-if ladder','An else-if ladder checks multiple conditions one after another. Its final else block is optional.'),(47,22,'Which of the following data types cannot be used in a switch statement according to your notes?','MEDIUM','float','Your notes list long, float, double and boolean as unsupported switch data types.'),(48,22,'What happens if break is omitted in a switch statement?','MEDIUM','Execution falls through into the following cases.','Without break, execution continues into the next case or cases until a break or the end of the switch is reached.'),(49,23,'How many types of looping statements are there in Java according to your notes?','EASY','Four: while, do-while, for and for-each.','Your notes list four looping statements: while, do-while, for and for-each.'),(50,23,'Which loop checks its condition before executing the loop body?','EASY','while loop','In a while loop, the condition is checked first and then the statement executes.'),(51,23,'Which loop executes its body at least once, even if the condition is initially false?','EASY','do-while loop','A do-while loop executes its statement first and checks the condition afterward. Therefore, its body executes at least once.'),(52,23,'Which loop is called an entry-controlled loop?','EASY','while loop','Your notes identify while as an entry-controlled loop and do-while as an exit-controlled loop.'),(53,23,'Which loop is called an exit-controlled loop?','EASY','do-while loop','The do-while loop executes the statement first and checks the condition afterward, so it is exit-controlled.'),(54,23,'What happens when all three segments of a for loop are left empty?','MEDIUM','It creates an infinite loop.','Your notes state that all three segments are optional and an empty condition defaults to true. Therefore, for(;;) creates an infinite loop.'),(55,23,'What is the purpose of a for-each loop?','MEDIUM','To iterate over each element of an array or collection directly without an index.','A for-each loop accesses each element of an array or collection directly without needing an index.'),(56,23,'What is the difference between break and continue?','MEDIUM','break exits the loop, while continue skips the current iteration.','break transfers control outside the loop. continue skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.'),(57,24,'What is a method in Java?','EASY','A block of code used to perform a particular task.','Methods are blocks of code used to perform a particular task.'),(58,24,'How many types of methods are mentioned in your notes?','EASY','Two: No-argument and Parameterized methods.','Your notes classify methods into No-argument methods and Parameterized methods.'),(59,24,'Which components are optional in the method syntax according to your notes?','EASY','Access modifier, modifier and formal arguments.','Your notes state that the access modifier, modifier and formal arguments are optional in the method syntax.'),(60,24,'What does an access modifier decide?','EASY','The visibility of a class member.','Access modifiers decide the visibility of a class member.'),(61,24,'What is the visibility order of Java access modifiers from widest to narrowest?','MEDIUM','public > protected > default > private','Your notes give the visibility order as public, protected, default and private.'),(62,24,'What does the return type of a method indicate?','MEDIUM','The type of data given back by the method when it exits.','The return type tells us what type of data is given back by the method at exit. It can be void, a primitive datatype or a non-primitive datatype.'),(63,24,'What is the purpose of the return keyword?','EASY','It exits and terminates the execution of a method.','The return keyword helps exit from a method and terminates its execution.'),(64,24,'Does a method execute automatically just because it is written?','MEDIUM','No. A method executes when it is called. Only main() is executed automatically by the JVM.','Your notes explain that method execution depends on the method call. Only main() is executed automatically by the JVM.'),(65,1,'What is Encapsulation?','EASY','The process of wrapping states and behaviour together by a class.','Encapsulation wraps states and behaviour together by a class.'),(66,1,'What is the main advantage of Encapsulation?','EASY','Data hiding.','Encapsulation provides data hiding, verification, validation and control over an object.'),(67,1,'Which access modifier helps achieve data hiding?','EASY','private','The private access modifier restricts visibility to within the class.'),(68,1,'What is Data Hiding?','MEDIUM','Restricting direct access to data and providing indirect access through getter and setter methods.','Data hiding provides secured, verified and validated access through getter and setter methods.'),(69,1,'Which of the following describes a Getter method?','MEDIUM','It is public, non-static, has no arguments and returns the same type as the private member.','A getter makes a private data member readable.'),(70,1,'What is the main purpose of a Setter method?','EASY','To modify a private data member.','A setter method helps to modify the data.'),(71,1,'What is the return type of a Setter method?','EASY','void','According to the notes, a setter method should have void as its return type.'),(72,1,'Which type of method should a Setter method be?','EASY','Parameterized method','A setter accepts new data through its parameter.'),(73,1,'Which combination describes a Setter method correctly?','MEDIUM','Public, non-static, void and parameterized.','The notes specify all four characteristics for a setter method.'),(74,1,'In the Instagram encapsulation example, how is a private password modified?','MEDIUM','By calling the setter method setPwd().','The example uses setPwd(String npwd) to modify the private password.'),(75,1,'What is a Singleton class?','EASY','A class that allows only one object to be created.','A Singleton class restricts object creation to only one object.'),(76,1,'Which constructor is used in the Singleton Database example?','EASY','Private constructor','The private constructor prevents object creation directly from another class.'),(77,1,'What is the initial value of the static Database reference in the notes example?','EASY','null','The example declares static Database ref = null.'),(78,1,'Which method is used to obtain the Database object in the notes example?','EASY','getDatabaseObj()','The example provides a public static getDatabaseObj() method.'),(79,1,'What happens when getDatabaseObj() is called multiple times?','MEDIUM','It returns the same object reference.','The object is created only when ref is null. Later calls return the existing reference.'),(80,1,'What is a Java Bean class (POJO)?','EASY','A class that follows the specified Java Bean rules.','Your notes define a Java Bean class through four rules.'),(81,1,'Which interface should a Java Bean class implement according to your notes?','EASY','Serializable','The notes specify that a Java Bean class implements the Serializable interface.'),(82,1,'How should the data members of a Java Bean class be declared?','EASY','Private','The notes specify that a Java Bean class should have private data.'),(83,1,'Which constructor should a Java Bean class have?','EASY','Public no-argument constructor','Your notes specify a public no-argument constructor.'),(84,1,'Which methods should a Java Bean class provide?','MEDIUM','Public getter and setter methods','The notes specify public getter and setter methods.'),(85,1,'What is a Relationship between objects?','EASY','The connection between objects.','Your notes define a relationship as the connection between objects.'),(86,1,'What is a Has-a Relationship?','MEDIUM','The dependency between objects where one object contains another object.','Examples in your notes include Car has Engine, Bank has Customer and Mobile has SIM.'),(87,1,'What are the two types of Has-a Relationship?','EASY','Composition and Aggregation.','Your notes classify Has-a Relationship into Composition and Aggregation.'),(88,1,'What is Composition?','MEDIUM','A dependency where one object cannot exist without another object.','Your notes give Car and Engine as an example of Composition.'),(89,1,'Which installation technique is followed by Composition?','EASY','Early Installation Technique.','In Composition, the dependent object is created and initialized inside another object.'),(90,1,'What is Aggregation?','MEDIUM','A dependency where one object can exist without another object.','Your notes explain that Aggregation allows an object to exist independently.'),(91,1,'Which installation technique is followed by Aggregation?','EASY','Lazy Installation Technique.','In Aggregation, the dependent object is initialized only when required.'),(92,1,'Which example represents Aggregation in your notes?','MEDIUM','Mobile and SIM.','The SIM can be inserted into or removed from a Mobile independently.'),(93,1,'What is an Is-a Relationship?','EASY','The connection between objects similar to a parent-child relationship.','Your notes define Is-a Relationship as a connection similar to parent and child.'),(94,1,'What is Inheritance?','MEDIUM','The process of acquiring states and behaviours from one object to another object.','Your notes identify code reusability as the advantage of inheritance.'),(95,1,'Which keywords are used to achieve inheritance in Java according to your notes?','EASY','extends and implements.','Your notes mention extends and implements as the keywords used to achieve inheritance.'),(96,1,'How many types of inheritance are mentioned in your notes?','EASY','Five types.','The five types are Single-level, Multilevel, Hierarchical, Multiple and Hybrid.'),(97,1,'What is Single-level Inheritance?','MEDIUM','Inheritance of only one level.','Example from your notes: class B extends class A.'),(98,1,'What is Multilevel Inheritance?','MEDIUM','Inheritance of more than one level.','Example: class B extends A and class C extends B.'),(99,1,'What is Hierarchical Inheritance?','MEDIUM','One parent class having more than one child class at the same level.','Your notes explain this using class A as the parent and classes B and C as its children.'),(100,1,'Why is Multiple Inheritance through classes not possible in Java according to your notes?','HARD','Because of the Diamond Problem.','Your notes explain that a class cannot extend two classes because of the Diamond Problem.'),(101,1,'What is Hybrid Inheritance?','HARD','The combination of Multiple Inheritance and Hierarchical Inheritance.','This is the definition given in your Java notes.'),(102,1,'Can static variables and static methods be inherited in Java?','EASY','Yes, both can be inherited.','Your notes confirm that static variables and static methods can be inherited.'),(103,1,'Can multiline static initializers be inherited?','MEDIUM','No, they cannot be inherited.','Your notes state that multiline static initializers are not inherited.'),(104,1,'What happens to parent and child static blocks when the child class is loaded?','MEDIUM','All three classes static blocks execute in the class loading chain.','Your notes explain that loading class C causes the static blocks of A, B and C to execute.'),(105,1,'Can non-static variables and non-static methods be inherited?','EASY','Yes, both can be inherited.','Your notes demonstrate that a child object can access the parent non-static variable and method.'),(106,1,'Which of the following is NOT inherited according to your notes?','HARD','Constructors.','Your notes list constructors, final class, private members and multiline initializers as not inherited.'),(107,1,'Which group contains only members that are inherited according to your notes?','HARD','Static variables, static methods, non-static variables and non-static methods.','These four member types are listed under the inherited category in your notes.'),(108,1,'Can static variables and static methods be inherited in Java?','EASY','Yes, both can be inherited.','Your notes confirm that static variables and static methods can be inherited.'),(109,1,'Can multiline static initializers be inherited?','MEDIUM','No, they cannot be inherited.','Your notes state that multiline static initializers are not inherited.'),(110,1,'What happens to parent and child static blocks when the child class is loaded?','MEDIUM','All three classes static blocks execute in the class loading chain.','Your notes explain that loading class C causes the static blocks of A, B and C to execute.'),(111,1,'Can non-static variables and non-static methods be inherited?','EASY','Yes, both can be inherited.','Your notes demonstrate that a child object can access the parent non-static variable and method.'),(112,1,'Which of the following is NOT inherited according to your notes?','HARD','Constructors.','Your notes list constructors, final class, private members and multiline initializers as not inherited.'),(113,1,'Which group contains only members that are inherited according to your notes?','HARD','Static variables, static methods, non-static variables and non-static methods.','These four member types are listed under the inherited category in your notes.'),(114,1,'What is Constructor Chaining?','EASY','The process of calling a constructor from another constructor.','Your notes define constructor chaining as calling a constructor from another constructor.'),(115,1,'Which two call statements are used to achieve Constructor Chaining in Java?','EASY','this() and super().','Your notes identify this() and super() as the two constructor call statements.'),(116,1,'What is the purpose of the this() call statement?','MEDIUM','To call the constructor of the same class.','Your notes explain that this() calls another constructor in the same class.'),(117,1,'Where should the this() call statement be placed inside a constructor?','MEDIUM','As the first statement.','Your notes specify that this() must be the first statement inside the constructor body.'),(118,1,'What happens to the Load Instruction when this() call statement is added?','HARD','The Load Instruction is removed.','Your notes state that adding this() removes the Load Instruction.'),(119,1,'What is the purpose of the super() call statement?','MEDIUM','To call the constructor of the parent class.','Your notes explain that super() calls the superclass constructor.'),(120,1,'If a class uses extends and the programmer forgets to add the super() call statement, what happens?','HARD','The compiler adds the super() call statement at compile time.','This is the compiler behaviour described in your notes.'),(121,1,'Where should the super() call statement be placed inside a child constructor?','MEDIUM','As the first statement.','Your notes specify that super() must be the first statement inside the child class constructor body.'),(122,1,'Can this() and super() call statements be used together in the same constructor?','HARD','No, they cannot be used together.','Your notes explicitly state that this() and super() cannot be used together.'),(123,1,'What is Constructor Chaining?','EASY','The process of calling a constructor from another constructor.','Your notes define constructor chaining as calling a constructor from another constructor.'),(124,1,'Which two call statements are used to achieve Constructor Chaining in Java?','EASY','this() and super().','Your notes identify this() and super() as the two constructor call statements.'),(125,1,'What is the purpose of the this() call statement?','MEDIUM','To call the constructor of the same class.','Your notes explain that this() calls another constructor in the same class.'),(126,1,'Where should the this() call statement be placed inside a constructor?','MEDIUM','As the first statement.','Your notes specify that this() must be the first statement inside the constructor body.'),(127,1,'What happens to the Load Instruction when this() call statement is added?','HARD','The Load Instruction is removed.','Your notes state that adding this() removes the Load Instruction.'),(128,1,'What is the purpose of the super() call statement?','MEDIUM','To call the constructor of the parent class.','Your notes explain that super() calls the superclass constructor.'),(129,1,'If a class uses extends and the programmer forgets to add the super() call statement, what happens?','HARD','The compiler adds the super() call statement at compile time.','This is the compiler behaviour described in your notes.'),(130,1,'Where should the super() call statement be placed inside a child constructor?','MEDIUM','As the first statement.','Your notes specify that super() must be the first statement inside the child class constructor body.'),(131,1,'Can this() and super() call statements be used together in the same constructor?','HARD','No, they cannot be used together.','Your notes explicitly state that this() and super() cannot be used together.'),(132,1,'What happens when multiple references are assigned to the same object?','EASY','All references point to the same object in memory.','Multiple references can point to the same object in memory.'),(133,1,'What is Non-Primitive Type Casting?','EASY','Converting one object into another object.','Non-primitive type casting is the process of converting one object into another.'),(134,1,'Which relationship is mandatory for non-primitive type casting?','MEDIUM','Is-a relationship or common-child relationship.','The notes state that either an Is-a relationship or a common-child relationship must exist.'),(135,1,'What is Upcasting?','EASY','Converting a child object into a parent reference.','Upcasting converts a child object into a parent reference and is implicitly possible.'),(136,1,'What is the advantage of Upcasting?','MEDIUM','Generalization.','Generalization means using a supermost parent container to store any child object.'),(137,1,'What is the disadvantage of Upcasting?','MEDIUM','Child members cannot be accessed using the parent reference.','The compiler makes the access decision based on the reference type.'),(138,1,'What is Downcasting?','MEDIUM','Converting a parent reference back to a child reference.','Downcasting converts a parent reference back to a child reference using an explicit cast.'),(139,1,'When can ClassCastException occur according to your notes?','HARD','During downcasting; it is a runtime problem.','The notes describe ClassCastException as a runtime problem encountered during downcasting.'),(140,1,'What type of operator is instanceof?','EASY','Binary operator','The instanceof operator is a binary operator.'),(141,1,'What is the return type of the instanceof operator?','EASY','Boolean','The instanceof operator returns boolean data.'),(142,1,'Which is the correct syntax of instanceof?','EASY','ref instanceof Type','The syntax given in your notes is ref instanceof Type.'),(143,1,'What is Polymorphism?','MEDIUM','The ability of an object to have members with the same name but different forms.','Polymorphism describes members such as variables, methods and constructors having the same name but different forms.'),(144,1,'How many types of polymorphism are mentioned in your notes?','EASY','Two','The two types are compile-time polymorphism and run-time polymorphism.'),(145,1,'Which is another name for compile-time polymorphism?','MEDIUM','Early Binding or Static Binding','Compile-time polymorphism is also called Early Binding or Static Binding.'),(146,1,'Which is another name for run-time polymorphism?','MEDIUM','Lazy Binding or Non-static Binding','Run-time polymorphism is also called Lazy Binding or Non-static Binding.'),(147,1,'Which of the following belongs to compile-time polymorphism according to your notes?','HARD','Method Overloading','The four types listed are Method Overloading, Constructor Overloading, Variable Shadowing and Method Shadowing.'),(148,1,'What type of operator is instanceof?','EASY','Binary operator','The instanceof operator is a binary operator.'),(149,1,'What is the return type of the instanceof operator?','EASY','Boolean','The instanceof operator returns boolean data.'),(150,1,'Which is the correct syntax of instanceof?','EASY','ref instanceof Type','The syntax given in your notes is ref instanceof Type.'),(151,1,'What is Polymorphism?','MEDIUM','The ability of an object to have members with the same name but different forms.','Polymorphism describes members such as variables, methods and constructors having the same name but different forms.'),(152,1,'How many types of polymorphism are mentioned in your notes?','EASY','Two','The two types are compile-time polymorphism and run-time polymorphism.'),(153,1,'Which is another name for compile-time polymorphism?','MEDIUM','Early Binding or Static Binding','Compile-time polymorphism is also called Early Binding or Static Binding.'),(154,1,'Which is another name for run-time polymorphism?','MEDIUM','Lazy Binding or Non-static Binding','Run-time polymorphism is also called Lazy Binding or Non-static Binding.'),(155,1,'Which of the following belongs to compile-time polymorphism according to your notes?','HARD','Method Overloading','The four types listed are Method Overloading, Constructor Overloading, Variable Shadowing and Method Shadowing.'),(156,1,'What is Method Overloading?','EASY','A class having methods with the same name but different formal arguments.','Method overloading uses the same method name with different formal arguments.'),(157,1,'Which component helps the compiler identify the correct overloaded method?','MEDIUM','Formal arguments','The notes demonstrate how the compiler selects a method based on the arguments passed.'),(158,1,'Which overloaded method is called by test(10)?','MEDIUM','int test(int i)','The notes show test(10) matching int test(int i).'),(159,1,'According to the notes, which method is selected for test(\'a\')?','HARD','int test(int i)','The char argument widens to int, so the int overload is selected.'),(160,1,'What happens when test(10, 10) is called with overloads test(int,float) and test(float,int)?','HARD','Compile-time error due to ambiguity.','The notes explain that either integer can widen to match either overload, making the call ambiguous.'),(161,1,'What is Constructor Overloading?','EASY','A class having constructors with the same name but different formal arguments.','Constructor overloading means multiple constructors in a class with different formal arguments.'),(162,1,'Which constructor is called by new student(12)?','MEDIUM','student(int sid)','The notes show new student(12) calling the constructor that accepts one int argument.'),(163,1,'Which constructor is called by new student(18, \"Dinga\")?','MEDIUM','student(int sid, String name)','The notes show this call matching the constructor with int and String arguments.'),(164,1,'What is Method Overloading?','EASY','A class having methods with the same name but different formal arguments.','Method overloading uses the same method name with different formal arguments.'),(165,1,'Which component helps the compiler identify the correct overloaded method?','MEDIUM','Formal arguments','The notes demonstrate how the compiler selects a method based on the arguments passed.'),(166,1,'Which overloaded method is called by test(10)?','MEDIUM','int test(int i)','The notes show test(10) matching int test(int i).'),(167,1,'According to the notes, which method is selected for test(\'a\')?','HARD','int test(int i)','The char argument widens to int, so the int overload is selected.'),(168,1,'What happens when test(10, 10) is called with overloads test(int,float) and test(float,int)?','HARD','Compile-time error due to ambiguity.','The notes explain that either integer can widen to match either overload, making the call ambiguous.'),(169,1,'What is Constructor Overloading?','EASY','A class having constructors with the same name but different formal arguments.','Constructor overloading means multiple constructors in a class with different formal arguments.'),(170,1,'Which constructor is called by new student(12)?','MEDIUM','student(int sid)','The notes show new student(12) calling the constructor that accepts one int argument.'),(171,1,'Which constructor is called by new student(18, \"Dinga\")?','MEDIUM','student(int sid, String name)','The notes show this call matching the constructor with int and String arguments.'),(172,1,'What is Method Overloading?','EASY','A class having methods with the same name but different formal arguments.','Method overloading uses the same method name with different formal arguments.'),(173,1,'Which component helps the compiler identify the correct overloaded method?','MEDIUM','Formal arguments','The notes demonstrate how the compiler selects a method based on the arguments passed.'),(174,1,'Which overloaded method is called by test(10)?','MEDIUM','int test(int i)','The notes show test(10) matching int test(int i).'),(175,1,'According to the notes, which method is selected for test(\'a\')?','HARD','int test(int i)','The char argument widens to int, so the int overload is selected.'),(176,1,'What happens when test(10, 10) is called with overloads test(int,float) and test(float,int)?','HARD','Compile-time error due to ambiguity.','The notes explain that either integer can widen to match either overload, making the call ambiguous.'),(177,1,'What is Constructor Overloading?','EASY','A class having constructors with the same name but different formal arguments.','Constructor overloading means multiple constructors in a class with different formal arguments.'),(178,1,'Which constructor is called by new student(12)?','MEDIUM','student(int sid)','The notes show new student(12) calling the constructor that accepts one int argument.'),(179,1,'Which constructor is called by new student(18, \"Dinga\")?','MEDIUM','student(int sid, String name)','The notes show this call matching the constructor with int and String arguments.'),(180,1,'What is Variable Shadowing?','EASY','Parent and child classes having variables with the same name.','Variable shadowing occurs when parent and child classes have variables with the same name.'),(181,1,'Variable Shadowing is applicable to which variables?','MEDIUM','Both static and non-static variables.','The notes state that variable shadowing is applicable to both static and non-static variables.'),(182,1,'In variable shadowing, which type determines the variable accessed?','HARD','Reference type','The notes explain that shadowed fields are accessed based on the reference type, not the actual object type.'),(183,1,'What is Method Shadowing?','EASY','Parent and child classes having the same static method.','Method shadowing occurs when the parent and child classes have the same static method.'),(184,1,'Which methods are applicable for Method Shadowing?','MEDIUM','Static methods only.','The notes specifically state that method shadowing is applicable only to static methods.'),(185,1,'Which relationship is mandatory for Method Shadowing?','MEDIUM','Is-a relationship','The notes state that an Is-a relationship is mandatory for method shadowing.'),(186,1,'In Method Shadowing, how does the compiler decide which static method to call?','HARD','Based on the reference type at compile time.','The notes explain that compile-time polymorphism uses the reference type rather than the actual object type.'),(187,1,'What is the key difference between Variable Shadowing and Method Overriding?','HARD','Shadowing uses reference type, while overriding uses actual object type.','The notes contrast reference-type resolution in shadowing with actual-object resolution in method overriding.'),(188,1,'What is Run-Time Polymorphism?','EASY','Binding is done by JVM at runtime.','In run-time polymorphism, binding is performed by JVM at runtime.'),(189,1,'Which type of polymorphism includes Method Overriding?','EASY','Run-Time Polymorphism','Method overriding is included in run-time polymorphism.'),(190,1,'What is Method Overriding?','EASY','Replacing the parent non-static method body with the child non-static method body.','Method overriding replaces the parent non-static method body with the child non-static method body.'),(191,1,'Which relationship is mandatory for Method Overriding?','EASY','Is-a relationship','An Is-a relationship between parent and child classes is mandatory.'),(192,1,'What should be the access modifier visibility of the child method when overriding a parent method?','MEDIUM','Same or higher visibility','The child method must have the same or higher visibility than the parent method.'),(193,1,'Method Overriding is applicable to which type of methods according to your notes?','EASY','Non-static methods only','Your notes specify that overriding applies only to non-static methods.'),(194,1,'What is the return type rule for Method Overriding?','MEDIUM','Same or covariant return type','The child method must have the same return type or a covariant return type.'),(195,1,'What should be the method signature of the parent and child methods during overriding?','EASY','Both method signatures should be the same.','The parent and child methods must have the same method signature.'),(196,1,'What is the final keyword in Java?','EASY','A keyword and modifier that represents a constant.','The final keyword is a modifier and represents a constant.'),(197,1,'What are the three types of final in Java?','EASY','Final class, final variable and final method.','Your notes classify final into three types.'),(198,1,'Can a final class be inherited?','EASY','No, inheriting a final class causes a compile-time error.','A class declared with final cannot be extended.'),(199,1,'What happens when a value is assigned to a final variable?','EASY','Its value cannot be changed.','Once a final variable receives a value, it cannot be changed.'),(200,1,'How should final variables be represented according to your notes?','EASY','In uppercase letters.','Your notes recommend representing final variables in uppercase.'),(201,1,'Can a final method be inherited and used by a child class?','MEDIUM','Yes, it can be inherited and used.','A final method can be inherited and used, but it cannot be overridden.'),(202,1,'Can a child class override a final method?','EASY','No, overriding a final method causes a compile-time error.','A final method can be inherited and used, but cannot be overridden.'),(203,1,'What is Abstraction in Java?','EASY','Providing important features while hiding the implementation.','Abstraction provides important features but hides their implementation.'),(204,1,'Which three terminologies are important before understanding Abstraction?','EASY','Service Specifier, Service Provider and End User','These are the three terminologies listed in your notes.'),(205,1,'Who specifies what help can be provided to end users?','EASY','Service Specifier','The service specifier specifies what help can be provided to end users.'),(206,1,'What is an Abstract Class?','EASY','A class prefixed with the abstract keyword.','Any class prefixed with the abstract keyword is called an abstract class.'),(207,1,'What is an Abstract Method?','EASY','A method prefixed with the abstract keyword.','Any method prefixed with the abstract keyword is called an abstract method.'),(208,1,'What is a Concrete Class?','EASY','A class that is not prefixed with abstract and provides implementation for abstract methods.','A concrete class takes responsibility for implementing abstract methods.'),(209,1,'Can we create an object for an Abstract Class?','EASY','No, we cannot create an object for an abstract class.','Your notes state that an abstract class represents an incomplete blueprint.'),(210,1,'Which keyword should not be used with an abstract method according to your notes?','MEDIUM','static','Your notes state that an abstract method cannot be static.'),(211,1,'Which keywords should not be used with abstract according to your notes?','MEDIUM','final and private','Your notes state that final and private keywords should not be used with abstract.'),(212,1,'Why do we use an Interface in Java?','EASY','To achieve 100% abstraction and multiple inheritance.','Your notes mention these two purposes of interfaces.'),(213,1,'Can an Interface contain a non-static method with a body according to your notes?','MEDIUM','No, it is not allowed.','Your notes state that a non-static method with a body is not allowed inside an interface.'),(214,1,'Can an Interface contain static methods with a body?','EASY','Yes.','Static methods with a body are allowed inside an interface according to your notes.'),(215,1,'Which modifiers are added to Interface variables at compile time?','MEDIUM','public, static and final','Interface variables are prefixed with public, static and final at compile time.'),(216,1,'Which modifiers are added to an incomplete Interface method at compile time?','MEDIUM','public abstract','An incomplete interface method is prefixed with public abstract at compile time.'),(217,1,'Can we create an object for an Interface?','EASY','No.','Your notes state that an object cannot be created for an interface.'),(218,1,'Can an Interface have a constructor according to your notes?','EASY','No.','Your notes state that an interface cannot have a constructor.'),(219,1,'Which methods are allowed inside an Interface according to your notes?','MEDIUM','Static methods, default methods and private non-static methods with a body.','Your notes list these three permitted method forms.'),(220,1,'Which keyword is used when a class implements an Interface?','EASY','implements','A class implements an interface using the implements keyword.'),(221,1,'Which keyword is used when one Interface extends another Interface?','EASY','extends','An interface can extend another interface using extends.'),(222,1,'How can a Java class achieve multiple inheritance using Interfaces?','MEDIUM','By implementing multiple interfaces.','A class can implement multiple interfaces, unlike extending multiple classes.'),(223,1,'Which are the three types of Interfaces?','EASY','Regular, Functional and Marker Interface','Your notes classify interfaces into these three types.'),(224,1,'What are the three types of Interfaces in Java?','EASY','Regular, Functional and Marker Interface','Your notes classify interfaces into these three types.'),(225,1,'What is a Regular Interface?','EASY','An interface containing more than one abstract method.','Your notes define a regular interface as one containing more than one abstract method.'),(226,1,'What is a Functional Interface?','EASY','An interface containing only one abstract method.','Your notes define a functional interface as an interface with only one abstract method.'),(227,1,'Which of the following is an example of a Functional Interface from your notes?','EASY','Runnable','Your notes list Comparable, Comparator and Runnable as examples.'),(228,1,'What is a Marker Interface?','EASY','An interface containing zero abstract methods.','Your notes define a marker interface as an interface containing zero abstract methods.'),(229,1,'What is the purpose of a Marker Interface?','MEDIUM','To indicate major activities to the JVM.','Your notes state that marker interfaces indicate major activities to the JVM.'),(230,1,'Which pair contains examples of Marker Interfaces?','EASY','Serializable and Cloneable','Your notes give Serializable and Cloneable as examples of marker interfaces.'),(231,1,'Lambda Expression is a feature of which Java version?','EASY','Java 8','Your notes identify lambda expression as a feature of Java 8.'),(232,1,'Which type of Interface can be used with Lambda Expressions?','EASY','Functional Interface','Your notes state that lambda expressions are used only with functional interfaces.'),(233,1,'Method reference was introduced as a feature of which Java version?','EASY','Java 8','Method reference is one of the features of Java 8.'),(234,1,'Which syntax is used to refer to a static method?','EASY','ClassName :: staticMethodName','Static method reference uses the class name followed by :: and the method name.'),(235,1,'Which operator is used in method references?','EASY','Double colon (::)','Method reference syntax uses the double-colon operator.'),(236,1,'Which syntax is used for a non-static method reference?','EASY','ref :: nonStaticMethodName','A non-static method reference uses an object reference.'),(237,1,'Which syntax is used for a constructor reference?','EASY','ClassName :: new','Constructor reference uses the class name followed by :: new.'),(238,1,'Method reference can be used with which type of interface?','EASY','Functional Interface','According to your notes, method reference is used only with functional interfaces.'),(239,1,'Method reference works as a shortcut for which feature?','EASY','Lambda Expression','Method reference can be used instead of a lambda expression.'),(240,1,'How many types of method references are listed in your notes?','EASY','Three','The notes list static method, non-static method and constructor references.'),(241,1,'In the syntax ClassName :: staticMethodName, what does ClassName represent?','MEDIUM','The class containing the static method','The static method is referenced using its class name.'),(242,1,'Which of the following is NOT one of the three method reference types in your notes?','MEDIUM','Lambda method reference','The three listed types are static method, non-static method and constructor references.'),(243,1,'What is Var-args in Java?','EASY','A technique that allows methods to accept any number of parameters','Var-args allows a method to accept any number of parameters, treated as an array.'),(244,1,'How are Var-args parameters treated inside a method?','EASY','As an array','The parameters passed through Var-args are treated as an array.'),(245,1,'Which symbol is used to declare Var-args?','EASY','Three dots (...)','Var-args uses three dots after the datatype and before the variable name.'),(246,1,'Choose the correct Var-args declaration syntax.','EASY','int... numbers','The syntax is datatype followed by three dots and then the variable name.'),(247,1,'Which method declaration uses Var-args correctly?','MEDIUM','public void printNumbers(int... a)','The three dots are placed after the datatype and before the variable name.'),(248,1,'Which method call is valid for printNumbers(int... a)?','MEDIUM','printNumbers(1,2,3,5,645,65,42)','A Var-args method can accept different numbers of arguments.'),(249,1,'What is the main purpose of using Var-args?','EASY','To make methods accept a different number of parameters','Var-args is useful when the number of parameters to pass is not known.'),(250,1,'Where should a Var-args parameter be placed in a method declaration?','MEDIUM','At the end of the parameter list','According to the notes, Var-args must be the last parameter.'),(251,1,'What is wrong with public void doSomething(int... a, int b)?','MEDIUM','Var-args cannot appear before another parameter','The notes state that a Var-args parameter must be the last parameter.'),(252,1,'How many Var-args parameters can be declared in one method according to your notes?','MEDIUM','Only one','The notes state that a method should not have more than one Var-args parameter.'),(253,1,'What is an Inner Class?','EASY','A class declared inside another class','An inner class is declared inside another class.'),(254,1,'Which members of the Outer Class can an Inner Class access according to your notes?','MEDIUM','A