ly','finally is a block that executes whether an exception is present or absent, according to the notes.'),(328,5,'Which one is a method?','EASY','finalize','finalize is described as a method used for cleanup before garbage collection.'),(329,5,'Which class has two branches: Exception and Error?','EASY','Throwable','Throwable is the root class shown in the exception hierarchy.'),(330,5,'Which of the following is an example of an Error?','EASY','OutOfMemoryError','OutOfMemoryError is listed as an example of an Error in the notes.'),(331,5,'What is a file?','EASY','A container used to store data','The notes define a file as a container to store data.'),(332,5,'In which package is the File class present?','EASY','java.io','The File class is present in the java.io package.'),(333,5,'What does the File class represent?','EASY','A file or folder','The File class represents a file or folder.'),(334,5,'What is file handling?','EASY','Creating, writing, reading, updating, closing and deleting files','These are the operations listed in the notes.'),(335,5,'What is a stream?','EASY','Continuous flow of data in one direction','This is the definition of stream in the notes.'),(336,5,'How many types of streams are there?','EASY','Two','The notes describe byte stream and character stream.'),(337,5,'How many bits does a byte stream hold?','EASY','8 bits','Byte stream holds 8 bits according to the notes.'),(338,5,'How many bits does a character stream hold?','EASY','16 bits','Character stream holds 16 bits according to the notes.'),(339,5,'What is System in Java according to the notes?','EASY','A class','System is a class; in and out are static variables.'),(340,5,'What are System.in and System.out?','MEDIUM','Static variables having input/output type of objects','The notes describe in and out as static variables holding input/output type objects.'),(341,5,'Which class represents a file or folder?','EASY','File','The File class represents a file or folder.'),(342,5,'Which class is used to read binary data?','EASY','FileInputStream','FileInputStream reads binary data.'),(343,5,'Which class is used to write binary data?','EASY','FileOutputStream','FileOutputStream writes binary data.'),(344,5,'Which class reads text data?','EASY','FileReader','FileReader reads text data.'),(345,5,'Which class writes text data?','EASY','FileWriter','FileWriter writes text data.'),(346,5,'Which classes provide fast reading and writing of text?','EASY','BufferedReader and BufferedWriter','BufferedReader provides fast reading and BufferedWriter provides fast writing of text.'),(347,5,'Which class provides easy print-like writing?','EASY','PrintWriter','PrintWriter is listed for easy print-like writing.'),(348,5,'Which class is used for serialization?','EASY','ObjectOutputStream','ObjectOutputStream is used for serialization.'),(349,5,'Which class is used for deserialization?','EASY','ObjectInputStream','ObjectInputStream is used for deserialization.'),(350,5,'What is Byte Stream used for?','EASY','Reading, writing, serialization and deserialization','The notes state that byte streams support reading, writing, serialization and deserialization.'),(351,5,'What is Serialization?','EASY','Writing an object into a file','Serialization is the process of writing an object into a file.'),(352,5,'Which marker interface is required for Serialization?','EASY','Serializable','The class must implement the Serializable marker interface.'),(353,5,'What happens if a class does not implement Serializable?','EASY','NotSerializableException occurs','The notes state that a NotSerializableException occurs.'),(354,5,'Which class is used for Serialization?','EASY','ObjectOutputStream','ObjectOutputStream is used to write the object.'),(355,5,'What is Deserialization?','EASY','Reading an object from a file into a Java application','Deserialization reads an object from a file into the Java application.'),(356,5,'Which class is used for Deserialization?','EASY','ObjectInputStream','ObjectInputStream reads the object back using readObject().'),(357,5,'What is the purpose of the transient keyword?','EASY','To prevent a data member from being serialized','A transient member is not serialized.'),(358,5,'What value does a transient member get after Deserialization?','EASY','Its default value','The notes state that transient members receive default values after deserialization.'),(359,6,'What is multitasking?','EASY','Performing multiple tasks at the same time','Multitasking means performing multiple tasks at the same time.'),(360,6,'What is a process?','EASY','An application under execution','An application under execution is called a process.'),(361,6,'What does every process have?','EASY','Its own code segment and data segment','Every process has its own code segment and data segment.'),(362,6,'Where are multiple threads created?','EASY','Inside a process','Multiple threads can be created inside a process.'),(363,6,'What is a thread?','EASY','A lightweight subprocess of a process','A thread is a lightweight subprocess that helps execute a program.'),(364,6,'Which two things does each thread have its own?','EASY','Program Counter and Stack','Each thread has its own Program Counter and Stack.'),(365,6,'What does PC stand for?','EASY','Program Counter','PC stands for Program Counter.'),(366,6,'What does the Program Counter contain?','EASY','Address of the next instruction to be executed','The PC contains the address of the next instruction to be executed.'),(367,6,'Which three threads does the PDF say JVM creates by default?','EASY','Main thread, Garbage collector thread and Thread scheduler','The notes list these three threads as being created by default.'),(368,6,'In how many ways can a programmer create a thread?','EASY','Two ways: Thread class and Runnable interface','The two approaches are using the Thread class or the Runnable interface.'),(369,6,'In which package is the Thread class present?','EASY','java.lang','The Thread class belongs to java.lang and is automatically imported.'),(370,6,'What does the Thread class extend and implement?','EASY','Extends Object and implements Runnable','The declaration in the notes is public class Thread extends Object implements Runnable.'),(371,6,'What is the purpose of the Thread class?','EASY','To create and control threads in Java','The Thread class is used to create and control threads.'),(372,6,'Which Thread constructor creates a default thread?','EASY','Thread()','Thread() creates a default thread.'),(373,6,'Which constructor creates a thread with a given name?','EASY','Thread(String name)','Thread(String name) creates a thread with the specified name.'),(374,6,'Which constructor accepts a Runnable object?','EASY','Thread(Runnable r)','Thread(Runnable r) creates a thread using a Runnable object.'),(375,6,'What does currentThread() return?','EASY','A reference to the currently running thread','currentThread() returns a reference to the currently running thread.'),(376,6,'Which static method pauses a thread for a specified time?','EASY','sleep()','sleep() pauses a thread for the specified milliseconds.'),(377,6,'Which exception does sleep() throw?','EASY','InterruptedException','The notes state that sleep() throws InterruptedException.'),(378,6,'Which non-static method starts a thread and internally calls run()?','EASY','start()','start() starts the thread and calls run() internally.'),(379,6,'What is the first state of a thread?','EASY','NEW','A thread starts in the NEW state.'),(380,6,'Which method moves a thread from NEW to RUNNABLE?','EASY','start()','Calling start() moves a newly created thread towards execution.'),(381,6,'What is the final state of a thread?','EASY','TERMINATED','After completing execution, the thread reaches TERMINATED.'),(382,6,'Which method can place a thread into timed waiting?','EASY','Thread.sleep(long)','The notes list Thread.sleep(long) under timed waiting.'),(383,6,'Which condition is associated with the BLOCK state in the notes?','EASY','Deadlock','The notes associate the BLOCK state with deadlock.'),(384,6,'How many thread priorities are listed in the notes?','EASY','Three','The three priorities are MIN, NORM and MAX.'),(385,6,'What is the MIN thread priority value?','EASY','1','MIN priority is 1.'),(386,6,'What is the NORM thread priority value?','EASY','5','NORM priority is 5.'),(387,6,'What is the MAX thread priority value?','EASY','10','MAX priority is 10.'),(388,6,'What is the priority of the main thread according to the notes?','EASY','5 (NORM)','The notes specify that the main thread has NORM priority, which is 5.'),(389,6,'Which keyword is used to create a thread by extending the Thread class?','EASY','extends','The class must extend Thread.'),(390,6,'Which method must be overridden when creating a thread?','EASY','run()','The user-defined thread task is written inside run().'),(391,6,'Which method starts a newly created thread?','EASY','start()','Calling start() starts the thread and internally calls run().'),(392,6,'What is the first step when creating a thread using Thread class?','EASY','Create a class and extend Thread','The first step is to create a class that extends Thread.'),(393,6,'Which interface is used in the second approach to create a thread?','EASY','Runnable','The second approach uses the Runnable interface.'),(394,6,'Which constructor is used to pass a Runnable object to a Thread?','EASY','Thread(Runnable r)','A Runnable object is passed to the Thread constructor.'),(395,6,'Which approach is preferred according to the notes?','EASY','Implementing Runnable','Runnable is preferred because it provides more flexibility and better OOP design.'),(396,6,'What is a limitation of extending the Thread class?','EASY','The class cannot extend any other class','Java allows a class to extend only one class.'),(397,6,'What is an advantage of implementing Runnable?','EASY','The class can still extend another class','Implementing Runnable allows the class to extend another class.'),(398,6,'Which statement correctly starts a Runnable-based thread?','EASY','new Thread(new Task()).start()','The Runnable object is passed to a Thread object, and start() is called.'),(399,6,'Which method pauses the execution of the current thread for a specified time?','EASY','sleep()','The sleep() method pauses the current thread for the specified time.'),(400,6,'Which exception can be thrown when using the sleep() method?','MEDIUM','InterruptedException','The sleep() method can throw InterruptedException.'),(401,6,'How many seconds are represented by 1000 milliseconds?','EASY','1 second','1000 milliseconds are equal to 1 second.'),(402,6,'What happens to a thread when it is executing sleep()?','MEDIUM','It enters the timed waiting state','A sleeping thread enters the timed waiting state.'),(403,6,'Which method makes one thread wait until another thread finishes its execution?','EASY','join()','The join() method makes the calling thread wait for another thread to complete.'),(404,6,'If the main thread calls t1.join(), what happens?','MEDIUM','The main thread waits until t1 finishes','Calling t1.join() makes the main thread wait for t1 to finish.'),(405,6,'When is the join() method useful?','MEDIUM','When one thread depends on the result of another thread','join() is useful when one thread needs another thread to finish before continuing.'),(406,6,'Which method indicates that the current thread is willing to give other threads a chance to execute?','MEDIUM','yield()','The yield() method indicates that the current thread is willing to give up CPU time.'),(407,6,'Which threads are given a chance to execute when a thread calls yield()?','MEDIUM','Threads with the same priority','The notes describe yield() as giving other threads with the same priority a chance to execute.'),(408,6,'What determines whether the operating system accepts a thread\'s yield() request?','HARD','The operating system scheduler','The operating system may accept or deny the thread\'s request to yield CPU time.'),(409,6,'Which method is used to interrupt a thread?','EASY','interrupt()','The interrupt() method is used to interrupt a thread.'),(410,6,'What is the return type of the interrupt() method?','MEDIUM','void','The interrupt() method has a void return type.'),(411,6,'What happens to a thread\'s interrupted flag when interrupt() is called?','MEDIUM','It changes from false to true','The interrupt() method changes the interrupted flag from false to true.'),(412,6,'Which method returns the interrupted status of a thread?','EASY','isInterrupted()','The isInterrupted() method returns the interrupted status as a boolean.'),(413,6,'What is a daemon thread?','EASY','A background service thread','A daemon thread runs as a background service for other threads.'),(414,6,'What determines the lifetime of a daemon thread?','MEDIUM','The user threads','The lifetime of a daemon thread depends on the user threads.'),(415,6,'What happens to daemon threads when all user threads finish?','MEDIUM','The JVM terminates the daemon threads','The JVM terminates daemon threads when all user threads finish.'),(416,6,'Which of the following is an example of a daemon thread mentioned in the notes?','MEDIUM','Garbage Collector thread','The notes mention the Garbage Collector and Finalizer as daemon-thread examples.'),(417,6,'Which method is used to make a thread a daemon thread?','EASY','setDaemon(true)','The setDaemon(true) method marks a thread as a daemon thread.'),(418,6,'When should setDaemon(true) be called?','HARD','Before starting the thread','The notes specify that setDaemon(true) must be called before start().'),(419,6,'What is a race condition in multithreading?','EASY','A problem caused when multiple threads access a critical section','A race condition can occur when multiple threads access a critical section.'),(420,6,'What can happen when multiple threads access shared data without proper synchronization?','MEDIUM','Data inconsistency','Concurrent access to a critical section can lead to inconsistent data.'),(421,6,'Which concept is used to solve race condition problems in Java?','EASY','Synchronization','Synchronization controls access to critical sections.'),(422,6,'What does every Java object have for synchronization?','MEDIUM','A lock or monitor','Each Java object has a lock or monitor used for synchronization.'),(423,6,'What happens when a thread enters a synchronized method?','MEDIUM','It acquires the object lock','A thread entering a synchronized instance method acquires the associated object lock.'),(424,6,'What happens to other threads when one thread holds the required lock?','MEDIUM','They wait until the lock becomes available','Other threads attempting to enter the same synchronized section must wait for the lock.'),(425,6,'What does a synchronized method lock?','MEDIUM','The entire method','A synchronized method applies synchronization to the entire method.'),(426,6,'What does a synchronized block lock?','MEDIUM','Only a specific section of code','A synchronized block applies synchronization to a specific section of code.'),(427,6,'Which approach can be more efficient when only a small section of code needs synchronization?','HARD','Synchronized block','A synchronized block locks only the required section instead of the entire method.'),(428,6,'What is the main purpose of synchronization?','EASY','To control access to shared resources','Synchronization controls access to critical sections and helps prevent data inconsistency.'),(429,6,'What is deadlock in multithreading?','EASY','Two or more threads permanently waiting for each other to release a lock','Deadlock occurs when threads are permanently waiting for each other to release locks.'),(430,6,'In a deadlock scenario, what does Thread-1 hold?','MEDIUM','Lock A','Thread-1 holds lock A and waits for lock B.'),(431,6,'In the same deadlock scenario, what does Thread-1 wait for?','MEDIUM','Lock B','Thread-1 holds lock A and waits for lock B.'),(432,6,'What does Thread-2 hold in the deadlock example?','MEDIUM','Lock B','Thread-2 holds lock B and waits for lock A.'),(433,6,'What does Thread-2 wait for in the deadlock example?','MEDIUM','Lock A','Thread-2 holds lock B and waits for lock A.'),(434,6,'What happens to both threads in a deadlock?','EASY','Both remain stuck and cannot proceed','Neither thread can proceed because each is waiting for a lock held by the other.'),(435,6,'What is the first recommended way to avoid deadlock?','MEDIUM','Always acquire locks in the same order in all threads','Using the same lock acquisition order helps avoid deadlock.'),(436,6,'Which coding practice should be avoided to prevent deadlock?','MEDIUM','Nested synchronized blocks','The notes recommend avoiding nested synchronized blocks.'),(437,6,'In the fixed deadlock example, which lock should both threads acquire first?','HARD','Lock A','The notes specify that both threads should acquire lock A first, followed by lock B.'),(438,6,'Which combination helps avoid deadlock according to the notes?','HARD','Acquire locks in the same order and avoid nested synchronized blocks','Both practices are recommended in the notes to avoid deadlock.'),(439,6,'What is inter-thread communication?','EASY','Communication between threads using wait(), notify() and notifyAll()','Threads communicate with each other using these three methods.'),(440,6,'Which methods are used for inter-thread communication?','EASY','wait(), notify(), notifyAll()','These three methods are used for inter-thread communication.'),(441,6,'In which class are wait(), notify() and notifyAll() defined?','MEDIUM','Object class','These methods are defined in the Object class, not the Thread class.'),(442,6,'Where must wait(), notify() and notifyAll() be called?','MEDIUM','Inside a synchronized block or method','The notes specify that these methods must be called inside synchronized code.'),(443,6,'What happens when a thread calls wait()?','EASY','It releases the lock and waits','The wait() method causes the thread to release the lock and wait.'),(444,6,'What does notify() do?','EASY','Wakes up one waiting thread','The notify() method wakes up one waiting thread.'),(445,6,'What does notifyAll() do?','EASY','Wakes up all waiting threads','The notifyAll() method wakes up all waiting threads.'),(446,6,'Which method wakes up only one waiting thread?','MEDIUM','notify()','notify() wakes up one waiting thread.'),(447,6,'Which method wakes up all waiting threads?','MEDIUM','notifyAll()','notifyAll() wakes up all waiting threads.'),(448,6,'Which statement correctly describes inter-thread communication methods?','HARD','They belong to Object class and must be called inside synchronized code','The methods are defined in Object class and must be called inside a synchronized block or method.'),(449,16,'Why is Java not considered 100% object-oriented according to the notes?','EASY','Because primitive data types can store simple values','Java allows data to be stored as simple values in primitive types.'),(450,16,'What is the purpose of wrapper classes?','EASY','To wrap primitive values and achieve boxing and unboxing','Wrapper classes wrap their respective primitive values.'),(451,16,'Which wrapper class corresponds to the int primitive data type?','EASY','Integer','Integer is the wrapper class for int.'),(452,16,'Which wrapper class corresponds to the char primitive data type?','EASY','Character','Character is the wrapper class for char.'),(453,16,'Which of the following wrapper classes is a subclass of Number?','MEDIUM','Integer','Byte, Short, Integer, Long, Float and Double are subclasses of Number.'),(454,16,'In which package are wrapper classes present?','EASY','java.lang','The notes state that all wrapper classes are present in java.lang.'),(455,16,'What is boxing in Java?','EASY','Converting a primitive type into a non-primitive type','Boxing converts a primitive value into its corresponding wrapper object.'),(456,16,'Which static method is used to achieve boxing?','MEDIUM','valueOf()','The valueOf() static method is used for boxing.'),(457,16,'What is unboxing?','EASY','Converting a non-primitive type into a primitive type','Unboxing converts a wrapper object into its corresponding primitive value.'),(458,16,'Which type of method is used for unboxing according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Non-static value method','Unboxing uses a non-static value method such as intValue() or byteValue().'),(459,16,'What is autoboxing?','EASY','Converting primitive to non-primitive without explicitly calling valueOf()','Autoboxing automatically converts a primitive value into its wrapper object.'),(460,16,'Which statement demonstrates autoboxing?','MEDIUM','Integer num = 10;','The primitive int value 10 is automatically converted into an Integer object.'),(461,16,'How many primitive types have corresponding wrapper classes for autoboxing?','EASY','Eight','The notes list all eight wrapper classes corresponding to primitive types.'),(462,16,'Which wrapper class corresponds to the boolean primitive type?','EASY','Boolean','Boolean is the wrapper class for boolean.'),(463,16,'Why is autoboxing useful when working with collections?','MEDIUM','It allows primitive values to be added directly as wrapper objects','Collections store objects, and autoboxing allows values like 10 to be added directly as Integer objects.'),(464,16,'What is auto-unboxing?','EASY','Converting a wrapper object into a primitive without explicitly calling the value method','Auto-unboxing automatically converts a non-primitive wrapper value into its primitive type.'),(465,16,'Which statement demonstrates auto-unboxing?','MEDIUM','int num = Integer.valueOf(10);','The Integer object is automatically converted into an int primitive.'),(466,16,'What is parsing in Java?','EASY','Converting a String into a primitive data type','Parsing converts a String into a primitive value.'),(467,16,'Which method is used to convert a String into an int?','MEDIUM','Integer.parseInt()','The static parseInt() method of Integer converts a String into an int.'),(468,16,'Which wrapper class does not have a parse function according to the notes?','HARD','Character','The notes state that parse functions are present in all wrapper classes except Character.'),(469,16,'When can NumberFormatException occur?','EASY','When parsing an invalid numeric String','The parsing process can generate NumberFormatException when a String cannot be converted into the requested numeric type.'),(470,16,'Which exception may occur when Integer.parseInt() receives a non-numeric String?','MEDIUM','NumberFormatException','Integer.parseInt() cannot convert a String such as \"Tabrez\" into an int.'),(471,16,'Which statement can cause NumberFormatException?','MEDIUM','int res = Integer.parseInt(\"Tabrez\");','The String \"Tabrez\" cannot be converted into an integer.'),(472,16,'How can NumberFormatException be handled?','EASY','Using try-catch','The notes demonstrate handling NumberFormatException using a try-catch block.'),(473,16,'Which catch block is used to handle an invalid numeric String in the notes?','MEDIUM','catch (NumberFormatException e)','The notes demonstrate catching NumberFormatException to handle an invalid String conversion.'),(474,16,'Is NumberFormatException checked or unchecked according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Unchecked exception','The compiler does not force the programmer to handle NumberFormatException.'),(475,16,'Why might a programmer handle NumberFormatException even though it is unchecked?','HARD','To keep the program running','The notes explain that handling it helps keep the program running.'),(476,16,'Which method is responsible for parsing the String in the example that causes NumberFormatException?','MEDIUM','Integer.parseInt()','The notes use Integer.parseInt(\"Tabrez\") as an example of an invalid conversion.'),(477,16,'What is a Collection in Java?','EASY','A group of objects stored together','A Collection represents a group of objects together.'),(478,16,'Which of the following can a collection hold according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Different types of objects together','The notes illustrate a collection containing a person, laptop and car.'),(479,16,'Which is one disadvantage of arrays mentioned in the notes?','EASY','Arrays hold homogeneous elements','Arrays are described as collections of homogeneous elements.'),(480,16,'What is another disadvantage of arrays?','EASY','They have a fixed size','The notes identify fixed size as a disadvantage of arrays.'),(481,16,'Which operations require complex logic when working with arrays?','MEDIUM','CRUD operations such as searching and sorting','The notes mention that CRUD operations with arrays require complex logic.'),(482,16,'What is the main reason for choosing the Collection Framework instead of arrays?','MEDIUM','To overcome the disadvantages of arrays','The framework addresses fixed size, homogeneous elements and complex CRUD logic.'),(483,16,'What does CRUD stand for?','EASY','Create, Read, Update, Delete','CRUD represents the four basic operations performed on data.'),(484,16,'What is a framework according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A combination of classes and interfaces with built-in functions','The notes define a framework as a combination of classes and interfaces containing built-in functions for CRUD operations.'),(485,16,'Why does the Collection Framework reduce the need to write complex logic?','MEDIUM','It provides built-in methods for CRUD operations','The framework provides built-in methods to perform CRUD operations.'),(486,16,'Which statement correctly compares arrays and collections?','HARD','Arrays are fixed-size and homogeneous, while collections can grow and provide built-in CRUD methods','This comparison follows the points listed in the notes.'),(487,16,'Which is the topmost interface in the Collection Framework hierarchy according to the notes?','EASY','Iterable','The hierarchy starts with Iterable, followed by Collection.'),(488,16,'Which interface comes directly below Iterable in the hierarchy?','MEDIUM','Collection','The hierarchy is Iterable → Collection → List, Set and Queue.'),(489,16,'Which three interfaces extend from the Collection interface in the hierarchy shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','List, Set and Queue','The notes show List, Set and Queue below Collection.'),(490,16,'Which of the following is an implementation class under List?','EASY','ArrayList','ArrayList is listed as an implementation class under List.'),(491,16,'Which of the following is an implementation class under Set?','EASY','HashSet','HashSet is listed under the Set interface.'),(492,16,'Which of the following is an implementation class under Queue?','MEDIUM','PriorityQueue','PriorityQueue is listed under Queue in the notes.'),(493,16,'Which class appears under both List and Queue in the hierarchy?','MEDIUM','LinkedList','LinkedList is listed under both List and Queue.'),(494,16,'In which package are the Collection Framework and its implementing classes present?','EASY','java.util','The notes place the Collection Framework and implementing classes in java.util.'),(495,16,'In which package is the Iterable interface present according to the notes?','MEDIUM','java.lang','The notes state that Iterable is present in the java.lang package.'),(496,16,'Which import statement can be used to import all classes from the java.util package?','MEDIUM','import java.util.*;','The notes show java.util.* as an option for importing the required Collection Framework types.'),(497,16,'In which package is the Collection interface defined?','EASY','java.util','The Collection interface is defined in the java.util package.'),(498,16,'What type of elements are stored in a Collection according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Objects','The notes state that collection elements are stored in object form.'),(499,16,'Which of the following is NOT a task supported by the Collection interface according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Compiling Java programs','The listed tasks are adding, searching, removing and accessing elements.'),(500,16,'Which method is used to add a single element to a Collection?','EASY','add()','add(Object) is used to add an element.'),(501,16,'Which method checks whether a Collection contains a particular element?','EASY','contains()','contains(Object) is used to search for an element.'),(502,16,'Which method removes all elements from a Collection?','MEDIUM','clear()','clear() removes everything from the Collection.'),(503,16,'Which method keeps only the elements common to two Collections?','HARD','retainAll()','retainAll(Collection) keeps only the common elements.'),(504,16,'What does removeAll(Collection) do?','MEDIUM','Removes all elements present in the given Collection','The method removes elements that are present in the supplied Collection.'),(505,16,'What is the return type of the iterator() method?','MEDIUM','Iterator','The iterator() method returns an Iterator reference.'),(506,16,'Which of the following is a miscellaneous method of the Collection interface?','MEDIUM','isEmpty()','The notes list size(), isEmpty(), toArray(), hashCode() and equals() as miscellaneous methods.'),(507,16,'In which package is the List interface defined?','EASY','java.util','The List interface is defined in the java.util package.'),(508,16,'List is a subinterface of which interface?','EASY','Collection','List extends the Collection interface.'),(509,16,'Which method adds an element at a particular index in a List?','MEDIUM','add(int index, Object)','This method inserts an element at the specified index.'),(510,16,'Which List method returns the index of a specified object?','MEDIUM','indexOf(Object)','indexOf(Object) searches for an element and returns its index.'),(511,16,'Which method accesses an element using its index?','EASY','get(int index)','get(int index) retrieves the element at the specified position.'),(512,16,'Which method removes an element based on its index?','MEDIUM','remove(int index)','remove(int index) removes the element at the specified position.'),(513,16,'Which method creates a ListIterator object?','MEDIUM','listIterator()','The listIterator() method belongs to the List interface and creates a ListIterator object.'),(514,16,'What does an ordered List maintain?','EASY','Insertion order','A List maintains elements in the order in which they were inserted.'),(515,16,'Does a List allow duplicate objects?','EASY','Yes','According to the notes, a List can contain duplicate objects.'),(516,16,'Which of the following is an index-based method available in List but not in Collection?','HARD','get(int index)','The List interface adds index-based methods such as get(), add(index, object), remove(index) and indexOf().'),(517,16,'ArrayList is an implementation class of which interface?','EASY','List','ArrayList is a concrete implementing class of the List interface.'),(518,16,'In which package is ArrayList defined?','EASY','java.util','ArrayList is defined in the java.util package.'),(519,16,'Why can we create an ArrayList object but not a List object?','MEDIUM','List is an interface, whereas ArrayList is a concrete class','List is an interface with abstract methods. ArrayList implements the abstract methods of List and Collection.'),(520,16,'Which statement creates an ArrayList using a List reference?','MEDIUM','List bag = new ArrayList();','The notes show creating an ArrayList object and storing its reference in a List variable.'),(521,16,'Which constructor creates an empty ArrayList?','EASY','ArrayList()','The no-argument ArrayList() constructor creates an empty ArrayList.'),(522,16,'What does the ArrayList(Collection) constructor do?','MEDIUM','Copies elements from the given Collection into a new ArrayList','This constructor creates an ArrayList and copies all elements from the supplied Collection.'),(523,16,'According to the notes, what is the initial capacity of an empty ArrayList?','EASY','10','The notes specify an initial capacity of 10.'),(524,16,'Which internal data structure does ArrayList use?','MEDIUM','Growable or resizable array','ArrayList uses a growable or resizable array internally.'),(525,16,'Which statement about ArrayList is correct?','MEDIUM','It allows duplicate elements and multiple null values','The notes state that ArrayList maintains insertion order and allows duplicates and multiple null values.'),(526,16,'Since which JDK version was ArrayList introduced?','MEDIUM','JDK 1.2','The notes state that ArrayList was introduced in JDK 1.2.'),(527,16,'What is the initial capacity of ArrayList according to the notes?','EASY','10','The initial capacity is 10.'),(528,16,'What is the formula for calculating the new ArrayList capacity?','HARD','(Current capacity x 3/2) + 1','The notes specify this formula for incremental capacity.'),(529,16,'If ArrayList has a current capacity of 10, what will its next capacity be according to the notes?','MEDIUM','16','Using the formula (10 x 3/2) + 1 gives 16.'),(530,16,'Which data type of array is created internally when an ArrayList is created?','MEDIUM','Object[]','The notes state that an array of type Object is created internally.'),(531,16,'Which of the following interfaces is implemented by ArrayList?','MEDIUM','RandomAccess','The notes list RandomAccess, Cloneable and Serializable.'),(532,16,'Which set contains the interfaces implemented by ArrayList according to the notes?','HARD','RandomAccess, Cloneable and Serializable','These are the three interfaces listed in the notes.'),(533,16,'For which operations is ArrayList suitable according to the notes?','EASY','Searching and accessing','ArrayList is suitable for searching and accessing elements.'),(534,16,'Why is adding or removing elements in between an ArrayList not preferred?','MEDIUM','Because elements after that position must be shifted','The notes explain that a shift operation is required.'),(535,16,'Why is accessing an ArrayList element fast?','MEDIUM','Because it is index based and implements RandomAccess','The get(index) method directly accesses the element using its index.'),(536,16,'Which statement correctly describes ArrayList characteristics?','HARD','It maintains insertion order and allows duplicates and multiple null values','The notes list insertion order, duplicates and multiple null values as ArrayList characteristics.'),(537,16,'In how many ways can we access List elements according to the notes?','EASY','Four ways','The four ways are get(), Iterator, ListIterator and for-each loop.'),(538,16,'Which of the following is NOT one of the four List access methods listed in the notes?','MEDIUM','Scanner','The notes list get(), Iterator, ListIterator and for-each loop.'),(539,16,'Which method accepts an index as an argument to access an ArrayList element?','EASY','get(index)','The get() method accepts an index as its argument.'),(540,16,'What is the return type of get(index) in the non-generic List example from the notes?','MEDIUM','Object','The notes state that get(index) returns Object.'),(541,16,'What does bag.get(3) access?','EASY','The element at index 3','get(3) returns the element present at index 3.'),(542,16,'Which method of accessing List elements is described as the simplest in the notes?','EASY','For-each loop','The notes identify the for-each loop as the simplest way.'),(543,16,'Which is the correct syntax of a for-each loop?','MEDIUM','for (Type e : reference)','The notes show the syntax as for (Type e : reference) followed by the task block.'),(544,16,'What is the disadvantage of traversing a List using a for-each loop?','MEDIUM','Reverse traversal is not possible','The notes state that reversing is not possible with a for-each loop.'),(545,16,'Which type is used in a for-each loop to access mixed values from a non-generic List?','MEDIUM','Object','The notes use Object as the loop variable type for a List containing mixed values.'),(546,16,'According to the notes, which option can be used when reverse traversal is required instead of a for-each loop?','HARD','ListIterator','The notes suggest get(index) or ListIterator as alternatives for reverse traversal.'),(547,16,'Which method is used to access ArrayList elements using an Iterator?','EASY','iterator()','The iterator() method creates an Iterator object to access elements.'),(548,16,'Which interface contains the iterator() method?','MEDIUM','Iterable','According to the notes, iterator() belongs to the Iterable interface.'),(549,16,'What is the return type of iterator()?','MEDIUM','Iterator','The method returns an Iterator interface reference.'),(550,16,'Which statement correctly creates an Iterator reference for an ArrayList named bag?','MEDIUM','Iterator i = bag.iterator();','The notes show this syntax for creating an Iterator reference.'),(551,16,'How many important methods of the Iterator interface are listed in the notes?','EASY','Two','The notes list hasNext() and next() as the two important methods.'),(552,16,'What is the purpose of hasNext()?','EASY','Checks whether another element is available','hasNext() returns true if an element is available to access, otherwise false.'),(553,16,'What does next() do in an Iterator?','MEDIUM','Accesses the current element and moves the cursor forward','next() returns the element and moves the cursor to the next element.'),(554,16,'What is the return type of next() according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Object','The notes specify Object as the return type of next().'),(555,16,'Where is the Iterator cursor positioned initially according to the example in the notes?','HARD','Before the first element','The notes describe the cursor as starting before the first element, at -1.'),(556,16,'Which sequence is correct when safely accessing elements using an Iterator?','MEDIUM','hasNext() followed by next()','The notes show checking hasNext() before accessing an element with next().'),(557,16,'When does NoSuchElementException occur while using an Iterator?','EASY','When next() is called but no element is available','The exception occurs when next() tries to access an element that is not present.'),(558,16,'When does NoSuchElementException occur?','MEDIUM','At runtime','The notes specify that it is a runtime exception, not a compile-time error.'),(559,16,'Which method helps avoid NoSuchElementException?','EASY','hasNext()','Check whether an element is available using hasNext() before calling next().'),(560,16,'Which is the correct sequence for safely accessing an Iterator element?','MEDIUM','Call hasNext() first, then next()','The notes recommend checking hasNext() before calling next().'),(561,16,'Which loop is recommended to safely iterate through a collection using Iterator?','MEDIUM','while (i.hasNext())','The notes demonstrate using hasNext() as the while-loop condition and next() inside it.'),(562,16,'How many times can an Iterator be used to traverse a collection according to the notes?','EASY','Only once','One disadvantage listed in the notes is that an Iterator can iterate only once.'),(563,16,'Which traversal direction is not supported by Iterator?','EASY','Backward traversal','Iterator moves forward only and cannot access elements in reverse order.'),(564,16,'Why must a new Iterator be created after reaching the end?','MEDIUM','Because its cursor moves forward only','The Iterator cursor moves forward only, so the same Iterator cannot be reused from the beginning.'),(565,16,'Which alternative is suggested to overcome Iterator traversal limitations?','MEDIUM','ListIterator','The notes recommend listIterator() for forward and backward access.'),(566,16,'Which statement about Iterator is correct according to the notes?','HARD','It can iterate only once and cannot traverse in reverse order','The two disadvantages listed are single forward traversal and no reverse access.'),(567,16,'When does NoSuchElementException occur while using an Iterator?','EASY','When next() is called but no element is available','The exception occurs when next() tries to access an element that is not present.'),(568,16,'When does NoSuchElementException occur?','MEDIUM','At runtime','The notes specify that it is a runtime exception, not a compile-time error.'),(569,16,'Which method helps avoid NoSuchElementException?','EASY','hasNext()','Check whether an element is available using hasNext() before calling next().'),(570,16,'Which is the correct sequence for safely accessing an Iterator element?','MEDIUM','Call hasNext() first, then next()','The notes recommend checking hasNext() before calling next().'),(571,16,'Which loop is recommended to safely iterate through a collection using Iterator?','MEDIUM','while (i.hasNext())','The notes demonstrate using hasNext() as the while-loop condition and next() inside it.'),(572,16,'How many times can an Iterator be used to traverse a collection according to the notes?','EASY','Only once','One disadvantage listed in the notes is that an Iterator can iterate only once.'),(573,16,'Which traversal direction is not supported by Iterator?','EASY','Backward traversal','Iterator moves forward only and cannot access elements in reverse order.'),(574,16,'Why must a new Iterator be created after reaching the end?','MEDIUM','Because its cursor moves forward only','The Iterator cursor moves forward only, so the same Iterator cannot be reused from the beginning.'),(575,16,'Which alternative is suggested to overcome Iterator traversal limitations?','MEDIUM','ListIterator','The notes recommend listIterator() for forward and backward access.'),(576,16,'Which statement about Iterator is correct according to the notes?','HARD','It can iterate only once and cannot traverse in reverse order','The two disadvantages listed are single forward traversal and no reverse access.'),(577,16,'Which interface provides the listIterator() method?','EASY','List interface','According to the notes, listIterator() belongs to the List interface.'),(578,16,'What is the return type of listIterator()?','EASY','ListIterator','The listIterator() method returns a reference of ListIterator interface type.'),(579,16,