 generic collection?','MEDIUM','List<Integer> box1 = new ArrayList();','The generic type Integer specifies the type of elements allowed in the list.'),(601,16,'Can a generic collection use int directly as its type parameter?','MEDIUM','No, it requires a non-primitive type such as Integer','The notes specify that generic collections use non-primitive types, such as wrapper classes.'),(602,16,'What happens if we add 2.3 to a List<Integer> collection?','MEDIUM','It causes a compile-time error','The generic list accepts Integer values, so adding a Double value causes a compile-time error.'),(603,16,'What is the return type when reading an element from List<String>?','MEDIUM','String','The return type of elements in a generic collection is the same as the specified generic type.'),(604,16,'What are the main advantages of generics mentioned in the notes?','HARD','Type safety and elimination of type casting','Generics provide stronger compile-time type checks and eliminate the need for type casting.'),(605,16,'Which class provides the sort() method used to sort a List?','EASY','Collections class','The notes specify that sorting an ArrayList is done using the sort() method in the Collections class.'),(606,16,'What is the nature of the sort() method in the Collections class?','EASY','Static method','The sort() method is static, so it can be called using the class name.'),(607,16,'How many overloaded sort() methods are mentioned in the notes?','EASY','Two','The two versions are sort(List l) and sort(List l, Comparator ref).'),(608,16,'Which are the two overloaded versions of sort()?','MEDIUM','sort(List l) and sort(List l, Comparator ref)','The notes list these two overloaded methods.'),(609,16,'Why can Collections.sort() be called without creating a Collections object?','MEDIUM','Because sort() is static','Static methods can be called using the class name.'),(610,16,'What is the difference between Collection and Collections?','MEDIUM','Collection is an interface, whereas Collections is a class','Collection is the root interface of List, Set and Queue. Collections is a utility class containing static methods such as sort().'),(611,16,'What is the default sorting order of Collections.sort(List l)?','EASY','Ascending order','The sort(List l) method sorts elements in ascending order by default.'),(612,16,'What is the output after sorting [10, 5, 1, 2] using Collections.sort(bag)?','MEDIUM','[1, 2, 5, 10]','The default sort order shown in the notes is ascending.'),(613,16,'When can ClassCastException occur while sorting a list using sort(List l)?','HARD','When the list contains mixed types or elements are not comparable','The notes specify that elements must be homogeneous and of a comparable type.'),(614,16,'How can we sort our own objects, such as Product objects, using the default sorting method?','HARD','Implement Comparable and write compareTo() logic','The notes recommend implementing Comparable and defining compareTo() for sorting custom objects.'),(615,16,'What type of interface is Comparable?','EASY','Functional interface','According to your notes, Comparable is a functional interface containing compareTo().'),(616,16,'Which method is present in the Comparable interface?','EASY','compareTo()','Comparable contains the compareTo() method.'),(617,16,'Where is the sorting logic written when using Comparable?','MEDIUM','Inside the class itself','Comparable is implemented by the class whose objects need to be sorted.'),(618,16,'What is the main purpose of Comparator?','EASY','To perform custom sorting','Comparator allows custom sorting in ascending or descending order.'),(619,16,'In which package is the Comparator interface present?','EASY','java.util','Your notes identify Comparator as a predefined interface in java.util.'),(620,16,'Which method is the single abstract method of Comparator?','MEDIUM','compare()','Comparator is a functional interface with one abstract method called compare().'),(621,16,'Which statement correctly compares 