osition of the Object class in the Java class hierarchy?','EASY','Supermost class','The Object class is the supermost class for all classes in Java.'),(677,16,'What does the compiler add if a class does not have an extends keyword?','MEDIUM','extends Object','According to the notes, the compiler adds extends Object at compile time.'),(678,16,'Which of the following is a protected method of the Object class according to the notes?','MEDIUM','clone()','The notes list clone() and finalize() as protected methods.'),(679,16,'Which group contains methods declared final in the Object class according to the notes?','HARD','wait(), notify(), notifyAll() and getClass()','The notes identify these methods as final and therefore not overridable.'),(680,16,'What is the default output format when an object reference is printed without overriding toString()?','MEDIUM','ClassName@HexaDecimalValue','The default format is the class name followed by @ and a hexadecimal value.'),(681,16,'How many public classes are allowed in a single Java source file according to the notes?','EASY','Only one','The notes state that only one class can be public in a single source file.'),(682,16,'What happens if two classes are declared public in the same Java source file?','MEDIUM','Compile-time error','The notes state that only one class in a source file can be public.'),(683,16,'Why are wait() and notify() methods present in the Object class according to the notes?','HARD','They work on an object\'s lock or monitor','Every Java object has a lock or monitor, and these methods work on that object\'s lock.'),(684,16,'What is the name of the public class that should be used as the Java source file name?','MEDIUM','The public class name','The notes give the example P3.java for public class P3.'),(685,16,'What happens when toString() is called without overriding it?','EASY','It returns ClassName@HexaDecimalValue','The default toString() output contains the class name and a hexadecimal value.'),(686,16,'What is the main purpose of overriding toString()?','EASY','To get the states of an object','Overriding toString() helps display the data or states of an object.'),(687,16,'What happens when System.out.println() receives an object reference?','MEDIUM','It calls the toString() method','Printing an object reference calls its toString() method.'),(688,16,'What does equals() compare by default when it is not overridden?','MEDIUM','Current object reference with passed object reference','Without overriding, equals() compares the current object reference with the passed object reference.'),(689,16,'What is the purpose of overriding equals()?','EASY','To compare the contents of two objects','The overridden equals() method compares the current object content with the passed object content.'),(690,16,'What is the first step mentioned in the notes for overriding equals()?','MEDIUM','Downcasting','The notes list downcasting as the first step before comparing object contents.'),(691,16,'What does the default hashCode() method generate according to the notes?','MEDIUM','An integer based on the address','Without overriding hashCode(), the notes state that it generates an integer based on the address.'),(692,16,'Why do we override hashCode()?','EASY','To generate an integer based on the state of an object','The overridden hashCode() generates an integer based on the object state.'),(693,16,'Which method should be overridden whenever equals() is overridden according to the notes?','MEDIUM','hashCode()','Equal objects should have equal hashcodes; otherwise, Set may allow duplicates.'),(694,16,'If two objects have the same hashcode, must they always be equal?','HARD','No, equal hashcodes do not guarantee equal objects','The notes explain that equal objects must have equal hashcodes, but the reverse is not required because collisions are possible.'),(695,16,'What is Garbage Collection in Java?','EASY','The process of removing unused objects from the heap area','Garbage Collection is the process of removing unused objects from the heap area.'),(696,16,'From which memory area are unused objects removed during Garbage Collection?','EASY','Heap area','The notes state that Garbage Collection removes unused objects from the heap area.'),(697,16,'When does an object become eligible for Garbage Collection according to the notes?','MEDIUM','When no reference variable points to it','An object becomes eligible for Garbage Collection when no reference variable points to it.'),(698,16,'Which method is used to request Garbage Collection?','EASY','System.gc()','The notes mention System.gc() as the way to request Garbage Collection.'),(699,16,'Does calling System.gc() guarantee that Garbage Collection happens immediately?','MEDIUM','No, it is only a request to the JVM','The JVM decides when to perform Garbage Collection; System.gc() does not guarantee immediate collection.'),(700,16,'The finalize() method belongs to which class?','EASY','Object class','finalize() is a method of the Object class.'),(701,16,'What is the access modifier of finalize() according to the notes?','MEDIUM','protected','The finalize() method is declared with protected access.'),(702,16,'What is the purpose of the finalize() method according to the notes?','MEDIUM','To perform cleanup processing before an object is garbage collected','The notes describe finalize() as helping with cleanup before garbage collection.'),(703,16,'Which statement correctly describes the relationship between System.gc() and finalize() in the notes?','HARD','System.gc() requests Garbage Collection, and the JVM may invoke finalize()','The notes describe the GC process as involving the JVM and the finalize() method.'),(704,16,'Which option correctly differentiates final, finally and finalize?','MEDIUM','final is a modifier, finally is a block, and finalize is a method','The notes distinguish final as a modifier, finally as a block and finalize as a method.'),(705,16,'What is cloning in Java?','EASY','B','Cloning is the process of creating an exact duplicate of an object.'),(706,16,'Which method is used to achieve cloning in Java?','EASY','C','Cloning is achieved using the clone() method present in the Object class.'),(707,16,'In which class is the clone() method present?','EASY','A','The clone() method is present in the Object class.'),(708,16,'What is the access modifier of the clone() method?','MEDIUM','B','The clone() method is declared as protected in the Object class.'),(709,16,'Which interface must a class implement to support cloning?','EASY','D','The class should implement the Cloneable marker interface.'),(710,16,'What should the overridden clone() method return to perform the copying?','MEDIUM','A','The notes describe overriding clone() and returning super.clone().'),(711,16,'What happens if a class does not implement the Cloneable interface and cloning is attempted?','MEDIUM','C','A CloneNotSupportedException is thrown.'),(712,16,'What type of interface is Cloneable?','EASY','B','Cloneable is a marker interface, which does not contain methods.'),(713,16,'In which package is the String class present?','EASY','A','The String class is a built-in class present in java.lang.'),(714,16,'Which keyword is used in the declaration of the String class?','MEDIUM','C','String is declared as a public final class.'),(715,16,'Which of the following interfaces is implemented by String?','MEDIUM','B','String implements Serializable, Comparable and CharSequence.'),(716,16,'How many basic ways are available to create a String object?','EASY','A','A String object can be created using the new keyword or without the new keyword.'),(717,16,'Where is the String Constant Pool located according to the notes?','EASY','D','The String Constant Pool is an area inside the heap.'),(718,16,'Which class uses the String Constant Pool concept?','MEDIUM','C','According to the notes, the SCP concept is applicable only to String class.'),(719,16,'What happens when two String variables use the same existing literal?','MEDIUM','B','If the content already exists in the SCP, the existing object reference is shared.'),(720,16,'Which String constructor converts a character array into a String?','EASY','A','String(char[] ch) creates a String by converting a character array.'),(721,16,'What is the nature of the String class?','EASY','C','String is immutable. Once created, its content cannot be modified.'),(722,16,'Which classes can be used to overcome the modification disadvantage of String?','EASY','D','StringBuffer and StringBuilder are used when mutable string objects are required.'),(723,16,'Which String method converts all characters into uppercase?','EASY','B','toUpperCase() converts the specified String to uppercase.'),(724,16,'Which method removes spaces before and after a String?','EASY','C','trim() removes spaces present before and after the String.'),(725,16,'What does charAt(int index) return?','EASY','A','charAt() returns the character present at the specified index.'),(726,16,'What does indexOf() return when the specified character is not found?','MEDIUM','D','The indexOf() method returns -1 when the specified character or text is not found.'),(727,16,'Which method returns the length of a String?','EASY','B','length() returns the length of the String.'),(728,16,'Which method compares two Strings while ignoring their case?','EASY','C','equalsIgnoreCase() compares two Strings by ignoring their case.'),(729,16,'Which method checks whether a specified String is present inside another String?','EASY','A','contains() returns true if the specified String is present; otherwise it returns false.'),(730,16,'Which method converts a String into a character array?','MEDIUM','D','toCharArray() converts the specified String into a character array.'),(731,16,'Which method breaks a String into multiple Strings based on a specified delimiter or pattern?','MEDIUM','B','split() breaks a String into multiple Strings and returns a String array.'),(732,16,'What is the end index behavior of substring(start, end)?','MEDIUM','C','The substring starts at the specified start index and ends at end-1.'),(733,16,'In which package is StringBuffer present?','EASY','A','StringBuffer is a built-in class defined in the java.lang package.'),(734,16,'What is the nature of StringBuffer?','EASY','C','StringBuffer helps to create mutable instances of String.'),(735,16,'What is the initial capacity of a default StringBuffer object?','EASY','B','The initial capacity of StringBuffer is 16.'),(736,16,'Which formula is used to calculate the new capacity of StringBuffer when its capacity is filled?','MEDIUM','D','The formula given in the notes is (current capacity x 2) + 2.'),(737,16,'What is the capacity of new StringBuffer(\"Hello\") according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The capacity is calculated as 16 plus the length of the given String: 16 + 5 = 21.'),(738,16,'Which StringBuffer method adds the specified text at the end?','EASY','C','append() joins the specified text at the end of the existing StringBuffer content.'),(739,16,'Which method inserts a specified String at a particular index in StringBuffer?','MEDIUM','B','insert(index, String) inserts the specified String at the given index.'),(740,16,'Which method reverses the StringBuffer content?','EASY','D','reverse() reverses the StringBuffer content.'),(741,16,'Why is StringBuffer considered thread safe according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','All StringBuffer methods are synchronized, so only one thread can access the object at a time.'),(742,16,'What is the main difference between StringBuffer and StringBuilder?','MEDIUM','C','StringBuffer methods are synchronized, whereas StringBuilder methods are non-synchronized.'),(743,16,'Which StringBuffer method returns its current capacity?','EASY','A','capacity() returns the current capacity of the StringBuffer.'),(744,16,'Which StringBuffer method deletes characters from a beginning index to end-1?','MEDIUM','C','delete(begin, end) removes characters from the specified beginning index to end-1.'),(745,16,'Which method removes a single character at a specified index?','EASY','B','deleteCharAt(index) removes the character present at the specified index.'),(746,16,'What does setLength(2) do to a StringBuffer containing \"hi world\"?','MEDIUM','D','setLength(2) keeps only the specified length, so the remaining content is \"hi\".'),(747,16,'Which method replaces characters between specified indexes with a given String?','MEDIUM','A','replace(begin, end, String) replaces content from the beginning index to end-1.'),(748,16,'Which method removes unused capacity from a StringBuffer?','MEDIUM','C','trimToSize() removes unused capacity or sets capacity to the length of the StringBuffer.'),(749,16,'Which method sets the capacity required for storing a String?','MEDIUM','B','ensureCapacity() sets the capacity for storing a String.'),(750,16,'Which constructor creates an empty StringBuffer with an initial capacity of 16?','EASY','A','StringBuffer() creates an empty StringBuffer with initial capacity 16.'),(751,16,'What is the capacity of new StringBuffer(\"abc\") according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The capacity is 16 plus the length of the given String: 16 + 3 = 19.'),(752,16,'Which method replaces the character at a specified index with a new character?','MEDIUM','C','setCharAt(index, new_char) replaces the character at the specified index.'),(753,16,'What is the nature of StringBuilder?','EASY','B','StringBuilder is mutable, meaning its content can be modified.'),(754,16,'Is the String Constant Pool applicable to StringBuilder?','EASY','C','The notes state that the String Constant Pool is not applicable to StringBuilder.'),(755,16,'Are StringBuilder methods synchronized according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','StringBuilder methods are non-synchronized.'),(756,16,'Which class is preferred when only one thread works on the String object and higher performance is desired?','MEDIUM','D','The notes recommend StringBuilder when only one thread works on the object because performance is higher.'),(757,16,'In which version was StringBuilder introduced according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes list StringBuilder as introduced in version 1.5.'),(758,16,'What does copying mean in Java according to the notes?','EASY','C','Copying means creating another object with the same data as an existing object.'),(759,16,'What happens when Student s2 = s1; is executed?','MEDIUM','A','Both s1 and s2 refer to the same object; a new object is not created.'),(760,16,'Which of the following is an example of creating a new object containing copied data?','MEDIUM','D','The notes give Student s2 = new Student(s1) as an example of creating a new object with copied data.'),(761,16,'How many main types of copying are mentioned in the notes?','EASY','B','The three types are shallow copy, deep copy and lazy copy.'),(762,16,'What is the default copying behavior of clone() according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The default clone() behavior is shallow copying.'),(763,16,'What is a shallow copy?','EASY','B','A shallow copy creates a new outer object, but the inner objects are shared.'),(764,16,'In shallow copying, what happens to the inner objects?','MEDIUM','C','The reference variables inside the copied object still refer to the same inner objects.'),(765,16,'What happens if s2.address.city is changed in a shallow copy?','MEDIUM','A','Both objects share the same Address object, so the change is visible through both references.'),(766,16,'Which is an advantage of shallow copying?','EASY','D','Shallow copying is faster and uses less memory.'),(767,16,'What is a deep copy?','EASY','B','A deep copy creates a new outer object and separate copies of the objects referred to by it.'),(768,16,'Which approach is mentioned in the notes for implementing a deep copy?','MEDIUM','C','The notes show using a deep-copy constructor that creates new inner objects.'),(769,16,'What is a drawback of deep copying?','MEDIUM','A','Deep copying is comparatively slower and uses more memory.'),(770,16,'What is lazy copying also known as?','EASY','D','Lazy copying is also called copy on write.'),(771,16,'When is a separate copy created in lazy copying?','MEDIUM','B','A separate copy is created only when modification is required.'),(772,16,'When is lazy copying useful according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','It is useful when copying is expensive, read operations are many and changes are rare.'),(773,16,'What is a Has-a relationship?','EASY','B','A Has-a relationship exists when one class contains a reference variable of another class.'),(774,16,'Which is an example of a Has-a relationship?','EASY','C','The notes give Student has an Address as an example.'),(775,16,'In a shallow copy of Student, what happens to the Address object?','MEDIUM','A','Both Student objects refer to the same Address object in a shallow copy.'),(776,16,'If Student 2 changes the shared Address city to Madurai in a shallow copy, what happens?','MEDIUM','D','Both Student objects share the same Address object, so both can show Madurai.'),(777,16,'In a deep copy of Student, how many separate Address objects are created for two students?','MEDIUM','B','Each Student object has a different Address object in a deep copy.'),(778,16,'In a deep copy, if Student 2 changes the city to Madurai, what happens to Student 1 city?','MEDIUM','A','The Address objects are separate, so Student 1 remains unchanged.'),(779,16,'Which copying type creates new references for inner objects?','MEDIUM','C','Deep copying creates separate inner objects and new references.'),(780,16,'Which copying type is comparatively faster and uses less memory?','EASY','B','Shallow copy is faster and uses less memory than deep copy.'),(781,16,'Which copying type is suitable when changes to the copied object must not affect the original?','MEDIUM','D','Deep copy creates independent inner objects, so changes remain independent.'),(782,16,'How can you identify whether two Student objects have a shallow or deep copy?','HARD','A','If both students share one Address object it is shallow copy; if each has a different Address object it is deep copy.'),(783,16,'What is a Has-a relationship?','EASY','B','A Has-a relationship exists when one class contains a reference variable of another class.'),(784,16,'Which is an example of a Has-a relationship?','EASY','C','The notes give Student has an Address as an example.'),(785,16,'In a shallow copy of Student, what happens to the Address object?','MEDIUM','A','Both Student objects refer to the same Address object in a shallow copy.'),(786,16,'If Student 2 changes the shared Address city to Madurai in a shallow copy, what happens?','MEDIUM','D','Both Student objects share the same Address object, so both can show Madurai.'),(787,16,'In a deep copy of Student, how many separate Address objects are created for two students?','MEDIUM','B','Each Student object has a different Address object in a deep copy.'),(788,16,'In a deep copy, if Student 2 changes the city to Madurai, what happens to Student 1 city?','MEDIUM','A','The Address objects are separate, so Student 1 remains unchanged.'),(789,16,'Which copying type creates new references for inner objects?','MEDIUM','C','Deep copying creates separate inner objects and new references.'),(790,16,'Which copying type is comparatively faster and uses less memory?','EASY','B','Shallow copy is faster and uses less memory than deep copy.'),(791,16,'Which copying type is suitable when changes to the copied object must not affect the original?','MEDIUM','D','Deep copy creates independent inner objects, so changes remain independent.'),(792,16,'How can you identify whether two Student objects have a shallow or deep copy?','HARD','A','If both students share one Address object it is shallow copy; if each has a different Address object it is deep copy.'),(793,37,'What does SQL stand for?','EASY','A','SQL stands for Structured Query Language.'),(794,37,'What is data?','EASY','B','Data is a raw fact that describes the property or attribute of an entity.'),(795,37,'What is a database?','EASY','C','A database stores data in a systematic and organized manner.'),(796,37,'Which of the following represents CRUD operations?','EASY','A','CRUD stands for Create, Read, Update and Delete.'),(797,37,'What is DBMS?','EASY','D','DBMS is software used to maintain and manage a database.'),(798,37,'Which two important features does DBMS provide according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes identify Security and Authorization as important DBMS features.'),(799,37,'How many types of DBMS are mentioned in the notes?','EASY','C','The notes list Network, Object-Oriented, Hierarchical and Relational DBMS.'),(800,37,'What is RDBMS?','EASY','A','RDBMS stands for Relational Database Management System.'),(801,37,'Who invented the Relational Model?','MEDIUM','D','The notes identify Edgar Franklin Codd as the inventor of the Relational Model.'),(802,37,'According to the notes, when does DBMS software become RDBMS?','HARD','B','The notes state that a DBMS following table format and E. F. Codd rules becomes RDBMS.'),(803,37,'What is the purpose of a data type in SQL?','EASY','B','A data type specifies what kind of data has to be stored in a column or table.'),(804,37,'How many data types are listed in the uploaded notes?','EASY','C','The six types are CHAR, VARCHAR/VARCHAR2, NUMBER, DATE, TIMESTAMP and Large Object.'),(805,37,'Which memory allocation does CHAR follow?','EASY','A','CHAR follows fixed-length memory allocation.'),(806,37,'What is the maximum character capacity of CHAR according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes state that CHAR can store up to 2000 characters.'),(807,37,'Which memory allocation does VARCHAR follow?','EASY','B','VARCHAR follows variable-length memory allocation.'),(808,37,'What is the maximum character capacity of VARCHAR/VARCHAR2 according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that VARCHAR/VARCHAR2 can store up to 4000 characters.'),(809,37,'What is the difference between VARCHAR and VARCHAR2 according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that VARCHAR and VARCHAR2 are the same.'),(810,37,'In NUMBER(5,2), what does precision represent?','HARD','D','Precision is used to store integer values and is mandatory; scale is used for decimal values and is optional.'),(811,37,'Which data type is used to store date and time together?','EASY','B','TIMESTAMP is used to store date and time together.'),(812,37,'Which Large Object type is used to store binary data such as images and MP3/MP4 files?','MEDIUM','C','BLOB stores large amounts of binary data such as images and media files.'),(813,37,'What is a constraint in SQL?','EASY','B','A constraint is assigned to a column for extra validation.'),(814,37,'How many types of constraints are listed in the notes?','EASY','C','The five types are UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, PRIMARY KEY and FOREIGN KEY.'),(815,37,'Which constraint prevents duplicate or repeated values?','EASY','A','UNIQUE does not allow duplicated or repeated values in the assigned column.'),(816,37,'Which constraint ensures that a column must contain a value?','EASY','D','NOT NULL does not accept NULL values and makes the column mandatory.'),(817,37,'What does a CHECK constraint use along with a column?','MEDIUM','B','CHECK is assigned to a column along with a condition.'),(818,37,'What happens when a value satisfies the CHECK constraint condition?','MEDIUM','C','The CHECK constraint accepts a value when its condition is satisfied.'),(819,37,'What is the main purpose of a Primary Key?','EASY','A','A Primary Key is used to identify records uniquely from a table.'),(820,37,'A Primary Key is a combination of which two constraints?','MEDIUM','D','The notes describe Primary Key as a combination of UNIQUE and NOT NULL.'),(821,37,'What is the purpose of a Foreign Key?','EASY','B','A Foreign Key is used to establish a connection between tables.'),(822,37,'Where is a Foreign Key present according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','A Foreign Key is present in the child table but belongs to the parent table.'),(823,37,'How many subsets or languages of SQL are listed in the notes?','EASY','C','The five subsets are DDL, DML, DCL, TCL and DQL.'),(824,37,'What is the full form of DDL?','EASY','A','DDL stands for Data Definition Language.'),(825,37,'What is the main purpose of DDL?','MEDIUM','B','DDL is used to create or alter the structure of a table.'),(826,37,'Which of the following commands belongs to DDL?','EASY','D','CREATE, ALTER, RENAME, TRUNCATE and DROP are listed as DDL commands.'),(827,37,'What is the full form of DML?','EASY','C','DML stands for Data Manipulation Language.'),(828,37,'Which set contains only DML commands according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE are the three DML commands listed in the notes.'),(829,37,'What is the purpose of DCL?','MEDIUM','B','DCL is used to control access over the data.'),(830,37,'Which commands are included in DCL?','EASY','D','GRANT and REVOKE are the two DCL commands listed in the notes.'),(831,37,'Which of the following is a TCL command?','EASY','A','COMMIT, ROLLBACK and SAVEPOINT are the TCL commands listed in the notes.'),(832,37,'What is the purpose of DQL?','MEDIUM','C','DQL is used to fetch or retrieve data from a table or database.'),(833,37,'Which SQL clause is used to retrieve data from a table?','EASY','A','The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data.'),(834,37,'Which clause specifies the table name in a SELECT statement?','EASY','C','The FROM clause accepts the table name as its argument.'),(835,37,'What is the order of execution of a basic SELECT query according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The execution order listed is FROM, WHERE and SELECT.'),(836,37,'What happens if the table name is missing or incorrect in the FROM clause?','MEDIUM','D','The notes state that an error occurs if the table name is missing or contains an error.'),(837,37,'What is the purpose of the SELECT clause according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','After FROM executes, SELECT produces the final output using its argument.'),(838,37,'What is an alias in SQL?','EASY','C','An alias is an alternative name assigned to a column name or expression in the result table.'),(839,37,'Which keyword can be used to assign an alias to a column or expression?','EASY','B','The notes show that an alias can be assigned with or without the AS keyword.'),(840,37,'Which statement about assigning an alias is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','An alias can be assigned with or without using AS.'),(841,37,'What is the purpose of DISTINCT in a SELECT statement?','EASY','A','DISTINCT removes duplicate or repeated values from the result table.'),(842,37,'Where should DISTINCT be placed in the SELECT clause according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that DISTINCT should be the first argument for the SELECT clause.'),(843,37,'What is the primary purpose of the WHERE clause?','EASY','B','WHERE is used to filter records.'),(844,37,'What type of value does a WHERE filter condition return according to the notes?','EASY','C','The filter condition returns a Boolean value: True or False.'),(845,37,'When does the WHERE clause execute in a basic SELECT query?','MEDIUM','A','WHERE executes after the FROM clause.'),(846,37,'How does the WHERE clause execute according to the notes?','EASY','D','The WHERE clause executes row by row.'),(847,37,'Which operators can be used to combine multiple conditions in a WHERE clause?','EASY','B','Logical operators are used to combine multiple conditions in WHERE.'),(848,37,'Which is a valid filter-condition format shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','A','A filter condition can compare a column or expression with a value.'),(849,37,'Which of the following is another valid filter-condition format?','MEDIUM','C','The notes also allow comparison between two column names or expressions.'),(850,37,'Which logical operator returns TRUE only when all conditions are satisfied?','EASY','D','AND returns TRUE when all the conditions are satisfied.'),(851,37,'What is the minimum number of conditions required to use AND?','EASY','B','According to the notes, AND requires a minimum of two conditions.'),(852,37,'What happens when AND and OR are used together without appropriate brackets, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that AND gets the first execution priority, which may produce invalid output. Brackets are suggested for OR conditions.'),(853,37,'How many types of SQL operators are listed in the notes?','EASY','C','The notes list eight types of SQL operators.'),(854,37,'Which of the following is an arithmetic operator in SQL?','EASY','A','The arithmetic operators listed are +, -, * and /.'),(855,37,'Which operators are listed as comparison operators in the notes?','EASY','B','The comparison operators listed are = and !=.'),(856,37,'Which of the following is a relational operator?','EASY','D','The relational operators listed are >, <, >= and <=.'),(857,37,'Which symbol is used as the concatenation operator in the notes?','EASY','C','The concatenation operator listed in the notes is ||.'),(858,37,'Which of the following contains only logical operators?','EASY','A','AND, OR and NOT are the logical operators listed in the notes.'),(859,37,'Which operator returns TRUE if all the conditions are satisfied?','EASY','B','AND returns TRUE only when all conditions are satisfied.'),(860,37,'Which operator returns TRUE if any one condition is satisfied?','EASY','D','OR returns TRUE when any one condition is satisfied.'),(861,37,'Which logical operator is listed along with AND and OR?','EASY','C','The three logical operators listed are AND, OR and NOT.'),(862,37,'Which operator category includes the symbols +, -, * and /?','MEDIUM','A','These four symbols are listed under arithmetic operators.'),(863,37,'Which operator is described as a special operator for the equals operator?','EASY','A','IN is a special operator for the equals operator and accepts multiple values on the RHS.'),(864,37,'What is the main difference between = and IN according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The equals operator takes one value on the RHS, whereas IN can take multiple values.'),(865,37,'Which operator rejects the matching values instead of selecting them?','EASY','B','NOT IN is similar to IN, but rejects the specified matching values.'),(866,37,'Which operator is used to fetch a range of values?','EASY','D','BETWEEN is used to fetch a range of values.'),(867,37,'Are the boundary values included when using BETWEEN?','EASY','A','The notes state that BETWEEN includes both boundary values.'),(868,37,'Which operator rejects values within a specified range?','EASY','C','NOT BETWEEN rejects values in the specified range.'),(869,37,'Which operator is used to compare NULL values according to the notes?','EASY','B','IS is used to compare NULL values.'),(870,37,'Which syntax is used to select NULL values according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes provide the syntax column_name IS NULL.'),(871,37,'What does the IS NOT operator do according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','IS NOT is similar to IS, but rejects the NULL values instead of selecting them.'),(872,37,'Which of the following is a special operator listed in the notes?','EASY','C','LIKE is included in the list of special operators.'),(873,37,'What is the DUAL table?','EASY','B','DUAL is a dummy table containing only one column. It is used when the value is not present in any table.'),(874,37,'Which function retrieves the number of characters or digits from a given string?','EASY','A','LENGTH() returns the number of characters or digits in a given string.'),(875,37,'What is the output of SELECT LENGTH(\'QST\') FROM DUAL according to the notes?','EASY','C','The string QST contains three characters.'),(876,37,'What is the purpose of the CONCAT() function?','EASY','D','CONCAT() is used to join two strings only.'),(877,37,'Which operator is suggested in the notes for concatenating more than two strings?','MEDIUM','B','The notes suggest using the concatenation operator (||) for more than two strings.'),(878,37,'Which function converts a given string into uppercase?','EASY','A','UPPER() converts the given string to uppercase.'),(879,37,'Which function converts a given string into lowercase?','EASY','C','LOWER() converts the given string to lowercase.'),(880,37,'What does INITCAP() do?','MEDIUM','D','INITCAP() converts the first letter of a given string into an initial capital letter and the remaining characters into small letters.'),(881,37,'Which function reverses a given string?','EASY','B','REVERSE() is used to reverse or transpose the given string.'),(882,37,'What is the purpose of the MOD() function?','EASY','A','MOD() retrieves the modulus or remainder of two numeric values.'),(883,37,'Which function retrieves the remainder of two numbers?','EASY','B','MOD() retrieves the modulus or remainder of two numeric values.'),(884,37,'What is the output of SELECT MOD(4,2) FROM DUAL?','EASY','C','MOD(4,2) returns the remainder, which is 0.'),(885,37,'Which condition retrieves employees having even employee numbers according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes use MOD(EMPNO,2) = 0 to retrieve even employee numbers.'),(886,37,'Which function rounds a number based on its decimal value?','EASY','D','ROUND() rounds a number based on the decimal value.'),(887,37,'What is the output of SELECT ROUND(8.4) FROM DUAL according to the notes?','EASY','B','A decimal value less than 0.5 rounds down to the same integer value.'),(888,37,'What is the output of SELECT ROUND(8.5) FROM DUAL according to the notes?','EASY','C','A decimal value greater than or equal to 0.5 rounds up to the next number.'),(889,37,'Which function cuts off the decimal part without rounding up?','EASY','A','TRUNC() cuts off the decimal part irrespective of its value.'),(890,37,'What is the output of SELECT TRUNC(8.8) FROM DUAL according to the notes?','EASY','D','TRUNC(8.8) returns 8 by cutting off the decimal part.'),(891,37,'Which statement correctly describes ROUND() and TRUNC()?','MEDIUM','B','ROUND() depends on the decimal value, whereas TRUNC() always cuts off the decimal part.'),(892,37,'What is the output of SELECT TRUNC(6.9) FROM DUAL according to the notes?','EASY','A','The notes give TRUNC(6.9) as 6.'),(893,37,'Which function is used to add a number of months to a given date?','EASY','B','ADD_MONTHS() adds a specified number of months to a given date.'),(894,37,'What does MONTHS_BETWEEN() return?','EASY','C','MONTHS_BETWEEN() retrieves the number of months between two date values.'),(895,37,'Which function retrieves the last date of a given month?','EASY','A','LAST_DAY() returns the last date of the month for the given date.'),(896,37,'According to the notes, what is the result of LAST_DAY(\'14-FEB-24\')?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show that the last date of February 2024 is 29-FEB-24.'),(897,37,'Which function converts a given date into a string using a format model?','EASY','B','TO_CHAR() converts a date into a string in the specified format model.'),(898,37,'Which TO_CHAR() format model represents a four-digit year?','EASY','C','YYYY represents the year using four digits.'),(899,37,'Which TO_CHAR() format model represents the full month name?','EASY','A','MONTH represents the full month name.'),(900,37,'Which TO_CHAR() format model represents the 24-hour format?','EASY','D','HH24 represents the hour in 24-hour format.'),(901,37,'Which TO_CHAR() format model represents the three-letter weekday?','MEDIUM','B','DY represents the three-letter weekday, such as THU.'),(902,37,'Which TO_CHAR() format model represents minutes?','EASY','C','MI represents minutes in the format model.'),(903,37,'What is the full form of NVL() according to the notes?','EASY','A','NVL stands for Null Value Logic.'),(904,37,'What is the purpose of NVL()?','EASY','C','NVL() substitutes Argument2 when Argument1 contains NULL.'),(905,37,'What happens when Argument1 in NVL(Argument1, Argument2) is NULL?','MEDIUM','B','If Argument1 is NULL, Argument2 is substituted in its place.'),(906,37,'What does NVL(COMM,0) return when COMM is NULL?','EASY','D','NVL replaces the NULL value in COMM with 0.'),(907,37,'Which function is used to replace a string with a new string?','EASY','A','REPLACE() is used to replace a string with a new string.'),(908,37,'Which function replaces the character Q with J in the string QSPIDERS?','MEDIUM','C','REPLACE(\'QSPIDERS\',\'Q\',\'J\') replaces Q with J.'),(909,37,'What is the output of REPLACE(\'BANANA\',\'NA\',\'HA\') according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show the output as BAHAHA.'),(910,37,'Which function extracts a part of a given string?','EASY','D','SUBSTR() extracts a part of the original string.'),(911,37,'Which is the correct syntax of SUBSTR() according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The syntax is SUBSTR(original string, position, optional length).'),(912,37,'What happens when the length argument is omitted in SUBSTR(\'QSPIDERS\',2)?','MEDIUM','C','Without the length argument, SUBSTR() extracts characters from the given position until the end of the string.'),(913,37,'What is another name for a Multi Row Function?','EASY','B','Multi Row Functions are also known as Aggregate Functions or Group Functions.'),(914,37,'Which function fetches the maximum value present in a column?','EASY','C','MAX() retrieves the maximum value present in a column.'),(915,37,'Which function fetches the minimum value present in a column?','EASY','A','MIN() retrieves the minimum value present in a column.'),(916,37,'Which function retrieves the total or summation of values in a column?','EASY','D','SUM() calculates the total of values present in a column.'),(917,37,'Which function retrieves the average value present in a column?','EASY','B','AVG() retrieves the average value present in a column.'),(918,37,'Which function fetches the number of values present in a column?','EASY','C','COUNT() retrieves the number of values present in a column.'),(919,37,'If a Multi Row Function receives n inputs, how many outputs does it produce?','MEDIUM','A','A Multi Row Function combines inputs and produces one output.'),(920,37,'Which clause should not contain a Multi Row Function according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes state that a Multi Row Function should not be used in the WHERE clause.'),(921,37,'Which Multi Row Function can take an asterisk (*) as its argument?','MEDIUM','B','COUNT() is the only Multi Row Function that can take * as an argument.'),(922,37,'Which statement about Multi Row Functions is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that Multi Row Functions ignore NULL values.'),(923,37,'What is the purpose of the GROUP BY clause?','EASY','B','GROUP BY is used to group records.'),(924,37,'What can be passed as an argument to GROUP BY?','EASY','C','The notes state that a column name or expression can be passed.'),(925,37,'Which clause executes immediately before GROUP BY in the execution order given in the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY and SELECT.'),(926,37,'What is the execution order when using GROUP BY according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY and SELECT.'),(927,37,'What happens after GROUP BY completes its execution?','EASY','B','After GROUP BY execution, groups are produced.'),(928,37,'According to the notes, how does GROUP BY execute?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that GROUP BY executes row by row and produces groups after completion.'),(929,37,'What happens if a column in SELECT is not present in the GROUP BY clause or GROUP BY expression, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that an error must occur in this situation.'),(930,37,'What is the execution of the GROUP BY clause called in the notes?','EASY','D','The notes refer to its execution as Group by Expression.'),(931,37,'Which clause can be used with GROUP BY according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show WHERE as an optional clause in the GROUP BY syntax.'),(932,37,'Which is the correct GROUP BY syntax according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The syntax places the optional WHERE clause before GROUP BY.'),(933,37,'What is the purpose of the HAVING clause?','EASY','B','HAVING is used to filter groups.'),(934,37,'Which clause is mandatory when using HAVING according to the notes?','EASY','C','The notes state that GROUP BY is mandatory for HAVING.'),(935,37,'When does HAVING execute?','EASY','A','HAVING executes after the GROUP BY clause.'),(936,37,'How does the HAVING clause execute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','HAVING executes group by group.'),(937,37,'What type of condition can be passed to the HAVING clause?','MEDIUM','B','The notes describe a group filter condition as a Multi Row Function, operator and value.'),(938,37,'What does a group filter condition mean according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes define it as MRF (Multi Row Function), operator and value.'),(939,37,'Which logical operators can be used to pass multiple conditions in HAVING?','EASY','A','The notes state that multiple conditions can be passed using logical operators.'),(940,37,'Which clause executes immediately before HAVING in the execution order given in the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The execution order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING and SELECT.'),(941,37,'What is the correct execution order when using HAVING according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING and SELECT.'),(942,37,'Which is the correct HAVING clause syntax according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','HAVING is placed after GROUP BY and takes a group filter condition.'),(943,37,'What is a subquery?','EASY','B','A query written inside another query is known as a subquery.'),(944,37,'Which query starts execution first in a normal subquery?','EASY','C','The inner query executes first and produces partial output.'),(945,37,'What happens to the output produced by the inner query?','MEDIUM','A','The inner query output is passed as input to the outer query.'),(946,37,'When do we go for a subquery according to the notes?','EASY','D','The notes state that a subquery is used whenever the question contains an unknown value.'),(947,37,'What is a Single Row Subquery?','EASY','B','A subquery producing only one output is called a Single Row Subquery.'),(948,37,'What is a Multi Row Subquery?','EASY','C','A subquery producing more than one output is called a Multi Row Subquery.'),(949,37,'Which operators are used to compare the output of a Single Row Subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','Single Row Subqueries are compared using normal operators such as =, > and <.'),(950,37,'Which operators can be used with a Multi Row Subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','Multi Row Subqueries use special operators such as IN, ALL and ANY.'),(951,37,'What error occurs when a Single Row Subquery returns more than one row while using a normal operator?','MEDIUM','B','The notes specify the error: Single row subquery returns more than one row.'),(952,37,'What is a Nested Subquery?','EASY','C','A subquery written inside another subquery is known as a Nested Subquery.'),(953,37,'What is a correlated subquery?','EASY','B','A query in which the inner and outer queries are interdependent is called a correlated subquery.'),(954,37,'Which query starts execution first in a correlated subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','In a correlated subquery, the outer query starts execution first.'),(955,37,'What does the outer query produce first in a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','C','The outer query starts first and produces a partial output that is passed to the inner query.'),(956,37,'What happens to the partial output of the outer query?','MEDIUM','D','The partial output of the outer query is given as input to the inner query.'),(957,37,'What is the relationship between the inner and outer queries in a correlated subquery?','EASY','B','Both queries are interdependent on each other during execution.'),(958,37,'Which condition must be maintained inside the inner query according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that a joining condition must be maintained inside the inner query.'),(959,37,'A correlated subquery works on the principle of which two concepts according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that a correlated subquery works on the principle of both subqueries and joins.'),(960,37,'In a normal subquery, which query executes first?','EASY','D','In a normal subquery, the inner query executes first independently.'),(961,37,'How does the normal subquery differ from a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','B','A normal subquery executes independently first, while a correlated subquery has interdependent inner and outer queries.'),(962,37,'Which statement correctly describes a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','A','The outer query passes partial output to the inner query, and the inner query returns partial output used by the outer query.'),(963,37,'What are ALL and ANY classified as in SQL according to the notes?','EASY','B','ALL and ANY are subquery operators used along with subqueries.'),(964,37,'When is the ALL operator used according to the notes?','EASY','C','ALL is used along with a subquery.'),(965,37,'When does the ALL operator return TRUE?','MEDIUM','A','ALL returns TRUE only when the condition is satisfied against all returned values.'),(966,37,'When does the ANY operator return TRUE?','MEDIUM','D','ANY returns TRUE when the condition is satisfied against at least one returned value.'),(967,37,'Which operator requires the condition to be satisfied against every value returned by a subquery?','EASY','B','ALL checks whether the condition is satisfied against all returned values.'),(968,37,'Which operator requires the condition to be satisfied against at least one value returned by a subquery?','EASY','C','ANY returns TRUE if the condition is satisfied against any one returned value.'),(969,37,'A subquery returns three values. 