lf Join is the process of joining a table by itself.'),(1004,37,'When do we use a Self Join according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','Self Join is used when the required data is present in different records of the same table.'),(1005,37,'What must be assigned to the table name in a Self Join?','EASY','A','The notes state that an alias must be assigned because the same table is joined with itself.'),(1006,37,'Why is an alias required in a Self Join according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The same table is joined with itself, so aliases are assigned to distinguish its references.'),(1007,37,'In the employee-manager Self Join example, which column represents the employee table reference?','MEDIUM','B','The notes state that the table reference from which MGR is taken is considered the Employee table.'),(1008,37,'In the employee-manager Self Join example, which column represents the manager table reference?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that the table reference from which Emp-no is taken is considered the Manager table.'),(1009,37,'In a Self Join, how should each argument be defined according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','Each argument must be defined with its table name or alias.'),(1010,37,'Which is the ANSI syntax structure for a Self Join?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show two aliases for the same table and use ON for the joining condition.'),(1011,37,'Which condition is used as an example in the Self Join notes?','MEDIUM','A','The example joining condition is E1.MGR = E2.Emp-no.'),(1012,37,'Which clause is used for the joining condition in the Oracle Self Join syntax shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The Oracle syntax uses the WHERE clause to specify the joining condition.'),(1013,37,'What is the purpose of an Outer Join?','EASY','B','Outer Join is used to fetch unmatched records from tables.'),(1014,37,'How many types of Outer Join are covered in the notes?','EASY','C','The three types are Left Outer Join, Right Outer Join and Full Outer Join.'),(1015,37,'What does a Left Outer Join retrieve?','EASY','A','It retrieves unmatched records from the left table along with matched records.'),(1016,37,'Which keyword is used in ANSI syntax for Left Outer Join?','EASY','D','The ANSI syntax uses LEFT OUTER JOIN between the table names.'),(1017,37,'In the Oracle Left Outer Join syntax shown in the notes, where is the (+) symbol placed?','MEDIUM','B','The (+) symbol is placed on the right table column in the joining condition.'),(1018,37,'What does a Right Outer Join retrieve?','EASY','C','It retrieves unmatched records from the right table along with matched records from the left table.'),(1019,37,'In the Oracle Right Outer Join syntax shown in the notes, where is the (+) symbol placed?','MEDIUM','A','The (+) symbol is placed after the left table column in the joining condition.'),(1020,37,'What does a Full Outer Join retrieve?','EASY','D','It retrieves unmatched records from both left and right tables along with matched records.'),(1021,37,'Which syntax is shown for Full Outer Join in the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show the ANSI FULL OUTER JOIN syntax with an ON condition.'),(1022,37,'According to the notes, which join does not have an Oracle-only (+) syntax equivalent?','MEDIUM','C','The notes specify that Full Outer Join is written using ANSI FULL OUTER JOIN syntax.'),(1023,37,'What is the purpose of Set Operators in SQL?','EASY','B','Set Operators combine the results of two or more SELECT statements.'),(1024,37,'How many Set Operators are covered in the notes?','EASY','C','The four operators are UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT and MINUS.'),(1025,37,'Which Set Operator combines results and removes duplicate rows?','EASY','A','UNION combines SELECT results and eliminates duplicate values from the result set.'),(1026,37,'Which operator combines results without removing duplicates?','EASY','D','UNION ALL returns the combined result without removing duplicate rows.'),(1027,37,'Which operator returns only the common rows from two SELECT statements?','EASY','B','INTERSECT returns rows that are common to both SELECT statements.'),(1028,37,'Which operator returns rows present in the first query but absent in the second query?','MEDIUM','C','MINUS returns rows from the first query that are not present in the second query.'),(1029,37,'What happens to duplicate rows in the result of INTERSECT according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','INTERSECT does not return duplicate rows.'),(1030,37,'What is the default ordering of INTERSECT and MINUS results according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','Both operators arrange their result data in ascending order by default.'),(1031,37,'What must be the same in both SELECT statements when using a Set Operator?','MEDIUM','B','Both SELECT statements must select the same number of columns with matching or compatible datatypes.'),(1032,37,'Table First contains rows A, B, C. Table Second contains rows C, D, E. What does First MINUS Second return?','MEDIUM','A','MINUS returns rows present in the first query but absent in the second query.'),(1033,37,'What is the purpose of the ORDER BY clause?','EASY','B','ORDER BY displays the result table in ascending or descending order.'),(1034,37,'Which two sorting orders are supported by ORDER BY according to the notes?','EASY','C','ORDER BY displays results in ascending or descending order.'),(1035,37,'What can be passed as an argument to ORDER BY?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that a column name or an expression can be passed as an argument.'),(1036,37,'Where is the ORDER BY clause written in a SQL query?','EASY','D','ORDER BY is written at the end of the query.'),(1037,37,'Which clause executes after all other clauses according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','ORDER BY is the only clause described in the notes as executing at the end.'),(1038,37,'Which statement correctly uses ORDER BY with a column name?','MEDIUM','C','The syntax places ORDER BY followed by the column name after the FROM clause.'),(1039,37,'Which keyword is used to specify descending 