es?','EASY','A','NVL stands for Null Value Logic.'),(1164,37,'What is the purpose of NVL()?','EASY','C','NVL() substitutes Argument2 when Argument1 contains NULL.'),(1165,37,'What happens when Argument1 in NVL(Argument1, Argument2) is NULL?','MEDIUM','B','If Argument1 is NULL, Argument2 is substituted in its place.'),(1166,37,'What does NVL(COMM,0) return when COMM is NULL?','EASY','D','NVL replaces the NULL value in COMM with 0.'),(1167,37,'Which function is used to replace a string with a new string?','EASY','A','REPLACE() is used to replace a string with a new string.'),(1168,37,'Which function replaces the character Q with J in the string QSPIDERS?','MEDIUM','C','REPLACE(\'QSPIDERS\',\'Q\',\'J\') replaces Q with J.'),(1169,37,'What is the output of REPLACE(\'BANANA\',\'NA\',\'HA\') according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show the output as BAHAHA.'),(1170,37,'Which function extracts a part of a given string?','EASY','D','SUBSTR() extracts a part of the original string.'),(1171,37,'Which is the correct syntax of SUBSTR() according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The syntax is SUBSTR(original string, position, optional length).'),(1172,37,'What happens when the length argument is omitted in SUBSTR(\'QSPIDERS\',2)?','MEDIUM','C','Without the length argument, SUBSTR() extracts characters from the given position until the end of the string.'),(1173,37,'What is another name for a Multi Row Function?','EASY','B','Multi Row Functions are also known as Aggregate Functions or Group Functions.'),(1174,37,'Which function fetches the maximum value present in a column?','EASY','C','MAX() retrieves the maximum value present in a column.'),(1175,37,'Which function fetches the minimum value present in a column?','EASY','A','MIN() retrieves the minimum value present in a column.'),(1176,37,'Which function retrieves the total or summation of values in a column?','EASY','D','SUM() calculates the total of values present in a column.'),(1177,37,'Which function retrieves the average value present in a column?','EASY','B','AVG() retrieves the average value present in a column.'),(1178,37,'Which function fetches the number of values present in a column?','EASY','C','COUNT() retrieves the number of values present in a column.'),(1179,37,'If a Multi Row Function receives n inputs, how many outputs does it produce?','MEDIUM','A','A Multi Row Function combines inputs and produces one output.'),(1180,37,'Which clause should not contain a Multi Row Function according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes state that a Multi Row Function should not be used in the WHERE clause.'),(1181,37,'Which Multi Row Function can take an asterisk (*) as its argument?','MEDIUM','B','COUNT() is the only Multi Row Function that can take * as an argument.'),(1182,37,'Which statement about Multi Row Functions is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that Multi Row Functions ignore NULL values.'),(1183,37,'What is the purpose of the GROUP BY clause?','EASY','B','GROUP BY is used to group records.'),(1184,37,'What can be passed as an argument to GROUP BY?','EASY','C','The notes state that a column name or expression can be passed.'),(1185,37,'Which clause executes immediately before GROUP BY in the execution order given in the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY and SELECT.'),(1186,37,'What is the execution order when using GROUP BY according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY and SELECT.'),(1187,37,'What happens after GROUP BY completes its execution?','EASY','B','After GROUP BY execution, groups are produced.'),(1188,37,'According to the notes, how does GROUP BY execute?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that GROUP BY executes row by row and produces groups after completion.'),(1189,37,'What happens if a column in SELECT is not present in the GROUP BY clause or GROUP BY expression, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that an error must occur in this situation.'),(1190,37,'What is the execution of the GROUP BY clause called in the notes?','EASY','D','The notes refer to its execution as Group by Expression.'),(1191,37,'Which clause can be used with GROUP BY according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show WHERE as an optional clause in the GROUP BY syntax.'),(1192,37,'Which is the correct GROUP BY syntax according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The syntax places the optional WHERE clause before GROUP BY.'),(1193,37,'What is the purpose of the HAVING clause?','EASY','B','HAVING is used to filter groups.'),(1194,37,'Which clause is mandatory when using HAVING according to the notes?','EASY','C','The notes state that GROUP BY is mandatory for HAVING.'),(1195,37,'When does HAVING execute?','EASY','A','HAVING executes after the GROUP BY clause.'),(1196,37,'How does the HAVING clause execute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','HAVING executes group by group.'),(1197,37,'What type of condition can be passed to the HAVING clause?','MEDIUM','B','The notes describe a group filter condition as a Multi Row Function, operator and value.'),(1198,37,'What does a group filter condition mean according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes define it as MRF (Multi Row Function), operator and value.'),(1199,37,'Which logical operators can be used to pass multiple conditions in HAVING?','EASY','A','The notes state that multiple conditions can be passed using logical operators.'),(1200,37,'Which clause executes immediately before HAVING in the execution order given in the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The execution order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING and SELECT.'),(1201,37,'What is the correct execution order when using HAVING according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING and SELECT.'),(1202,37,'Which is the correct HAVING clause syntax according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','HAVING is placed after GROUP BY and takes a group filter condition.'),(1203,37,'What is a subquery?','EASY','B','A query written inside another query is known as a subquery.'),(1204,37,'Which query starts execution first in a normal subquery?','EASY','C','The inner query executes first and produces partial output.'),(1205,37,'What happens to the output produced by the inner query?','MEDIUM','A','The inner query output is passed as input to the outer query.'),(1206,37,'When do we go for a subquery according to the notes?','EASY','D','The notes state that a subquery is used whenever the question contains an unknown value.'),(1207,37,'What is a Single Row Subquery?','EASY','B','A subquery producing only one output is called a Single Row Subquery.'),(1208,37,'What is a Multi Row Subquery?','EASY','C','A subquery producing more than one output is called a Multi Row Subquery.'),(1209,37,'Which operators are used to compare the output of a Single Row Subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','Single Row Subqueries are compared using normal operators such as =, > and <.'),(1210,37,'Which operators can be used with a Multi Row Subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','Multi Row Subqueries use special operators such as IN, ALL and ANY.'),(1211,37,'What error occurs when a Single Row Subquery returns more than one row while using a normal operator?','MEDIUM','B','The notes specify the error: Single row subquery returns more than one row.'),(1212,37,'What is a Nested Subquery?','EASY','C','A subquery written inside another subquery is known as a Nested Subquery.'),(1213,37,'What is a correlated subquery?','EASY','B','A query in which the inner and outer queries are interdependent is called a correlated subquery.'),(1214,37,'Which query starts execution first in a correlated subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','In a correlated subquery, the outer query starts execution first.'),(1215,37,'What does the outer query produce first in a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','C','The outer query starts first and produces a partial output that is passed to the inner query.'),(1216,37,'What happens to the partial output of the outer query?','MEDIUM','D','The partial output of the outer query is given as input to the inner query.'),(1217,37,'What is the relationship between the inner and outer queries in a correlated subquery?','EASY','B','Both queries are interdependent on each other during execution.'),(1218,37,'Which condition must be maintained inside the inner query according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that a joining condition must be maintained inside the inner query.'),(1219,37,'A correlated subquery works on the principle of which two concepts according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that a correlated subquery works on the principle of both subqueries and joins.'),(1220,37,'In a normal subquery, which query executes first?','EASY','D','In a normal subquery, the inner query executes first independently.'),(1221,37,'How does the normal subquery differ from a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','B','A normal subquery executes independently first, while a correlated subquery has interdependent inner and outer queries.'),(1222,37,'Which statement correctly describes a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','A','The outer query passes partial output to the inner query, and the inner query returns partial output used by the outer query.'),(1223,37,'What are ALL and ANY classified as in SQL according to the notes?','EASY','B','ALL and ANY are subquery operators used along with subqueries.'),(1224,37,'When is the ALL operator used according to the notes?','EASY','C','ALL is used along with a subquery.'),(1225,37,'When does the ALL operator return TRUE?','MEDIUM','A','ALL returns TRUE only when the condition is satisfied against all returned values.'),(1226,37,'When does the ANY operator return TRUE?','MEDIUM','D','ANY returns TRUE when the condition is satisfied against at least one returned value.'),(1227,37,'Which operator requires the condition to be satisfied against every value returned by a subquery?','EASY','B','ALL checks whether the condition is satisfied against all returned values.'),(1228,37,'Which operator requires the condition to be satisfied against at least one value returned by a subquery?','EASY','C','ANY returns TRUE if the condition is satisfied against any one returned value.'),(1229,37,'A subquery returns three values. Which operator checks the condition against all three values?','MEDIUM','A','ALL requires the condition to be satisfied against every returned value.'),(1230,37,'A subquery returns multiple values. Which operator checks whether the condition is satisfied against at least one of them?','MEDIUM','D','ANY returns TRUE when at least one returned value satisfies the condition.'),(1231,37,'Which statement correctly describes the difference between ALL and ANY?','MEDIUM','B','ALL requires every value to satisfy the condition, while ANY requires at least one value to satisfy it.'),(1232,37,'Which of the following is a subquery operator mentioned in the notes?','EASY','C','ALL and ANY are both listed as subquery operators.'),(1233,37,'EXISTS and NOT EXISTS belong to which category of SQL operators?','EASY','B','The notes classify EXISTS and NOT EXISTS as subquery operators.'),(1234,37,'Which operator returns TRUE when the subquery produces a non-NULL value according to the notes?','EASY','C','The notes state that EXISTS returns TRUE when the subquery produces a non-NULL value.'),(1235,37,'Which operator returns TRUE when the subquery produces a NULL value according to the notes?','EASY','A','The notes state that NOT EXISTS returns TRUE when the subquery produces a NULL value.'),(1236,37,'Which operator is used in the notes to check for matching department records?','MEDIUM','D','The example uses EXISTS with a subquery matching Dept.Deptno and EMP.Deptno.'),(1237,37,'In the EXISTS example from the notes, which tables are used?','MEDIUM','B','The example retrieves department names from Dept and checks matching department numbers in EMP.'),(1238,37,'Which column is matched between Dept and EMP in the EXISTS example?','MEDIUM','C','The joining condition is Dept.Deptno = EMP.Deptno.'),(1239,37,'Which operator is used to retrieve department names based on the NOT EXISTS example in the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The NOT EXISTS example selects Dname from Dept and checks the subquery against EMP.'),(1240,37,'Which statement correctly describes EXISTS and NOT EXISTS according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes describe EXISTS as returning TRUE for a non-NULL result and NOT EXISTS as returning TRUE for a NULL result.'),(1241,37,'In the EXISTS example, which column is selected by the inner query?','MEDIUM','B','The inner query selects Deptno from EMP.'),(1242,37,'Which operator pair is listed under subquery operators in the uploaded notes?','EASY','C','The notes list EXIST and NOT EXIST as subquery operators.'),(1243,37,'What is the main purpose of joins in SQL?','EASY','B','Joins are used to fetch or retrieve data from multiple tables simultaneously.'),(1244,37,'What happens in a Cross Join?','EASY','C','A record from table 2 merges with all records of table 1.'),(1245,37,'What is the number of records produced by a Cross Join?','MEDIUM','A','The number of records equals the product of the record counts of both tables.'),(1246,37,'How many columns will the result of a Cross Join contain?','MEDIUM','D','The result has a number of columns equal to the sum of the columns in both tables.'),(1247,37,'Which is the ANSI syntax for Cross Join?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show CROSS JOIN between the two table names in ANSI syntax.'),(1248,37,'What is the purpose of an Inner Join?','EASY','A','Inner Join fetches only matched records from the tables.'),(1249,37,'Which keyword specifies the joining condition in ANSI Inner Join syntax?','MEDIUM','C','The notes use the ON keyword followed by the joining condition.'),(1250,37,'Where is the joining condition specified in the Oracle syntax for Inner Join shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The Oracle syntax in the notes uses WHERE for the joining condition.'),(1251,37,'How does Natural Join behave when there is a relation between the tables, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','Natural Join behaves as an Inner Join when there is a relation between the tables.'),(1252,37,'How does Natural Join behave when there is no relation between the tables, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that Natural Join behaves as a Cross Join when there is no relation between the tables.'),(1253,37,'What is a Self Join?','EASY','B','Self Join is the process of joining a table by itself.'),(1254,37,'When do we use a Self Join according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','Self Join is used when the required data is present in different records of the same table.'),(1255,37,'What must be assigned to the table name in a Self Join?','EASY','A','The notes state that an alias must be assigned because the same table is joined with itself.'),(1256,37,'Why is an alias required in a Self Join according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The same table is joined with itself, so aliases are assigned to distinguish its references.'),(1257,37,'In the employee-manager Self Join example, which column represents the employee table reference?','MEDIUM','B','The notes state that the table reference from which MGR is taken is considered the Employee table.'),(1258,37,'In the employee-manager Self Join example, which column represents the manager table reference?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that the table reference from which Emp-no is taken is considered the Manager table.'),(1259,37,'In a Self Join, how should each argument be defined according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','Each argument must be defined with its table name or alias.'),(1260,37,'Which is the ANSI syntax structure for a Self Join?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show two aliases for the same table and use ON for the joining condition.'),(1261,37,'Which condition is used as an example in the Self Join notes?','MEDIUM','A','The example joining condition is E1.MGR = E2.Emp-no.'),(1262,37,'Which clause is used for the joining condition in the Oracle Self Join syntax shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The Oracle syntax uses the WHERE clause to specify the joining condition.'),(1263,37,'What is the purpose of an Outer Join?','EASY','B','Outer Join is used to fetch unmatched records from tables.'),(1264,37,'How many types of Outer Join are covered in the notes?','EASY','C','The three types are Left Outer Join, Right Outer Join and Full Outer Join.'),(1265,37,'What does a Left Outer Join retrieve?','EASY','A','It retrieves unmatched records from the left table along with matched records.'),(1266,37,'Which keyword is used in ANSI syntax for Left Outer Join?','EASY','D','The ANSI syntax uses LEFT OUTER JOIN between the table names.'),(1267,37,'In the Oracle Left Outer Join syntax shown in the notes, where is the (+) symbol placed?','MEDIUM','B','The (+) symbol is placed on the right table column in the joining condition.'),(1268,37,'What does a Right Outer Join retrieve?','EASY','C','It retrieves unmatched records from the right table along with matched records from the left table.'),(1269,37,'In the Oracle Right Outer Join syntax shown in the notes, where is the (+) symbol placed?','MEDIUM','A','The (+) symbol is placed after the left table column in the joining condition.'),(1270,37,'What does a Full Outer Join retrieve?','EASY','D','It retrieves unmatched records from both left and right tables along with matched records.'),(1271,37,'Which syntax is shown for Full Outer Join in the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show the ANSI FULL OUTER JOIN syntax with an ON condition.'),(1272,37,'According to the notes, which join does not have an Oracle-only (+) syntax equivalent?','MEDIUM','C','The notes specify that Full Outer Join is written using ANSI FULL OUTER JOIN syntax.'),(1273,37,'What is the purpose of Set Operators in SQL?','EASY','B','Set Operators combine the results of two or more SELECT statements.'),(1274,37,'How many Set Operators are covered in the notes?','EASY','C','The four operators are UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT and MINUS.'),(1275,37,'Which Set Operator combines results and removes duplicate rows?','EASY','A','UNION combines SELECT results and eliminates duplicate values from the result set.'),(1276,37,'Which operator combines results without removing duplicates?','EASY','D','UNION ALL returns the combined result without removing duplicate rows.'),(1277,37,'Which operator returns only the common rows from two SELECT statements?','EASY','B','INTERSECT returns rows that are common to both SELECT statements.'),(1278,37,'Which operator returns rows present in the first query but absent in the second query?','MEDIUM','C','MINUS returns rows from the first query that are not present in the second query.'),(1279,37,'What happens to duplicate rows in the result of INTERSECT according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','INTERSECT does not return duplicate rows.'),(1280,37,'What is the default ordering of INTERSECT and MINUS results according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','Both operators arrange their result data in ascending order by default.'),(1281,37,'What must be the same in both SELECT statements when using a Set Operator?','MEDIUM','B','Both SELECT statements must select the same number of columns with matching or compatible datatypes.'),(1282,37,'Table First contains rows A, B, C. Table Second contains rows C, D, E. What does First MINUS Second return?','MEDIUM','A','MINUS returns rows present in the first query but absent in the second query.'),(1283,37,'What is the purpose of the ORDER BY clause?','EASY','B','ORDER BY displays the result table in ascending or descending order.'),(1284,37,'Which two sorting orders are supported by ORDER BY according to the notes?','EASY','C','ORDER BY displays results in ascending or descending order.'),(1285,37,'What can be passed as an argument to ORDER BY?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that a column name or an expression can be passed as an argument.'),(1286,37,'Where is the ORDER BY clause written in a SQL query?','EASY','D','ORDER BY is written at the end of the query.'),(1287,37,'Which clause executes after all other clauses according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','ORDER BY is the only clause described in the notes as executing at the end.'),(1288,37,'Which statement correctly uses ORDER BY with a column name?','MEDIUM','C','The syntax places ORDER BY followed by the column name after the FROM clause.'),(1289,37,'Which keyword is used to specify descending order?','EASY','A','DESC specifies descending order in an ORDER BY clause.'),(1290,37,'Which keyword is used to specify ascending order?','EASY','D','ASC specifies ascending order in an ORDER BY clause.'),(1291,37,'Which statement sorts employee records by salary in descending order?','MEDIUM','B','ORDER BY SAL DESC sorts the result by salary in descending order.'),(1292,37,'Which statement about ORDER BY is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','ORDER BY is the only clause written and executed at the end of a query.'),(1293,37,'What are attributes in SQL?','EASY','B','Attributes are properties that define an entity.'),(1294,37,'Which part of a table is also referred to as an attribute, property or field?','EASY','C','A column is also called an attribute, property or field.'),(1295,37,'What is a Key attribute or Candidate Key?','EASY','A','A key attribute identifies a record uniquely from a table.'),(1296,37,'Which of the following is an example of a Non-Key attribute according to the notes?','EASY','D','Name, age, gender and DOB are examples of Non-Key attributes.'),(1297,37,'What is a Prime Key attribute?','MEDIUM','B','It is the key attribute chosen as the main attribute to identify records uniquely.'),(1298,37,'What is a Non-Prime or Secondary Key attribute?','MEDIUM','C','It refers to all key attributes other than the selected Prime Key attribute.'),(1299,37,'What is a Composite Key attribute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes describe it as a combination of two or more non-key attributes used to identify a record uniquely.'),(1300,37,'What is a Super Key attribute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes define a Super Key attribute as a set of all key attributes.'),(1301,37,'What is the purpose of a Foreign Key attribute?','MEDIUM','B','A Foreign Key attribute represents a relationship between two entities.'),(1302,37,'Which of the following is an example of a Foreign Key attribute mentioned in the notes?','EASY','C','The notes give Dno as an example of a Foreign Key attribute.'),(1303,37,'What is a Functional Dependency?','EASY','B','A Functional Dependency exists when one attribute in a relation determines another attribute.'),(1304,37,'How many types of Functional Dependencies are covered in the notes?','EASY','C','The three types are Total, Partial and Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1305,37,'What is Total Functional Dependency (TFD)?','MEDIUM','A','TFD exists when one attribute determines all the other attributes in a relation.'),(1306,37,'In the relation Emp(Eid, ename, sal, dob), Eid determines ename, sal and dob. What type of dependency is this?','MEDIUM','D','Eid determines all the other attributes, so it is Total Functional Dependency.'),(1307,37,'What is Partial Functional Dependency?','MEDIUM','B','It occurs when a part of a composite key determines another attribute uniquely.'),(1308,37,'A composite key consists of (phone_no, mail_id). If phone_no alone determines cname and address, what dependency is shown in the notes?','HARD','C','The notes identify phone_no determining cname and address as Partial Functional Dependency.'),(1309,37,'What is Transitive Functional Dependency?','MEDIUM','A','A non-key attribute determined by a key attribute itself determines another attribute.'),(1310,37,'In the dependency Cid → Pincode → City, which attribute determines City?','MEDIUM','D','Pincode is the non-key attribute that determines City in the given transitive dependency.'),(1311,37,'What is Redundancy?','EASY','B','Redundancy means repetition of unwanted data.'),(1312,37,'According to the notes, which dependencies have redundancy and anomalies?','MEDIUM','C','Partial and Transitive Functional Dependencies have redundancy and anomalies; Total Functional Dependency does not.'),(1313,37,'What is Normalisation in SQL?','EASY','B','Normalisation reduces a large table into smaller tables to remove redundancy and anomalies by identifying functional dependencies.'),(1314,37,'What is a Normal Form according to the notes?','EASY','C','A table without redundancies and anomalies is said to be in Normal Form.'),(1315,37,'Which of the following lists the normalisation levels covered in the notes?','EASY','A','The notes list 1NF, 2NF, 3NF and BCNF.'),(1316,37,'Which condition is required for First Normal Form (1NF)?','MEDIUM','D','1NF requires a single atomic value in each column, no multivalued data, no duplicate records and a unique row identifier.'),(1317,37,'Which condition must a table satisfy to be in 2NF according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','A table must satisfy 1NF and must not have Partial Functional Dependency.'),(1318,37,'Which dependency must be removed to achieve 3NF according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','3NF requires the table to be in 2NF and not have Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1319,37,'What must a table satisfy before it can reach 3NF?','MEDIUM','A','According to the notes, a table must first satisfy all 2NF conditions.'),(1320,37,'According to the notes, when is a table said to be normalized?','MEDIUM','D','The notes state that if a table is reduced to 3NF, it is said to be normalized.'),(1321,37,'A table contains a multivalued Course column. Which normal form condition is violated?','MEDIUM','B','1NF requires a single atomic value in each column and does not allow multivalued data.'),(1322,37,'What is the correct order of progression from 1NF to 3NF?','EASY','C','The notes show the progression as First Normal Form, Second Normal Form and Third Normal Form.'),(1323,37,'What is the primary purpose of DDL?','EASY','B','DDL is used to create or modify the structure of a table.'),(1324,37,'Which command is used to view the structure of a table?','EASY','C','DESCRIBE or DESC displays the structure of a table.'),(1325,37,'Which is a valid alternative command for DESCRIBE?','EASY','A','DESC is the alternative form of the DESCRIBE command.'),(1326,37,'Which command is used to change the name of an existing table?','EASY','D','RENAME changes the name of an existing table.'),(1327,37,'What happens to table data when a table is renamed?','MEDIUM','B','RENAME changes only the table name; the existing data remains the same.'),(1328,37,'Which command is used to modify the structure of an existing table?','EASY','C','ALTER is used to modify table structure.'),(1329,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to add a new column to a table?','MEDIUM','A','The ADD clause is used with ALTER TABLE to add a column.'),(1330,37,'Which statement correctly adds an AGE column to table A_2?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show ALTER TABLE table_name ADD column_name datatype syntax.'),(1331,37,'Which statement about the RENAME command is correct?','MEDIUM','B','RENAME changes only the table name, and the old table name cannot be used after renaming.'),(1332,37,'Which command can be used to view the structure after renaming a table?','EASY','C','The notes state that DESC can be used to view the new table structure after renaming.'),(1333,37,'Which ALTER operation removes a single column from a table?','EASY','B','DROP COLUMN is used to remove a column from a table.'),(1334,37,'Which syntax is shown in the notes for dropping multiple columns?','MEDIUM','C','The notes show ALTER TABLE table_name DROP (column1, column2, ... columnN).'),(1335,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to change an existing column name?','EASY','A','RENAME COLUMN changes an existing column name to a new column name.'),(1336,37,'Which command changes the datatype of an existing column according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The MODIFY operation is used to change a column datatype.'),(1337,37,'Which keyword is used with MODIFY to make a column accept only non-NULL values?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show MODIFY column_name datatype NOT NULL for adding a NOT NULL requirement.'),(1338,37,'Which ALTER statement adds a primary key constraint to an existing table?','MEDIUM','C','The notes show ADD CONSTRAINT followed by the constraint name and PRIMARY KEY column definition.'),(1339,37,'Which clause is used to add a foreign key constraint using ALTER TABLE?','MEDIUM','A','The notes show ADD CONSTRAINT followed by FOREIGN KEY and REFERENCES.'),(1340,37,'Which ALTER operation adds more than one column to a table?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show multiple ADD clauses in one ALTER TABLE statement to add multiple columns.'),(1341,37,'Which statement renames the BOYFRIEND column to EX in table A_2?','MEDIUM','B','The notes show ALTER TABLE table_name RENAME COLUMN old_column_name TO new_column_name.'),(1342,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to change a column datatype or its NULL/NOT NULL property?','MEDIUM','C','The MODIFY operation is used in the notes for changing a column datatype and its NULL/NOT NULL property.'),(1343,37,'Which command is used to create a new table?','EASY','A','CREATE TABLE is used to create a new table.'),(1344,37,'Can two tables in the same database have the same table name?','EASY','B','The notes state that duplicated table names are not possible in a single database.'),(1345,37,'Can a single table contain duplicated column names?','EASY','C','The notes state that duplicated column names are not possible in a single table.'),(1346,37,'How should multiple words be written in a table or column name according to the notes?','EASY','D','Multiple words should be separated using an underscore.'),(1347,37,'What is the minimum number of columns required when creating a table?','EASY','A','According to the notes, every table must contain at least one column.'),(1348,37,'How many datatypes can be assigned to a single column according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','Each column must contain only one datatype, but it can contain multiple constraints.'),(1349,37,'Can a column have multiple constraints according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The notes state that each column has one datatype but can have multiple constraints.'),(1350,37,'Which is the correct general syntax for creating a table?','MEDIUM','A','The notes show CREATE TABLE followed by the table name and column definitions inside parentheses.'),(1351,37,'Where are column definitions written in a CREATE TABLE statement?','EASY','D','Column definitions are written inside parentheses after the table name.'),(1352,37,'Which statement about a column definition is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','A column definition contains a column name, one datatype and may contain multiple constraints.'),(1353,37,'What is the primary purpose of DML?','EASY','B','DML is used to manipulate the data stored in a table.'),(1354,37,'Which command is used to insert new records into a table?','EASY','C','INSERT is used to add data into a table.'),(1355,37,'Which INSERT syntax explicitly specifies the column names?','MEDIUM','A','The column names are written after the table name and before the VALUES keyword.'),(1356,37,'What is the purpose of the UPDATE statement?','EASY','D','UPDATE is used to change an existing value to a new value.'),(1357,37,'Which clause is used to specify the records to be updated?','MEDIUM','B','The WHERE clause provides the filter condition for updating specific records.'),(1358,37,'What happens when UPDATE is executed without a WHERE condition?','MEDIUM','C','The notes show that an UPDATE without WHERE changes the entire column for all rows.'),(1359,37,'Which command is used to remove records from a table?','EASY','A','DELETE is used to remove one record, a few records or all records from a table.'),(1360,37,'What is the purpose of the WHERE condition in a DELETE statement?','MEDIUM','D','WHERE is used to specify which records should be deleted.'),(1361,37,'Is the WHERE condition mandatory in UPDATE and DELETE statements?','MEDIUM','B','The notes state that WHERE is not mandatory. Without it, the operation can affect all records.'),(1362,37,'According to the notes, when are DML changes saved permanently?','MEDIUM','C','DML is not auto-commit. Its changes remain temporary until an explicit COMMIT is issued.'),(1363,37,'What is the primary purpose of TCL?','EASY','B','TCL is used to control transactions performed on the database.'),(1364,37,'Which of the following is a TCL statement?','EASY','C','COMMIT, ROLLBACK and SAVEPOINT are the three TCL statements covered in the notes.'),(1365,37,'What is the purpose of the COMMIT statement?','EASY','A','COMMIT saves the transaction into the database permanently.'),(1366,37,'Which is the correct syntax for the COMMIT statement?','EASY','D','The syntax shown in the notes is COMMIT;'),(1367,37,'What is the purpose of the ROLLBACK statement?','EASY','B','ROLLBACK undoes transactions up to the point where the last COMMIT was performed, according to the notes.'),(1368,37,'Which is the correct syntax for the ROLLBACK statement?','EASY','C','The syntax shown in the notes is ROLLBACK;'),(1369,37,'What is the purpose of the SAVEPOINT statement?','MEDIUM','A','SAVEPOINT creates a checkpoint or restoration point in a transaction.'),(1370,37,'Which syntax is used to create a SAVEPOINT?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show SAVEPOINT followed by a savepoint name and a semicolon.'),(1371,37,'Which TCL statement is used to save a transaction permanently?','EASY','B','COMMIT permanently saves the transaction into the database.'),(1372,37,'Which of the following contains all three TCL statements?','EASY','C','The three TCL statements covered in the notes are COMMIT, ROLLBACK and SAVEPOINT.'),(1373,37,'What is the primary purpose of DCL?','EASY','B','DCL is used to control data access between users.'),(1374,37,'How many statements are covered under DCL in the notes?','EASY','C','The two DCL statements covered are GRANT and REVOKE.'),(1375,37,'Which statement is used to give permissions to a user?','EASY','A','GRANT is used to give permissions to a user.'),(1376,37,'Which statement is used to take back permissions from a user?','EASY','D','REVOKE is used to take back permissions from a user.'),(1377,37,'Which keyword identifies the user receiving permissions in a GRANT statement?','MEDIUM','B','The GRANT syntax uses TO followed by the username.'),(1378,37,'Which keyword identifies the user whose permissions are being revoked?','MEDIUM','C','The REVOKE syntax uses FROM followed by the username.'),(1379,37,'Which statement correctly grants SELECT permission on EMP to HR?','MEDIUM','A','The notes provide GRANT SELECT ON EMP TO HR as the example.'),(1380,37,'Which statement correctly takes back SELECT permission on EMP from HR?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show REVOKE SELECT ON EMP FROM HR as the example.'),(1381,37,'In the statement GRANT SELECT ON EMP TO HR, what does EMP represent?','MEDIUM','B','The GRANT syntax places the table name after the ON keyword.'),(1382,37,'Which pair of statements belongs to Data Control Language?','EASY','C','GRANT and REVOKE are the two DCL statements in the notes.'),(1383,37,'What is the main difference between DDL and DML?','EASY','A','DDL modifies table structure, while DML manipulates table data.'),(1384,37,'Which of the following contains only DDL commands?','EASY','C','CREATE, ALTER, RENAME, TRUNCATE and DROP belong to DDL.'),(1385,37,'Which statement is auto-commit according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','DDL statements automatically save changes, whereas DML changes remain temporary until COMMIT.'),(1386,37,'Which statement correctly describes DELETE?','EASY','D','DELETE is a DML statement used to remove selected or all records from a table.'),(1387,37,'Which statement can remove selected records using a WHERE condition?','EASY','A','DELETE can remove one, a few or all records using a WHERE condition.'),(1388,37,'What happens when TRUNCATE is executed on a table?','EASY','C','TRUNCATE removes all records but keeps the table structure.'),(1389,37,'Which statement removes both the table structure and its data?','EASY','B','DROP removes the entire table, including its structure and data.'),(1390,37,'Which statement is used to modify the structure of an existing table?','EASY','D','ALTER modifies table structure, including columns and constraints.'),(1391,37,'Which statement modifies existing data values inside table rows?','EASY','A','UPDATE modifies existing data values using SET and an optional WHERE condition.'),(1392,37,'Which statement about TRUNCATE is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','TRUNCATE cannot filter records using a WHERE condition; it removes all records.'),(1393,37,'Which command removes all records while keeping the table structure?','EASY','B','TRUNCATE removes all records but keeps the table structure.'),(1394,37,'What happens when a table is dropped according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','The dropped table moves to the Recycle Bin and is stored with a BIN name.'),(1395,37,'Which command is used to restore a dropped table from the Recycle Bin?','MEDIUM','A','FLASHBACK TABLE ... TO BEFORE DROP restores the table according to the notes.'),(1396,37,'Which syntax restores a table to its state before it was dropped?','MEDIUM','D','The notes give the syntax FLASHBACK TABLE table_name TO BEFORE DROP;'),(1397,37,'Which command permanently removes a table from the Recycle Bin?','EASY','B','PURGE permanently removes the table from the Recycle Bin.'),(1398,37,'Which command removes a table permanently using the DROP statement?','MEDIUM','C','The notes provide DROP TABLE table_name PURGE; as a way to remove it permanently.'),(1399,37,'Which command changes only the name of an existing table?','EASY','A','RENAME changes the table name without changing its data.'),(1400,37,'Which statement is used to view the structure of a table?','EASY','D','DESCRIBE or DESC displays the table structure.'),(1401,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to add a new column to a table?','MEDIUM','B','ALTER TABLE table_name ADD column_name datatype; adds a column.'),(1402,37,'Which ALTER operation renames an existing column?','MEDIUM','C','ALTER TABLE table_name RENAME COLUMN old_column_name TO new_column_name; renames a column.'),(1403,37,'Which command is used to modify the structure of an existing table?','EASY','B','ALTER is used to modify the structure of a table.'),(1404,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to add a new column?','EASY','A','ADD is used with ALTER TABLE to add a new column.'),(1405,37,'Which syntax correctly adds a column named AGE with datatype NUMBER(2)?','MEDIUM','C','The notes show ALTER TABLE table_name ADD column_name datatype.'),(1406,37,'Which keyword is used to remove a column using ALTER TABLE?','EASY','D','DROP COLUMN removes a column from an existing table.'),(1407,37,'Which statement correctly removes the column AWARDS from A_2?','MEDIUM','B','The syntax is ALTER TABLE table_name DROP COLUMN column_name.'),(1408,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to rename an existing column?','EASY','A','RENAME COLUMN changes the existing column name to a new column name.'),(1409,37,'Which syntax is used to modify a column datatype?','MEDIUM','C','The notes show ALTER TABLE table_name MODIFY column_name new_datatype.'),(1410,37,'Which keyword is used to make a column NOT NULL using ALTER?','MEDIUM','D','The notes show MODIFY column_name datatype NOT NULL.'),(1411,37,'Which ALTER operation is used to add a Primary Key constraint?','MEDIUM','B','ADD CONSTRAINT followed by PRIMARY KEY is used to add a primary key.'),(1412,37,'Which command is used to view the structure of a table after renaming it?','EASY','A','The notes state that DESC can be used to view the new table structure.'),(1413,37,'What is the main purpose of the INSERT statement?','EASY','B','INSERT is used to insert data into a table.'),(1414,37,'Which INSERT syntax specifies column names explicitly?','MEDIUM','C','The column names are written after the table name and before VALUES.'),(1415,37,'Which symbol is used for substitution variables in the INSERT syntax shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','A','The notes use ampersand (&) before substitution variable names.'),(1416,37,'What is the purpose of substitution variables in the INSERT syntax from the notes?','MEDIUM','D','Substitution variables prompt the user to enter values.'),(1417,37,'What is the purpose of the UPDATE statement?','EASY','B','UPDATE changes an existing value to a new value.'),(1418,37,'Which clause is used to specify the records to be updated?','EASY','C','WHERE provides the filter condition for updating specific records.'),(1419,37,'What happens when UPDATE is executed without a WHERE condition, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','Without WHERE, the specified column is updated for all rows.'),(1420,37,'How can multiple columns be updated in a single UPDATE statement?','MEDIUM','D','Multiple column assignments are separated by commas in the SET clause.'),(1421,37,'What is the purpose of the DELETE statement?','EASY','C','DELETE removes one record, a few records or all records from a table.'),(1422,37,'According to the notes, is the WHERE condition mandatory in DELETE and UPDATE statements?','MEDIUM','B','WHERE is optional, but it is needed when specific records must be updated or deleted.'),(1423,37,'What is the main purpose of SQL set operators?','EASY','A','Set operators combine the results of two or more SELECT statements.'),(1424,37,'Which set operator combines results and removes duplicate rows?','EASY','C','UNION combines the results and eliminates duplicate rows.'),(1425,37,'Which operator combines results without removing duplicates?','EASY','B','UNION ALL retains duplicate rows in the combined result.'),(1426,37,'Which set operator returns only the common rows from both SELECT statements?','EASY','D','INTERSECT returns rows that are common to both query results.'),(1427,37,'Which operator returns rows present in the first query but absent in the second query?','MEDIUM','A','MINUS returns rows from the first query that are not present in the second query.'),(1428,37,'Which set operator preserves duplicate rows?','EASY','C','UNION ALL does not remove duplicate rows.'),(1429,37,'What must be the same in both SELECT statements when using a set operator?','MEDIUM','B','Both SELECT statements must have the same number of columns and corresponding compatible datatypes.'),(1430,37,'Which set operators return results without duplicates according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','UNION, INTERSECT and MINUS eliminate duplicates; UNION ALL preserves them.'),(1431,37,'Which operator returns common rows and arranges the result in ascending order by default?','MEDIUM','A','INTERSECT returns common rows without duplicates and arranges them in ascending order by default.'),(1432,37,'Consider First = {1, 2, 3} and Second = {3, 4, 5}. What is the result of First MINUS Second?','MEDIUM','C','MINUS returns values present in the first result but absent in the second: 1 and 2.'),(1433,37,'What is the purpose of the ORDER BY clause?','EASY','A','ORDER BY displays the result table in ascending or descending order.'),(1434,37,'Which clause is used to display records in ascending or descending order?','EASY','C','ORDER BY is used to sort the result table.'),(1435,37,'What can be passed as an argument to the ORDER BY clause?','MEDIUM','B','The notes state that a column name or an expression can be passed to ORDER BY.'),(1436,37,'Where is ORDER BY written in a SELECT query?','EASY','D','ORDER BY is written at the end of a query.'),(1437,37,'Which clause executes after all other clauses according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','ORDER BY is the only clause that executes at the end after all other clauses.'),(1438,37,'Which clause is used to sort the result table in descending order?','EASY','C','ORDER BY is the clause used for sorting in ascending or descending order.'),(1439,37,'Which clause is written at the end of a query?','EASY','B','ORDER BY is the only clause written at the end of a query according to the notes.'),(1440,37,'What is the main difference between ORDER BY and WHERE?','MEDIUM','D','ORDER BY sorts the result, while WHERE filters records.'),(1441,37,'Which clause can accept an expression as its argument?','MEDIUM','A','ORDER BY accepts either a column name or an expression.'),(1442,37,'Which statement about ORDER BY is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','ORDER BY is written and executed after the other clauses.'),(1443,37,'What are attributes in SQL?','EASY','B','Attributes are properties that define an entity.'),(1444,37,'Which term is also used to refer to a column in a table?','EASY','C','A column is also called an attribute, property or field.'),(1445,37,'What is a Key Attribute or Candidate Key?','EASY','A','A key attribute identifies a record uniquely from a table.'),(1446,37,'What is a Non-Key Attribute?','EASY','D','Non-key attributes are all attributes other than key attributes.'),(1447,37,'What is a Prime Key Attribute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','B','Among the key attributes, the one chosen as the main identifier is the prime key attribute.'),(1448,37,'What is a Non-Prime or Secondary Key Attribute?','MEDIUM','C','These are the key attributes other than the prime key attribute.'),(1449,37,'What is a Composite Key Attribute?','MEDIUM','A','The notes describe it as a combination of two or more non-key attributes used to identify a record uniquely.'),(1450,37,'What is a Super Key Attribute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes define a super key attribute as a set of all the key attributes.'),(1451,37,'What is a Foreign Key Attribute?','MEDIUM','B','A foreign key attribute behaves as an attribute of another entity and represents a relationship between entities.'),(1452,37,'Which of the following is an example of a Non-Key Attribute in the notes?','EASY','C','Name, age, gender and date of birth are examples of non-key attributes in the notes.'),(1453,37,'What is a Functional Dependency?','EASY','B','It exists when an attribute in a relation determines another attribute.'),(1454,37,'Which symbol is used to represent a Functional Dependency in the notes?','EASY','C','The notes use an arrow to represent a functional dependency, for example Eid --> ename.'),(1455,37,'How many types of Functional Dependencies are covered in the notes?','EASY','A','The three types are Total, Partial and Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1456,37,'What is Total Functional Dependency (TFD)?','MEDIUM','D','An attribute determining all the other attributes is called Total Functional Dependency.'),(1457,37,'Which example represents Total Functional Dependency in the notes?','MEDIUM','B','Eid determines ename, sal and dob in the Employee example.'),(1458,37,'What is Partial Functional Dependency?','MEDIUM','C','A part of a composite key determines another attribute uniquely.'),(1459,37,'In the Customer example, which two attributes together form the composite key?','MEDIUM','A','The notes identify phone_no and mail_id together as the composite key.'),(1460,37,'What is Transitive Functional Dependency?','MEDIUM','D','A non-key attribute determined by a key attribute itself determines another attribute.'),(1461,37,'In the example Cid --> Pincode --> City, which attribute determines City?','MEDIUM','B','Pincode is determined by Cid and in turn determines City.'),(1462,37,'Which statement about Total Functional Dependency is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','Total Functional Dependency has no redundancy and no anomalies according to the notes.'),(1463,37,'In the Employee example, Eid determines ename, sal and dob. Which dependency does this represent?','MEDIUM','A','Eid determines all the other attributes, so it represents Total Functional Dependency.'),(1464,37,'A composite key consists of phone_no and mail_id. If phone_no alone determines cname and address, what is this called?','MEDIUM','C','Part of a composite key determining another attribute is Partial Functional Dependency.'),(1465,37,'In Cid --> Pincode --> City, which attribute is the non-key attribute that determines another attribute?','MEDIUM','B','Pincode is determined by Cid and in turn determines City.'),(1466,37,'Which types of Functional Dependency introduce redundancy and anomalies according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','Partial and Transitive Functional Dependencies introduce redundancy and anomalies.'),(1467,37,'What is redundancy?','EASY','A','Redundancy is the repetition of unwanted data.'),(1468,37,'What is an anomaly according to the notes?','EASY','C','An anomaly is a side effect caused during DML operations.'),(1469,37,'Which dependency is associated with no redundancy and no anomalies in the notes?','MEDIUM','B','The notes state that Total Functional Dependency has no redundancy and no anomalies.'),(1470,37,'A table has a composite key, but one part of that key determines another attribute. What issue is present?','MEDIUM','D','This describes Partial Functional Dependency, which introduces redundancy and anomalies according to the notes.'),(1471,37,'Which dependency is represented by Cid --> Pincode --> City?','MEDIUM','A','Pincode is determined by Cid and then determines City, which is Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1472,37,'Which two types of Functional Dependency are specifically associated with redundancy in the notes?','MEDIUM','C','Partial and Transitive Functional Dependencies both introduce redundancy.'),(1473,37,'What is the main purpose of Normalisation?','EASY','B','Normalisation reduces large tables into smaller tables to remove redundancies and anomalies.'),(1474,37,'Which condition is required for a table to satisfy 1NF?','MEDIUM','C','1NF requires atomic values, no multivalued data, no duplicate records and a unique row identifier.'),(1475,37,'A table contains multiple course values such as JAVA, SQL in a single cell. Which normal form requirement is violated?','MEDIUM','A','1NF does not allow multivalued data in a single cell.'),(1476,37,'A table satisfies 1NF but contains Partial Functional Dependency. Which normal form requirement is not satisfied?','MEDIUM','B','2NF requires the table to satisfy 1NF and have no Partial Functional Dependency.'),(1477,37,'Which dependency must be removed to achieve 3NF according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','3NF requires 2NF and removal of Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1478,37,'An Employee table has Eid --> ename, sal and Deptno --> dname, loc. Which decomposition is shown in the notes for removing partial dependency?','HARD','A','The notes decompose the table into Employee details and Department details.'),(1479,37,'In the dependency Eid --> Pincode --> State, what type of dependency is represented?','MEDIUM','C','A non-key attribute determined by the key determines another attribute, representing Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1480,37,'Which normal form comes immediately after 1NF in the normalisation flow?','EASY','B','The sequence is 1NF, 2NF and 3NF.'),(1481,37,'What does BCNF stand for?','EASY','D','BCNF stands for Boyce-Codd Normal Form.'),(1482,37,'According to the notes, when is a table said to be normalized?','MEDIUM','A','The notes state that when a table is reduced to 3NF, it is said to be normalized.'),(1483,37,'What is Projection in SQL?','EASY','B','Projection retrieves data by selecting only the required columns.'),(1484,37,'What is Selection in SQL?','EASY','C','Selection retrieves rows by filtering them.'),(1485,37,'Which operation is used when you want to retrieve only the employee name and salary columns?','MEDIUM','A','Projection is used to retrieve selected columns.'),(1486,37,'Which operation is used when you want to retrieve only employees whose salary is greater than 30000?','MEDIUM','D','Selection filters rows based on a condition.'),(1487,37,'What is the correct order of the three tiers in a web application?','MEDIUM','B','The three tiers are Presentation Layer, Middleware/Business Logic Layer and Data Layer.'),(1488,37,'Which tier contains the webpage or user interface where users interact?','EASY','A','The Presentation Layer is the front end where users interact with the application.'),(1489,37,'Which tier processes requests and connects the front end to the database?','MEDIUM','C','The Middleware or Business Logic Layer processes requests and connects to the database using SQL.'),(1490,37,'Where is actual application data such as usernames and passwords stored in a 3-tier architecture?','EASY','D','The Data Layer is the back end/database where actual data is stored.'),(1491,37,'Which type of application can be accessed through a web browser?','EASY','B','A web application can be opened or accessed through a platform like a web browser.'),(1492,37,'Which statement describes a standalone application according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','A standalone application can be accessed on a single device and does not require additional features to work.'),(1493,37,'Which datatype uses fixed-length memory allocation according to the notes?','EASY','A','CHAR follows fixed-length memory allocation.'),(1494,37,'Which datatype uses variable-length memory allocation according to the notes?','EASY','C','VARCHAR follows variable-length memory allocation.'),(1495,37,'According to the notes, what is the maximum character capacity of CHAR?','MEDIUM','B','The notes state that CHAR stores up to 2000 characters.'),(1496,37,'According to the notes, what is the maximum character capacity of VARCHAR/VARCHAR2?','MEDIUM','D','The notes state that VARCHAR/VARCHAR2 stores up to 4000 characters.'),(1497,37,'What is the difference between UNIQUE and DISTINCT?','MEDIUM','A','UNIQUE is a constraint at table-definition level; DISTINCT removes duplicates from query results.'),(1498,37,'Which statement about a Primary Key and Foreign Key is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','C','A table can have only one Primary Key, while multiple Foreign Keys are allowed.'),(1499,37,'Which statement correctly describes RENAME and ALTER?','MEDIUM','B','RENAME changes the table name, while ALTER modifies the table structure.'),(1500,37,'What happens when DROP is used according to the notes?','MEDIUM','D','DROP removes the table and moves it to the Recycle Bin, where it may be restored using FLASHBACK.'),(1501,37,'What is the purpose of PURGE according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A','PURGE permanently removes the table from the Recycle Bin so it cannot be restored.'),(1502,37,'Which statement correctly compares ROUND() and TRUNC()?','MEDIUM','C','ROUND() rounds based on the decimal value; TRUNC() cuts off the decimal without rounding up.'),(1503,37,'Which datatype follows fixed-length memory allocation?','EASY','CHAR','CHAR follows fixed-length memory allocation.'),(1504,37,'According to the notes, what is the maximum storage size of VARCHAR/VARCHAR2?','EASY','4000 characters','The notes state that VARCHAR/VARCHAR2 can store up to 4000 characters.'),(1505,37,'What is the main difference between UNIQUE and DISTINCT?','MEDIUM','UNIQUE is a constraint; DISTINCT is a SELECT keyword','UNIQUE prevents duplicate values at the table-definition level; DISTINCT removes duplicates from query results.'),(1506,37,'Which statement correctly describes a subquery?','EASY','A query written inside another query','A query written inside another query is called a subquery.'),(1507,37,'Which is a key difference between a subquery and a join according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A subquery connects tables, while a join merges tables','The notes describe a subquery as connecting tables and a join as merging tables.'),(1508,37,'How does a normal subquery differ from a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','A normal subquery executes independently; a correlated subquery has interdependent inner and outer queries','The notes explain that a normal inner query executes independently, whereas correlated queries depend on each other.'),(1509,37,'Which join explicitly requires a joining condition using ON/WHERE according to the notes?','MEDIUM','INNER JOIN','The notes state that INNER JOIN uses an explicitly specified joining condition.'),(1510,37,'What happens when a NATURAL JOIN finds no relation between the tables, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','It behaves like a CROSS JOIN','The notes state that NATURAL JOIN behaves like a CROSS JOIN when there is no relation.'),(1511,37,'Which subquery produces more than one output?','EASY','Multi-row subquery','A multi-row subquery produces more than one output.'),(1512,37,'Which operator returns TRUE when a condition is satisfied against at least one value returned by a subquery?','MEDIUM','ANY','ANY returns TRUE if the condition is satisfied against any one of the returned values.'),(1513,37,'Which of the following is NOT considered a NULL value according to the notes?','EASY','Zero (0)','The notes state that zero or space should not be considered NULL.'),(1514,37,'What is the result of an arithmetic operation involving a NULL value, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','NULL','Arithmetic operations involving NULL produce NULL as the result.'),(1515,37,'Which operator cannot be used to equate NULL values according to the notes?','EASY','=','The notes state that NULL values cannot be equated using the = operator.'),(1516,37,'Which clause is used to filter rows before grouping?','MEDIUM','WHERE','WHERE filters records row by row after FROM.'),(1517,37,'Which clause filters groups after GROUP BY?','EASY','HAVING','HAVING filters groups group by group after GROUP BY.'),(1518,37,'Which clause is written and executed at the end of a query according to the notes?','EASY','ORDER BY','ORDER BY is written and executed after all other clauses.'),(1519,37,'A query must combine results from two SELECT statements without removing duplicate rows. Which operator should be used?','MEDIUM','UNION ALL','UNION ALL combines results without removing duplicates.'),(1520,37,'Which set operator returns rows present in the first SELECT result but absent in the second?','MEDIUM','MINUS','MINUS returns rows present in the first query but absent in the second.'),(1521,37,'What must match between SELECT statements when using set operators?','MEDIUM','Number of columns and their datatypes','The notes state that the number of columns and their datatypes must match.'),(1522,37,'Which set operator returns only common rows from both SELECT statements?','EASY','INTERSECT','INTERSECT returns only common rows from both statements.'),(1523,37,'What is the minimum number of conditions required to use the AND operator?','EASY','2','The notes state that AND requires a minimum of two conditions.'),(1524,37,'When does the AND operator return TRUE?','EASY','When all conditions are satisfied','AND returns TRUE only when all the conditions are satisfied.'),(1525,37,'What is the minimum number of conditions required to use the OR operator?','EASY','2','The notes state that OR requires a minimum of two conditions.'),(1526,37,'When does the OR operator return TRUE?','EASY','When at least one condition is satisfied','OR returns TRUE if any one condition is satisfied.'),(1527,37,'When AND and OR are used together, which operator gets the first execution priority according to the notes?','MEDIUM','AND','The notes state that AND receives the first priority over OR.'),(1528,37,'According to the notes, how can we avoid unexpected output when AND and OR are used together?','MEDIUM','Use brackets for OR conditions','The notes recommend passing brackets around OR operation conditions.'),(1529,37,'Which operator is described in the notes as performing binary multiplication?','MEDIUM','AND','The notes describe AND as performing binary multiplication.'),(1530,37,'Which operator is described in the notes as performing binary addition?','MEDIUM','OR','The notes describe OR as performing binary addition.'),(1531,37,'What type of value does a WHERE filter condition return?','EASY','Boolean (TRUE/FALSE)','The notes state that a WHERE filter condition returns a Boolean value.'),(1532,37,'Which is a valid filter-condition structure according to the notes?','MEDIUM','column_name operator value','The notes give column_name/expression operator value as a valid filter-condition structure.'),(1533,37,'Which logical operator returns TRUE only when all conditions are satisfied?','EASY','AND','AND returns TRUE when all conditions are satisfied.'),(1534,37,'Which logical operator returns TRUE when at least one condition is satisfied?','EASY','OR','OR returns TRUE if any one condition is satisfied.'),(1535,37,'According to the notes, what is the minimum number of conditions required to use AND?','EASY','Two','AND requires a minimum of two conditions.'),(1536,37,'What is the minimum number of conditions required to use OR according to the notes?','EASY','Two','OR requires a minimum of two conditions.'),(1537,37,'When AND and OR are used together without brackets, which operator gets the first execution priority according to the notes?','MEDIUM','AND','The notes state that AND receives the first execution priority.'),(1538,37,'According to the notes, what should be used to avoid incorrect output when combining AND and OR conditions?','MEDIUM','Brackets () for OR conditions','The notes recommend passing brackets around OR operation conditions.'),(1539,37,'What type of value does a WHERE filter condition return?','EASY','Boolean value (TRUE/FALSE)','The WHERE filter condition returns a Boolean value.'),(1540,37,'After which clause does WHERE execute according to the notes?','MEDIUM','FROM','WHERE executes after the FROM clause.'),(1541,37,'Which of the following is a valid filter-condition format according to the notes?','MEDIUM','column_name operator value','The notes provide column_name/expression operator value as a valid filter-condition format.'),(1542,37,'Which statement about the WHERE clause is correct?','MEDIUM','It filters records row by row','The WHERE clause filters records and executes row by row.'),(1543,37,'What is an alias in SQL?','EASY','An alternative name assigned to a column or expression','An alias is an alternative name assigned to a column name or expression in the result table.'),(1544,37,'Can an SQL column alias be assigned without using the AS keyword?','EASY','Yes','The notes state that an alias can be assigned with or without AS.'),(1545,37,'According to the notes, how should an alias containing multiple words be represented?','MEDIUM','Separated by underscore or enclosed in double quotes','The notes state that an alias must be a single string, separated by underscore or enclosed within double quotes.'),(1546,37,'What is the purpose of assigning an alias?','EASY','To improve the understandability of a column name or expression','Aliases make column names or expressions easier to understand in the result table.'),(1547,37,'Which clause is executed first in the SELECT query order described in the notes?','EASY','FROM','The execution order in the notes is FROM, WHERE, then SELECT.'),(1548,37,'What is the second execution step in the SELECT query order described in the notes?','EASY','WHERE','The execution order is FROM, WHERE, SELECT.'),(1549,37,'What is the purpose of the FROM clause?','EASY','To accept a table name as an argument','The FROM clause accepts a table name as its argument.'),(1550,37,'According to the notes, what happens if the table name is missing or incorrect in the FROM clause?','MEDIUM','An error occurs','The notes state that if the table name is missing or contains an error, an error occurs.'),(1551,37,'When does the SELECT clause produce the final output according to the notes?','MEDIUM','After FROM clause execution is completed','The SELECT clause produces the final output after FROM execution is completed.'),(1552,37,'According to the SELECT syntax in the notes, where is the table name specified?','EASY','FROM clause','The SELECT syntax places the table name after FROM.'),(1553,37,'Which aggregate function returns the highest value in a column?','EASY','MAX()','MAX() fetches the maximum value present in a column.'),(1554,37,'Which aggregate function returns the total of values in a column?','EASY','SUM()','SUM() fetches the total or summation of values present in a column.'),(1555,37,'Which aggregate function returns the number of values present in a column?','EASY','COUNT()','COUNT() fetches the number of values present in a column.'),(1556,37,'Which aggregate function can take an asterisk (*) as its argument according to the notes?','MEDIUM','COUNT()','COUNT() is the only multi-row function listed in the notes that can take * as an argument.'),(1557,37,'How many arguments should a multi-row function take according to the notes?','MEDIUM','One argument','The notes state that a multi-row function takes only one argument: a column name or expression.'),(1558,37,'Which clause should not contain a multi-row aggregate function according to the notes?','MEDIUM','WHERE','The notes state that a multi-row function should not be used in the WHERE clause.'),(1559,37,'What happens to NULL values when aggregate functions are executed according to the notes?','MEDIUM','They are ignored','The notes state that multi-row aggregate functions ignore NULL values.'),(1560,37,'What is the output pattern of a multi-row aggregate function when n inputs are passed?','MEDIUM','n inputs produce one output','Multi-row functions execute group by group and produce one output from n inputs.'),(1561,37,'Which of the following is NOT listed as an aggregate function in the notes?','EASY','UPPER()','The notes list MAX, MIN, SUM, AVG and COUNT as aggregate functions. UPPER is a single-row function.'),(1562,37,'Which statement about aggregate functions is correct according to the notes?','MEDIUM','They are also called group functions','Multi-row functions are also known as aggregate functions or group functions.'),(1563,37,'LIKE belongs to which category of SQL operators in the notes?','EASY','Special Operator','The notes list LIKE under Special Operators.'),(1564,37,'NOT LIKE belongs to which category of SQL operators?','EASY','Special Operator','The notes include NOT LIKE in the list of Special Operators.'),(1565,37,'Which of the following is listed alongside LIKE in the special operator category?','EASY','NOT LIKE','The notes list LIKE and NOT LIKE together.'),(1566,37,'Which of these is NOT listed as a special operator in the notes?','MEDIUM','UNION','UNION is listed under Set Operators, not Special Operators.'),(1567,37,'Which operator category contains IN, NOT IN, BETWEEN and LIKE?','MEDIUM','Special Operator','All four are listed under Special Operators in the notes.'),(1568,37,'Which of the following is a special operator used in SQL?','EASY','LIKE','LIKE is included in the special operator list.'),(1569,37,'Which pair contains two special operators according to the notes?','MEDIUM','LIKE and NOT LIKE','Both LIKE and NOT LIKE appear in the special operator list.'),(1570,37,'Which of these operator pairs is listed under special operators?','MEDIUM','IS and IS NOT','The notes list IS and IS NOT as special operators.'),(1571,37,'Which operator is listed under the sub-query operator category rather than the special operator category?','MEDIUM','ANY','ANY is listed under Sub-query Operators in the notes.'),(1572,37,'How many special operators are listed in the notes?','MEDIUM','Eight','The list contains IN, NOT IN, BETWEEN, NOT BETWEEN, IS, IS NOT, LIKE and NOT LIKE.'),(1573,37,'Which statement about UNIQUE and DISTINCT is correct?','MEDIUM','UNIQUE is a constraint; DISTINCT is used in a SELECT query','UNIQUE prevents duplicate values at table-definition level. DISTINCT removes duplicates from query results.'),(1574,37,'Which clause filters records row by row?','EASY','WHERE','WHERE filters rows and executes row by row after FROM.'),(1575,37,'Which clause filters groups after GROUP BY?','EASY','HAVING','HAVING filters groups and executes group by group after GROUP BY.'),(1576,37,'According to the notes, which clause requires GROUP BY to be used?','MEDIUM','HAVING','The notes state that HAVING requires GROUP BY.'),(1577,37,'Which statement correctly describes a normal subquery?','MEDIUM','The inner query executes first and passes its output to the outer query','The notes describe the inner query executing first, producing partial output for the outer query.'),(1578,37,'Which operator returns true only when a condition is satisfied against all values returned by a subquery?','MEDIUM','ALL','ALL returns true only if the condition is satisfied against all returned values.'),(1579,37,'Which operator returns true if a condition is satisfied against any one value returned by a subquery?','MEDIUM','ANY','ANY returns true if the condition is satisfied against any one of the returned values.'),(1580,37,'Which type of join fetches only matched records based on an explicitly specified joining condition?','MEDIUM','Inner Join','The notes state that Inner Join fetches only matched records using an explicitly specified ON/WHERE joining condition.'),(1581,37,'Which join automatically joins tables using matching column names according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Natural Join','Natural Join automatically joins on matching column names when a relation exists between the tables.'),(1582,37,'Which statement correctly describes a Full Outer Join?','MEDIUM','It fetches matched records and unmatched records from both tables','The notes describe Full Outer Join as fetching unmatched records from both left and right tables along with matched records.'),(1583,37,'What is a correlated subquery?','EASY','A subquery in which the inner and outer queries are inter-dependent','The notes define a correlated subquery as one where the inner and outer queries depend on each other.'),(1584,37,'Which query starts execution first in a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','Outer query','The notes state that the outer query starts first and produces partial output.'),(1585,37,'What is passed from the outer query to the inner query in a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','Partial output of the outer query','The outer query produces partial output, which is given as input to the inner query.'),(1586,37,'What does the inner query produce after receiving input from the outer query?','MEDIUM','Partial output','The inner query executes and produces its own partial output.'),(1587,37,'Where is the inner-query result passed after execution in a correlated subquery?','MEDIUM','Back to the outer query','The inner query output is given back as input to the outer query.'),(1588,37,'What is required inside the inner query of a correlated subquery according to the notes?','MEDIUM','A joining condition','The notes state that a joining condition must be maintained inside the inner query.'),(1589,37,'A correlated subquery works on the principle of which two concepts?','MEDIUM','Subquery and joins','The notes state that correlated subqueries work on the principle of both subqueries and joins.'),(1590,37,'What is the main execution difference between a normal subquery and a correlated subquery?','HARD','Normal subquery inner query executes first independently; correlated subquery outer query starts first','The notes explain the different execution order and dependency between the two query types.'),(1591,37,'In a normal subquery, how is the inner query output used?','MEDIUM','It is passed once to the outer query','The notes state that the normal inner query executes independently and its output is passed once to the outer query.'),(1592,37,'Which statement correctly describes the relationship between the inner and outer queries in a correlated subquery?','HARD','They are inter-dependent on each other','Both queries depend on each other during execution, according to the notes.'),(1593,37,'What is the purpose of SQL Set Operators?','EASY','To combine results of two or more SELECT statements','Set Operations combine the result of two or more SQL SELECT statements.'),(1594,37,'How many Set Operators are listed in the notes?','EASY','Four','The four operators are UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT and MINUS.'),(1595,37,'Which Set Operator combines results and removes duplicate rows?','EASY','UNION','UNION combines SELECT results and eliminates duplicate values from the result set.'),(1596,37,'Which Set Operator combines results without removing duplicates?','EASY','UNION ALL','UNION ALL returns the combined set without removing duplicates.'),(1597,37,'Two SELECT statements contain the same row. Which operator returns that common row only once?','MEDIUM','INTERSECT','INTERSECT returns common rows from both SELECT statements without duplicates.'),(1598,37,'Which operator returns rows present in the first SELECT result but absent in the second?','MEDIUM','MINUS','MINUS displays rows present in the first query but absent in the second query.'),(1599,37,'What must be the same in both SELECT statements when using Set Operators?','MEDIUM','Number of columns and corresponding column datatypes','The notes require the same number of columns and matching or compatible datatypes.'),(1600,37,'Which Set Operators remove duplicates according to the notes?','MEDIUM','UNION, INTERSECT and MINUS','UNION eliminates duplicates; INTERSECT and MINUS also return results without duplicates.'),(1601,37,'According to the notes, which operators arrange their results in ascending order by default?','MEDIUM','INTERSECT and MINUS','The notes state that INTERSECT and MINUS arrange their results in ascending order by default.'),(1602,37,'A developer wants to combine two SELECT results while retaining duplicate rows. Which operator should be used?','MEDIUM','UNION ALL','UNION ALL combines results without removing duplicate rows.'),(1603,37,'What are attributes in a table?','EASY','Properties that define an entity','The notes define attributes as properties that define an entity.'),(1604,37,'Which term is also used for a column in a table?','EASY','Attribute','A column is also referred to as an Attribute, Property or Field.'),(1605,37,'Which attribute is used to identify a record uniquely from a table?','EASY','Key attribute / Candidate Key','A key attribute identifies a record uniquely.'),(1606,37,'Name, age, gender and date of birth are examples of which type of attributes in the notes?','MEDIUM','Non-Key attributes','The notes list name, age, gender and DOB as examples of Non-Key attributes.'),(1607,37,'Among the key attributes, what is the attribute chosen as the main identifier called?','MEDIUM','Prime Key attribute','The selected main identifier among key attributes is called the Prime Key attribute.'),(1608,37,'What is another name for a Non-Prime Key attribute?','EASY','Secondary Key attribute','The notes use Non-Prime Key attribute and Secondary Key attribute as equivalent terms.'),(1609,37,'A combination of two or more non-key attributes used to identify a record uniquely is called what in the notes?','MEDIUM','Composite Key attribute','The notes define a Composite Key attribute as a combination of two or more non-key attributes used for unique identification.'),(1610,37,'According to the notes, a Super Key attribute is which of the following?','MEDIUM','A set of all key attributes','The notes define Super Key as the set of all key attributes.'),(1611,37,'Which attribute represents a relationship between two entities according to the notes?','MEDIUM','Foreign Key attribute','The Foreign Key attribute behaves as an attribute of another entity and represents a relationship between entities.'),(1612,37,'In the notes, Dno is given as an example of which attribute?','MEDIUM','Foreign Key attribute','Dno is the example given for a Foreign Key attribute.'),(1613,37,'What is a Functional Dependency?','EASY','A relationship where one attribute determines another attribute','The notes define functional dependency as one attribute in a relation determining another attribute.'),(1614,37,'Which symbol is used in the notes to represent functional dependency?','EASY','→','The notes use an arrow to represent dependency, for example Eid → ename.'),(1615,37,'How many types of Functional Dependencies are listed in the notes?','EASY','Three','The three types are Total, Partial and Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1616,37,'If one attribute determines all the other attributes in a relation, what type of dependency is it?','MEDIUM','Total Functional Dependency','The notes define TFD as one attribute determining all other attributes.'),(1617,37,'A part of a composite key determines another attribute uniquely. Which dependency is this?','MEDIUM','Partial Functional Dependency','The notes define partial dependency as a part of a composite key determining another attribute uniquely.'),(1618,37,'A non-key attribute is determined by a key attribute and then determines another attribute. What is this called?','MEDIUM','Transitive Functional Dependency','This is the definition of transitive dependency in the notes.'),(1619,37,'In the example Eid → ename, sal, dob, which type of dependency is represented?','MEDIUM','Total Functional Dependency','Eid determines all the other attributes in the example, so it is TFD.'),(1620,37,'Which dependency is represented by Cid → Pincode → City?','MEDIUM','Transitive Functional Dependency','The notes use this chain as an example of transitive dependency.'),(1621,37,'What is redundancy?','EASY','Repetition of unwanted data','The notes define redundancy as repetition of unwanted data.'),(1622,37,'According to the notes, what is an anomaly?','MEDIUM','Side effects caused during DML operations','The notes define anomaly as side effects caused during DML operations.'),(1623,37,'What is the main purpose of Normalisation?','EASY','To remove redundancies and anomalies','Normalisation decomposes large tables into smaller tables to remove redundancies and anomalies.'),(1624,37,'Which of the following is a requirement of First Normal Form (1NF)?','EASY','Each column must contain a single atomic value','The notes state that each column must contain a single atomic value in 1NF.'),(1625,37,'A table contains multiple course values such as JAVA and SQL in a single cell. Which normal form requirement is violated?','MEDIUM','1NF','The notes state that multivalued data should not be present in 1NF.'),(1626,37,'A table already satisfies 1NF but still contains a Partial Functional Dependency. Which normal form requirement is not satisfied?','MEDIUM','2NF','2NF requires the table to be in 1NF and have no Partial Functional Dependency.'),(1627,37,'Which dependency must be removed to satisfy the 2NF requirement described in the notes?','MEDIUM','Partial Functional Dependency','The notes state that 2NF requires the removal of Partial Functional Dependency.'),(1628,37,'A table satisfies 2NF but contains a Transitive Functional Dependency. Which normal form requirement is not satisfied?','MEDIUM','3NF','3NF requires the table to satisfy 2NF and have no Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1629,37,'Which dependency must be removed to satisfy the 3NF requirement described in the notes?','MEDIUM','Transitive Functional Dependency','The notes state that 3NF requires the removal of Transitive Functional Dependency.'),(1630,37,'According to the notes, when is a table said to be normalized?','MEDIUM','When it is reduced to 3NF','The notes state that a table reduced to 3NF is said to be normalized.'),(1631,37,'Which sequence correctly represents the normalisation flow described in the notes?','EASY','1NF → 2NF → 3NF','The notes summarise the flow as First Normal Form, Second Normal Form and Third Normal Form.'),(1632,37,'Which of the following normal forms is also listed in the uploaded notes?','EASY','BCNF','The notes list 1NF, 2NF, 3NF and BCNF as levels of Normal Form.'),(1633,37,'A SPIDERS table stores \"JAVA, SQL\" together in one Course cell. What issue does this represent?','EASY','Multivalued data','The uploaded notes use multiple course values in one cell as an example of multivalued data.'),(1634,37,'In the SPIDERS table example, what is done to bring the multivalued Course data into the format shown in the notes?','MEDIUM','Split the course values into separate columns','The notes show the Course values being split into C1, C2 and C3 columns.'),(1635,37,'In the Employee example, Eid determines ename and sal, while Deptno determines dname and loc. Which dependency is identified for the composite key (Eid, Deptno)?','HARD','Partial Functional Dependency','The notes identify the composite key (Eid, Deptno) and describe the dependency as partial.'),(1636,37,'In the Employee decomposition example, which attributes are placed in R1?','MEDIUM','Eid, ename, sal','The notes show R1 as (Eid, ename, sal).'),(1637,37,'In the same Employee decomposition example, which attributes are placed in R2?','MEDIUM','Deptno, dname, loc','The notes show R2 as (Deptno, dname, loc).'),(1638,37,'In the Customer example, Cid determines Pincode, and Pincode determines City. Which attribute is the non-key attribute that determines City?','MEDIUM','Pincode','The notes identify Pincode as the non-key attribute determined by Cid, which in turn determines City.'),(1639,37,'In the Employee example for 3NF, Eid determines pincode, and pincode determines state and country. What type of dependency is shown?','MEDIUM','Transitive Functional Dependency','The notes identify the chain Eid → pincode → state, country as a transitive dependency.'),(1640,37,'In the 3NF Employee decomposition example, which attributes are placed in R2?','MEDIUM','pincode, state, country','The notes show R2 as (pincode, state, country).'),(1641,37,'Which dependency is removed in the Employee example when decomposing the table to satisfy 2NF?','HARD','Partial Functional Dependency','The notes explain that the composite key (Eid, Deptno) produces a partial dependency, which is removed through decomposition.'),(1642,37,'Which dependency is removed in the Employee example when decomposing the table to satisfy 3NF?','HARD','Transitive Functional Dependency','The notes explain that Eid → pincode → state, country is a transitive dependency removed through decomposition.'),(1643,37,'A query needs to filter individual employee records before grouping them. Which clause should be used?','MEDIUM','WHERE','WHERE filters individual rows and executes after FROM, according to the notes.'),(1644,37,'A query needs to filter groups after applying GROUP BY. Which clause should be used?','MEDIUM','HAVING','HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY and requires GROUP BY according to the notes.'),(1645,37,'A query must retrieve only the columns ENAME and SAL from an EMP table. Which concept does this represent?','EASY','Projection','Projection retrieves data by selecting only the required columns.'),(1646,37,'A query must retrieve employee records from two tables simultaneously. Which SQL concept is used?','EASY','Joins','Joins are used to retrieve data from multiple tables simultaneously.'),(1647,37,'A subquery returns multiple rows. Which type of operator can be used to compare its output, according to the notes?','MEDIUM','ALL or ANY','The notes state that multi-row subqueries use special operators such as IN, ALL and ANY.'),(1648,37,'Two SELECT statements must combine their results while retaining duplicate rows. Which set operator is appropriate?','MEDIUM','UNION ALL','UNION ALL combines results without removing duplicates.'),(1649,37,'A query needs to retrieve only the rows common to two SELECT statements. Which set operator should be used?','MEDIUM','INTERSECT','INTERSECT returns common rows from both SELECT statements.'),(1650,37,'A developer wants to permanently save changes made using DML statements. Which command should be used?','EASY','COMMIT','COMMIT makes the transaction changes permanent according to the notes.'),(1651,37,'A table must be sorted in descending order based on salary. Which clause should be used?','EASY','ORDER BY','ORDER BY sorts query results in ascending or descending order and is written at the end of the query.'),(1652,37,'An employee table contains repeated unwanted department details, causing redundancy. Which process is used to reduce redundancies and anomalies?','MEDIUM','Normalisation','Normalisation decomposes large tables into smaller tables to reduce redundancies and anomalies.'),(1653,37,'A developer wants to change the structure of an existing table by adding a column. Which command should be used?','EASY','ALTER','ALTER is a DDL command used to modify table structure, including adding columns.'),(1654,37,'A developer wants to change an employee salary value in an existing row. Which command should be used?','EASY','UPDATE','UPDATE modifies values stored in existing table rows.'),(1655,37,'A table must be renamed without changing its data or structure. Which command is appropriate according to the notes?','MEDIUM','RENAME','RENAME changes the name of an existing table without otherwise affecting its data or structure.'),(1656,37,'A developer wants to remove all records but retain the table structure. Which command should be used?','MEDIUM','TRUNCATE','TRUNCATE removes all records while keeping the table structure.'),(1657,37,'A developer wants to remove a table completely, including its structure and data. Which command is used?','MEDIUM','DROP','DROP removes the complete table object, including its structure and data.'),(1658,37,'A table has been dropped and moved to the Recycle Bin. Which command is used to remove it permanently from the Recycle Bin?','MEDIUM','PURGE','PURGE permanently removes the table from the Recycle Bin, so it cannot be restored.'),(1659,37,'A developer wants to remove only selected employee records using a WHERE condition. Which command should be used?','MEDIUM','DELETE','DELETE can remove selected records using a WHERE condition.'),(1660,37,'A developer wants to retrieve the table after it has been dropped into the Recycle Bin. Which command is mentioned in the notes?','MEDIUM','FLASHBACK','The notes state that a dropped table in the Recycle Bin can be restored using FLASHBACK.'),(1661,37,'A developer wants to prevent repeated values from being stored in a column. Which option should be used?','EASY','UNIQUE constraint','UNIQUE is a constraint assigned to a column to prevent duplicate values from being stored.'),(1662,37,'A developer wants to remove duplicate values only from the output of a SELECT query. Which keyword should be used?','EASY','DISTINCT','DISTINCT removes duplicate values from the result table at query time.'),(1663,37,'A developer wants to change the structure of an existing table by adding a column. Which command should be used?','EASY','ALTER','ALTER is a DDL command used to modify table structure, including adding columns.'),(1664,37,'A developer wants to change an employee salary value in an existing row. Which command should be used?','EASY','UPDATE','UPDATE modifies values stored in existing table rows.'),(1665,37,'A table must be renamed without changing its data or structure. Which command is appropriate according to the notes?','MEDIUM','RENAME','RENAME changes the name of an existing table without otherwise affecting its data or structure.'),(1666,37,'A developer wants to remove all records but retain the table structure. Which command should be used?','MEDIUM','TRUNCATE','TRUNCATE removes all records while keeping the table structure.'),(1667,37,'A developer wants to remove a table completely, including its structure and data. Which command is used?','MEDIUM','DROP','DROP removes the complete table object, including its structure and data.'),(1668,37,'A table has been dropped and moved to the Recycle Bin. Which command is used to remove it permanently from the Recycle Bin?','MEDIUM','PURGE','PURGE permanently removes the table from the Recycle Bin, so it cannot be restored.'),(1669,37,'A developer wants to remove only selected employee records using a WHERE condition. Which command should be used?','MEDIUM','DELETE','DELETE can remove selected records using a WHERE condition.'),(1670,37,'A developer wants to retrieve the table after it has been dropped into the Recycle Bin. Which command is mentioned in the notes?','MEDIUM','FLASHBACK','The notes state that a dropped table in the Recycle Bin can be restored using FLASHBACK.'),(1671,37,'A developer wants to prevent repeated values from being stored in a column. Which option should be used?','EASY','UNIQUE constraint','UNIQUE is a constraint assigned to a column to prevent duplicate values from being stored.'),(1672,37,'A developer wants to remove duplicate values only from the output of a SELECT query. Which keyword should be used?','EASY','DISTINCT','DISTINCT removes duplicate values from the result table at query time.'),(1673,37,'What is the main purpose of a constraint in SQL?','EASY','Extra validation for a column','A constraint is assigned to a column for extra validation.'),(1674,37,'How many constraint types are listed in your SQL notes?','EASY','Five','The notes list UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, PRIMARY KEY and FOREIGN KEY.'),(1675,37,'Which constraint prevents duplicate values in a column?','EASY','UNIQUE','UNIQUE prevents duplicate or repeated values from being stored in the column.'),(1676,37,'Which constraint prevents NULL values?','EASY','NOT NULL','NOT NULL is one of the five constraint types listed in the notes.'),(1677,37,'Which constraint is used to establish a relationship between tables?','EASY','FOREIGN KEY','The foreign key establishes a connection between tables.'),(1678,37,'Which constraint is described as a combination of UNIQUE and NOT NULL?','MEDIUM','PRIMARY KEY','The notes describe a primary key as a combination of UNIQUE and NOT NULL.'),(1679,37,'Which constraint is also known as the Referential Integrity Constraint?','EASY','FOREIGN KEY','The notes identify the foreign key as the Referential Integrity Constraint.'),(1680,37,'Which of the following is NOT listed as a constraint type in your notes?','MEDIUM','DEFAULT','The five listed types are UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, PRIMARY KEY and FOREIGN KEY.'),(1681,37,'What does a SQL data type specify?','EASY','What kind of data can be stored in a column or table','A data type specifies what type of data has to be stored in a column or table.'),(1682,37,'How many data types are listed in your SQL notes?','EASY','Six','The six listed types are CHAR, VARCHAR/VARCHAR2, NUMBER, DATE, TIMESTAMP and LARGE OBJECT.'),(1683,37,'Which data type follows fixed-length memory allocation?','EASY','CHAR','CHAR follows fixed-length memory allocation.'),(1684,37,'Which data type follows variable-length memory allocation?','EASY','VARCHAR','VARCHAR follows variable-length memory allocation.'),(1685,37,'According to your notes, what is the maximum number of characters CHAR can store?','MEDIUM','2000','The notes specify that CHAR can store up to 2000 characters.'),(1686,37,'According to your notes, what is the maximum number of characters VARCHAR can store?','MEDIUM','4000','The notes specify that VARCHAR/VARCHAR2 can store up to 4000 characters.'),(1687,37,'Which data type is used to store numbers only?','EASY','NUMBER','NUMBER is used to store numbers only.'),(1688,37,'In NUMBER(5,2), what does the value 2 represent?','MEDIUM','Scale','Scale is used to store decimal values and is optional.'),(1689,37,'Which data type is used to store date and time together?','EASY','TIMESTAMP','TIMESTAMP is used to store date and time together.'),(1690,37,'Which of the following is listed as a Large Object data type?','MEDIUM','CLOB/BLOB','The notes list Large Object as a data type, with CLOB and BLOB.'),(1691,37,'What is DBMS?','EASY','Software used to maintain and manage a database','DBMS is software used to maintain and manage a database.'),(1692,37,'Which two important features are provided by DBMS according to the notes?','EASY','Security and Authorization','The notes list Security and Authorization as two important DBMS features.'),(1693,37,'What does RDBMS stand for?','EASY','Relational Database Management System','RDBMS stands for Relational Database Management System.'),(1694,37,'In which format is data stored in an RDBMS?','EASY','Table format','The notes state that RDBMS stores data in table format.'),(1695,37,'Who invented the Relational Model according to the notes?','EASY','Edgar Franklin Codd','The notes identify Edgar Franklin Codd as the inventor of the Relational Model.'),(1696,37,'What are the two requirements mentioned for DBMS software to become an RDBMS?','MEDIUM','Table format and E.F. Codd rules','The notes state that DBMS follows table format and E.F. Codd rules to become RDBMS.'),(1697,37,'What is a cell in a table?','EASY','Combination of a row and a column','A cell is the combination of a row and a column.'),(1698,37,'Which term refers to the vertical divisions of a table?','EASY','Columns / Attributes / Fields','Columns are also called attributes, properties or fields.'),(1699,37,'Which term refers to the horizontal divisions of a table?','EASY','Rows / Records / Tuples','Rows are also known as records or tuples.'),(1700,37,'According to the E.F. Codd rules listed in the notes, what kind of data should be entered in a cell?','MEDIUM','Single-value data','The notes state that data entered in a cell must be single-value data.'),(1701,37,'According to E.F. Codd\'s rules, what type of data must be entered in a table cell?','EASY','Single-value data','The notes state that data entered in a cell must be single-value data.'),(1702,37,'According to the notes, in which format is data stored in RDBMS?','EASY','Table format along with metadata','The notes state that RDBMS stores data in table format along with metadata.'),(1703,37,'According to E.F. Codd\'s rules, how can data be stored across multiple tables?','MEDIUM','With the help of key attributes','The notes state that key attributes help store data in multiple tables.'),(1704,37,'According to the notes, what are the two steps used to validate data entered into a cell?','MEDIUM','Assigning a data type and constraints','The notes list data types and constraints as the two validation steps.'),(1705,37,'Which of the following is a type of DBMS mentioned in the notes?','EASY','Hierarchical DBMS','The four listed types are Network, Object-Oriented, Hierarchical and Relational DBMS.'),(1706,37,'Which of the following is NOT one of the four DBMS types listed in the notes?','MEDIUM','Network CSS','The notes list Network, Object-Oriented, Hierarchical and Relational DBMS.'),(1707,37,'Which language is used to communicate with RDBMS according to the notes?','EASY','Structured Query Language','The notes state that SQL is used to communicate with RDBMS.'),(1708,37,'Which two features are mentioned for both DBMS and RDBMS?','MEDIUM','Security and Authorization','The notes list Security and Authorization as features of both DBMS and RDBMS.'),(1709,37,'What is the correct order of execution of a SELECT query according to the notes?','EASY','FROM, WHERE, SELECT','The notes specify that FROM executes first, followed by WHERE and SELECT.'),(1710,37,'What is an alias in SQL?','EASY','An alternative name assigned to a column or expression','An alias improves the understandability of a column name or expression in the result table.'),(1711,37,'Which keyword is used to remove duplicate values from a query result?','EASY','DISTINCT','DISTINCT removes duplicate or repeated values from the result table.'),(1712,37,'Which statement about NULL values is supported by the notes?','MEDIUM','Arithmetic operations involving NULL return NULL','The notes state that arithmetic operations with NULL values produce NULL.'),(1713,37,'How does the WHERE clause execute according to the notes?','EASY','Row by row','WHERE filters records and executes row by row after the FROM clause.'),(1714,37,'Which logical operator returns TRUE only when all conditions are satisfied?','EASY','AND','AND returns TRUE when all the given conditions are satisfied.'),(1715,37,'What happens when AND and OR are used together in a query according to the notes?','MEDIUM','AND gets the first execution priority','The notes state that AND gets the first priority and recommend brackets for OR conditions.'),(1716,37,'Which operator allows multiple values on the right-hand side of a condition?','EASY','IN','IN accepts multiple values on the RHS, whereas the equals operator accepts only one value.'),(1717,12,'What does HTML stand for?','EASY','B','HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.'),(1718,12,'What is the main purpose of HTML?','EASY','C','HTML is used to create the structure of a webpage.'),(1719,12,'Why is HTML not considered a programming language according to the notes?','EASY','A','HTML does not involve writing logic; it creates webpage structure using tags.'),(1720,12,'What is hypertext?','EASY','D','Any text that contains a link to another webpage is called hypertext.'),(1721,12,'Which tag is the root tag of an HTML document?','EASY','B','The html tag is the root tag and contains the document content.'),(1722,12,'Which section provides information about the HTML document?','EASY','A','The head tag provides information about the HTML document.'),(1723,12,'Which tag defines the document name displayed in the browser tab?','EASY','C','The title tag provides the document name shown in the browser tab.'),(1724,12,'Why is the meta tag described as an unpaired tag in the notes?','MEDIUM','D','The notes describe meta as having only an opening tag.'),(1725,12,'Which tag contains the content displayed on the webpage UI?','EASY','B','The body tag contains the HTML content displayed on the webpage.'),(1726,12,'What does <!DOCTYPE html> indicate according to the notes?','EASY','A','It tells the version of HTML; the notes ident